window.DARKTALE_SEARCH={"settings":{"duskworld":{"name":"Duskworld","url":"settings/duskworld/index.html"}},"entries":[["Rules","Rolls and results","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Pool: Attribute + Skill in d6. Every 5 or 6 is a success. Untrained skills: When you roll a skill you have no dots in, take −1d for a Physical or Social skill, or −3d for a Mental skill. Chance die: If penalties reduce your pool to 0 dice or fewer, roll a single d6 instead. Only a 6 is a success. A 1 is a Complication with no success. Rerolls: Each die can be rerolled only once per roll. Exploding dice: When a rule says 6s explode, each 6 adds another die to the roll, which can explode too. Exploding dice are new dice, not rerolls. The stakes die and chance dice never explode. Contested rolls: Both sides roll. The side with more successes wins; the margin is the net successes. Ties go to the side that acts: the attacker, or whoever is trying to do something to the other. A tie is a win with 0 net successes, so an attack that ties is a hit with 0 net successes. Success with Opportunity: Any roll with 5 or more successes is a success with Opportunity. The stakes die can also give an Opportunity (below), even on a failed roll. Talents that \"expand the Opportunity range\" lower this threshold by 1 per step (to 4+, then 3+). The threshold never goes below 3. Stakes die: When a character ",""],["Rules","Pool","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Pool: Attribute + Skill in d6. Every 5 or 6 is a success. ",""],["Rules","Untrained skills","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Untrained skills: When you roll a skill you have no dots in, take −1d for a Physical or Social skill, or −3d for a Mental skill. ",""],["Rules","Chance die","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Chance die: If penalties reduce your pool to 0 dice or fewer, roll a single d6 instead. Only a 6 is a success. A 1 is a Complication with no success. ",""],["Rules","Rerolls","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Rerolls: Each die can be rerolled only once per roll. ",""],["Rules","Exploding dice","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Exploding dice: When a rule says 6s explode, each 6 adds another die to the roll, which can explode too. Exploding dice are new dice, not rerolls. The stakes die and chance dice never explode. ",""],["Rules","Contested rolls","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Contested rolls: Both sides roll. The side with more successes wins; the margin is the net successes. Ties go to the side that acts: the attacker, or whoever is trying to do something to the other. A tie is a win with 0 net successes, so an attack that ties is a hit with 0 net successes. ",""],["Rules","Success with Opportunity","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Success with Opportunity: Any roll with 5 or more successes is a success with Opportunity. The stakes die can also give an Opportunity (below), even on a failed roll. Talents that \"expand the Opportunity range\" lower this threshold by 1 per step (to 4+, then 3+). The threshold never goes below 3. ",""],["Rules","Stakes die","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Stakes die: When a character raises the stakes, they roll an extra d6 of a different colour along with their pool: 5–6: an Opportunity, but no success. 1–2: a Complication, which also counts as a success. 3–4: nothing. Overall, the stakes die succeeds as often as a normal die; what it adds is the swing. An Opportunity from the stakes die applies even if the roll fails: you don't get what you wanted, but you gain something else.",""],["Rules","Raising the stakes","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Raising the stakes: The GM raises the stakes on a roll when the outcome could change the situation in a big way: a desperate leap, a lie to someone powerful, a blow that could end a fight. Once per day, a player can also choose to raise the stakes on their own roll, before rolling. Talents and effects can add more uses. A roll never has more than one stakes die. ",""],["Rules","Opportunities and Complications","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Opportunities and Complications: On an Opportunity, the player chooses a benefit that fits the roll (an extra effect, a useful detail, a better position), and the GM approves it. On a Complication, the GM adds a setback that fits the roll (a lost item, an alerted guard, a slip). A Complication never cancels a success. A roll can show both. ",""],["Rules","Teamwork","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Teamwork: Before a character rolls, a helper can roll a fitting Attribute + Skill (in combat, this costs the helper 1 action). With at least 1 success, the main roll gets +1d; on an Opportunity, +2d. If the helper's roll shows a Complication, the main roll suffers it too. Helpers give at most +2d to a single roll in total. ",""],["Rules","Group actions","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Group actions: when the whole group does the same thing together and one result decides it for everyone (sneaking past a guard post, climbing a wall, holding a line), one character leads it. Everyone involved rolls, the leader included, and the best roll counts for the whole group, with its own Opportunity or Complication; ties go to the leader's roll if it's one of them. The leader takes 1 Stress for each roll that failed (no successes). Each participant can push themselves on their own roll. Teamwork is for helping one character's roll; a group action is for when everyone's roll is at stake. ",""],["Rules","Specialties","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Specialties: +1d or more when one applies (see Specialties). ",""],["Rules","Fixed difficulty","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Fixed difficulty: When there's no opposing roll, the roll needs a number of successes: 2 is a hard task, 3 is a very hard one. ",""],["Rules","Modifiers","Core Rules › Modifiers","rules/rolls-and-results.html#modifiers","Situational bonuses and penalties to dice pools. They add together; if they bring a pool to 0 dice or fewer, roll a chance die. Advantage / disadvantage: +1d / −1d, for anything that helps or hinders a roll and has no rule of its own.",""],["Rules","Advantage / disadvantage","Core Rules › Modifiers","rules/rolls-and-results.html#modifiers","Advantage / disadvantage: +1d / −1d, for anything that helps or hinders a roll and has no rule of its own. ",""],["Rules","Light","Core Rules › Modifiers","rules/rolls-and-results.html#light","Rolls that rely on sight take −1d in dim light and −3d in darkness. In total darkness (no light at all, or unnatural darkness a setting defines), you're effectively Blinded: rolls that rely on sight use a chance die.",""],["Rules","Cover and concealment","Core Rules › Modifiers","rules/rolls-and-results.html#cover-and-concealment","Cover: Something solid blocks part of you. Ranged attacks against you take −1d. With full cover, you can't be targeted. Obscured: Smoke, fog or shadow makes you Obscured: rolls to see or target you take −1d, and you can try to hide with Stealth. Deeper darkness uses the Light rules.",""],["Rules","Cover","Core Rules › Cover and concealment","rules/rolls-and-results.html#cover-and-concealment","Cover: Something solid blocks part of you. Ranged attacks against you take −1d. With full cover, you can't be targeted. ",""],["Rules","Obscured","Core Rules › Cover and concealment","rules/rolls-and-results.html#cover-and-concealment","Obscured: Smoke, fog or shadow makes you Obscured: rolls to see or target you take −1d, and you can try to hide with Stealth. Deeper darkness uses the Light rules. ",""],["Rules","Position","Core Rules › Modifiers","rules/rolls-and-results.html#position","Prone: melee attacks against you get +1d; ranged attacks against you get −1d. Difficult terrain: −1d to Physical rolls, including attacks and defense rolls, while you stand in it (see Terrain and forced movement). Bracing: the Brace action gives +1d to your defense rolls while you're in cover.",""],["Rules","Prone","Core Rules › Position","rules/rolls-and-results.html#position","Prone: melee attacks against you get +1d; ranged attacks against you get −1d. ",""],["Rules","Difficult terrain","Core Rules › Position","rules/rolls-and-results.html#position","Difficult terrain: −1d to Physical rolls, including attacks and defense rolls, while you stand in it (see Terrain and forced movement). ",""],["Rules","Bracing","Core Rules › Position","rules/rolls-and-results.html#position","Bracing: the Brace action gives +1d to your defense rolls while you're in cover. ",""],["Rules","Other modifiers","Core Rules › Modifiers","rules/rolls-and-results.html#other-modifiers","Defined where they're used: Modifier Effect See Untrained skill −1d Physical or Social, −3d Mental Rolls and results Specialty +1d to +3d Specialties Pushing yourself +2d for 2 Stress Stress Teamwork +1d, or +2d on an Opportunity Rolls and results Set up +1d on your next roll against the target Actions Called shot −1d to −3d Called shots Parrying an armed attack unarmed −2d Defense and reactions Beyond a weapon's range −2d Weapon traits Heavy weapon without Strength 3 −1d per missing dot Weapon traits Medium or heavy armor −1d to Stealth and Athletics Armor Kit / no proper tools +1d / −1d Kits Treating yourself with First aid −1d Actions Exhausted −1d per stack Conditions",""],["Rules","Contested pools","Core Rules › Contested pools","rules/rolls-and-results.html#contested-pools","When an effect is \"contested by\" a pool, the target rolls the one that fits: Against Contested by Feints, tricks, lies, mental attacks Intelligence + Resolve Pressure, taunts, intimidation, fear Wits + Composure Physical effects that aren't attacks (shoves, poisons, traps) Dexterity + Stamina Noticing something hidden (a sneaking character, a concealed trap) Wits + Composure",""],["Rules","Extended actions","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","An extended action is a task that takes many rolls over time: researching in an archive, repairing a machine, forging papers, tracking someone across open country. The GM sets: Pool: the Attribute + Skill that fits, as usual. Target: the total successes needed. Interval: how long each roll takes (minutes, hours or days). Task Target Rolls with a 6-die pool Quick 3 about 2 Standard 6 about 3 Long 10 about 5 Major 15 about 8 Rolling: each roll adds its successes to the total. When the total reaches the target, the task is done. Opportunity: choose a benefit as usual, such as finishing this interval in half the time, saving materials, or a useful discovery. An Opportunity never adds successes to the total. Complication: the GM adds a setback that fits: lose 1 success already gained, spend extra time or materials, or draw attention. No successes: take 1 Stress to press on, or the attempt fails. Trying again usually needs a new approach, new tools or new information. (During downtime, no Stress is paid: the work stops until a later Project action.) Pushing, the stakes die and specialties work on each roll as normal, except that downtime rolls can't be pushed. Teamwork works on each roll",""],["Rules","Pool","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Pool: the Attribute + Skill that fits, as usual. ",""],["Rules","Target","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Target: the total successes needed. ",""],["Rules","Interval","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Interval: how long each roll takes (minutes, hours or days). ",""],["Rules","Rolling","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Rolling: each roll adds its successes to the total. When the total reaches the target, the task is done. ",""],["Rules","Opportunity","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Opportunity: choose a benefit as usual, such as finishing this interval in half the time, saving materials, or a useful discovery. An Opportunity never adds successes to the total. ",""],["Rules","Complication","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Complication: the GM adds a setback that fits: lose 1 success already gained, spend extra time or materials, or draw attention. ",""],["Rules","No successes","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","No successes: take 1 Stress to press on, or the attempt fails. Trying again usually needs a new approach, new tools or new information. (During downtime, no Stress is paid: the work stops until a later Project action.) ",""],["Rules","Pushing, the stakes die and specialties","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Pushing, the stakes die and specialties work on each roll as normal, except that downtime rolls can't be pushed. ",""],["Rules","Teamwork","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Teamwork works on each roll. If the GM agrees the task can be split (searching an archive, but not forging one document), several characters can instead work in parallel: each rolls their own pool every interval, and all their successes count toward the target. ",""],["Rules","Deadlines","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Deadlines: the GM can set one (\"before the curfew bell\"). If the target isn't reached in time, the task fails or only partly succeeds. ",""],["Rules","Crafting and repair","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Crafting and repair are extended actions with their own targets and upgrades (see Crafting). ",""],["Rules","Contested extended actions","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Contested extended actions (chases, races, a long debate): both sides roll each interval, and the first to reach the target wins. Ties go to the side that started it (the pursuer, the challenger). ",""],["Rules","Skills","Core Rules › Skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#skills","Every character uses the same 24 skills, eight in each category. Skills run 0–5: 1 below average, 2 normally skilled, 3 highly trained, 4 exceptional, 5 the best of the best. Settings can add power skills for characters with access to supernatural powers.",""],["Rules","Mental","Core Rules › Skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#mental","Skill Covers Example specialties Academics Learning from study: history, law, languages, theology History, Law, Theology Crafts Making, building and repairing physical things Smithing, Carpentry, Repairs Under Pressure Investigation Finding clues, solving puzzles, research Crime Scenes, Riddles, Research Medicine Treating wounds and illness, anatomy Surgery, Trauma, Pharmacology Occult Lore of the supernatural: rituals, folklore, curses Folklore, Rituals, Curses Politics Institutions, bureaucracy, and who holds power Bureaucracy, Scandals, Local Government Science How the world works in principle: natural philosophy, chemistry, engineering theory Chemistry, Engineering, Astronomy Technology Operating, understanding and subverting the most advanced machines of the setting Security Systems, Power Systems, Communication Systems Crafts, Science and Technology: Crafts makes and repairs things, Science understands how they work, and Technology operates and manipulates complex systems. What counts as \"advanced machines\" depends on the setting: computers in a modern world, runetech and kren machines in Duskworld.",""],["Rules","Physical","Core Rules › Skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#physical","Skill Covers Example specialties Athletics Running, climbing, swimming, jumping Climbing, Running, Swimming Brawl Unarmed fighting Grappling, Dirty Fighting, Boxing Drive Controlling any vehicle or mount, from horses and wagons to trains Wagons, Riding, Pursuit Ranged Bows, crossbows, firearms and thrown weapons Bows, Crossbows, Thrown Weapons Stealth Moving unseen and unheard Crowds, Shadowing, Rooftops Survival Living off the land, tracking, finding your way in the wild Foraging, Tracking, Navigation Thievery Locks, pickpocketing, security, sleight of hand Lockpicking, Pickpocketing, Safecracking Weaponry Fighting with melee weapons Swords, Knives, Spears",""],["Rules","Social","Core Rules › Skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#social","Skill Covers Example specialties Animal Ken Understanding, calming and training animals Dogs, Horses, Training Empathy Reading people's emotions and motives Lies, Emotions, Motives Expression Art, performance, speeches and writing Speeches, Music, Poetry Intimidation Threats and coercion Threats, Interrogation, Stare-downs Persuasion Convincing people by argument or charm Bargaining, Fast Talk, Oratory Socialize Fitting in, etiquette, working a crowd Etiquette, Parties, Carousing Streetwise The streets, the underworld and its rumors Rumors, Black Market, Gangs Subterfuge Lying, disguise, hiding your intentions Lying, Disguises, Hiding Emotions",""],["Rules","Power skills","Core Rules › Skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-skills","A setting's magic, psychic or other supernatural system can add power skills: skills for using that power, such as shaping dreams or bending shadows. The setting defines each one, including which attribute it's usually rolled with. Access: only a character with access to the power can have its power skills. Access comes from whatever the setting says grants the power: a talent, a racial trait, a community trait, or a story event. First dot free: when you gain access, you gain 1 permanent dot in the power skill. Outside the categories: power skills aren't Mental, Physical or Social. They aren't part of the 22 skill dots at creation and don't count against the 11/7/4 split. Improving: raise them through advancement like any other skill, at the same cost. No untrained rolls: without access, you can't roll a power skill at all. Everything else works as usual: specialties, the 0–5 scale, and the rule that talent prerequisites count only permanent dots.",""],["Rules","Access","Core Rules › Power skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-skills","Access: only a character with access to the power can have its power skills. Access comes from whatever the setting says grants the power: a talent, a racial trait, a community trait, or a story event. ",""],["Rules","First dot free","Core Rules › Power skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-skills","First dot free: when you gain access, you gain 1 permanent dot in the power skill. ",""],["Rules","Outside the categories","Core Rules › Power skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-skills","Outside the categories: power skills aren't Mental, Physical or Social. They aren't part of the 22 skill dots at creation and don't count against the 11/7/4 split. ",""],["Rules","Improving","Core Rules › Power skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-skills","Improving: raise them through advancement like any other skill, at the same cost. ",""],["Rules","No untrained rolls","Core Rules › Power skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-skills","No untrained rolls: without access, you can't roll a power skill at all. ",""],["Rules","Everything else works as usual","Core Rules › Power skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-skills","Everything else works as usual: specialties, the 0–5 scale, and the rule that talent prerequisites count only permanent dots. ",""],["Rules","Power pools","Core Rules › Skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-pools","Each power has its own pool, which the setting names (Investiture, Mana, Dread…). Its abilities spend from it. Size: 2 + the higher of two attributes the setting names for that power, unless the power sets its own size. Spending: each ability says its cost. A sustained ability costs its upkeep each round in combat, or each minute or hour outside it, as the ability says. Running empty: when your pool is empty, you can pay a cost by taking 2 Stress per point. Stress taken this way can overflow like any other (see Stress). Refilling: the setting says how a pool refills. By default, it refills fully at the end of a rest and at the end of a week of downtime. Reserved points: an effect's initial cost stays reserved while the effect is active: a refill doesn't return those points until the effect ends. Upkeep paid to sustain it is spent as usual and isn't reserved. Instant effects reserve nothing. You can end your own effect at any time, as a free action. More than one power: each power keeps its own pool.",""],["Rules","Size","Core Rules › Power pools","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-pools","Size: 2 + the higher of two attributes the setting names for that power, unless the power sets its own size. ",""],["Rules","Spending","Core Rules › Power pools","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-pools","Spending: each ability says its cost. A sustained ability costs its upkeep each round in combat, or each minute or hour outside it, as the ability says. ",""],["Rules","Running empty","Core Rules › Power pools","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-pools","Running empty: when your pool is empty, you can pay a cost by taking 2 Stress per point. Stress taken this way can overflow like any other (see Stress). ",""],["Rules","Refilling","Core Rules › Power pools","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-pools","Refilling: the setting says how a pool refills. By default, it refills fully at the end of a rest and at the end of a week of downtime. ",""],["Rules","Reserved points","Core Rules › Power pools","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-pools","Reserved points: an effect's initial cost stays reserved while the effect is active: a refill doesn't return those points until the effect ends. Upkeep paid to sustain it is spent as usual and isn't reserved. Instant effects reserve nothing. You can end your own effect at any time, as a free action. ",""],["Rules","More than one power","Core Rules › Power pools","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-pools","More than one power: each power keeps its own pool. ",""],["Rules","Compulsion","Core Rules › Skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#compulsion","Only supernatural powers can decide what a character does. Ordinary effects (threats, persuasion, fear) change dice, Stress and conditions; the player still chooses. When a power compels a target, these rules always apply unless the power says otherwise: Contested: the target resists with Intelligence + Resolve (commands, charms, false loyalties) or Wits + Composure (fear, overwhelming urges), as the power says. One command: the power imposes one short instruction, for the duration the power sets. Broken by harm: the compulsion ends at once if the target is harmed, or is ordered to harm themselves. Breaking free: a compelled character can take 2 Stress to act freely until the end of their turn, but they're Stunned for that turn: in combat, they take a slow turn with one action and no reaction. Aftermath: when it ends, the target knows something was done to them. Possession is the strongest compulsion: the possessor controls the victim's body directly. It follows the rules above, except: No commands: the possessor moves the body with their own actions, on their own turn. The victim can't act on its own turn, except to try to break free. A foreign body: each round the possessor moves",""],["Rules","Contested","Core Rules › Compulsion","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#compulsion","Contested: the target resists with Intelligence + Resolve (commands, charms, false loyalties) or Wits + Composure (fear, overwhelming urges), as the power says. ",""],["Rules","One command","Core Rules › Compulsion","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#compulsion","One command: the power imposes one short instruction, for the duration the power sets. ",""],["Rules","Broken by harm","Core Rules › Compulsion","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#compulsion","Broken by harm: the compulsion ends at once if the target is harmed, or is ordered to harm themselves. ",""],["Rules","Breaking free","Core Rules › Compulsion","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#compulsion","Breaking free: a compelled character can take 2 Stress to act freely until the end of their turn, but they're Stunned for that turn: in combat, they take a slow turn with one action and no reaction. ",""],["Rules","Aftermath","Core Rules › Compulsion","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#compulsion","Aftermath: when it ends, the target knows something was done to them. ",""],["Rules","No commands","Core Rules › Compulsion","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#compulsion","No commands: the possessor moves the body with their own actions, on their own turn. The victim can't act on its own turn, except to try to break free. ",""],["Rules","A foreign body","Core Rules › Compulsion","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#compulsion","A foreign body: each round the possessor moves the body costs them 1 Stress. The body's rolls use its own Strength, Dexterity and Stamina, and the possessor's other attributes and skills. ",""],["Rules","Harm doesn't break it","Core Rules › Compulsion","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#compulsion","Harm doesn't break it: harm to the body falls on the victim, not the possessor, who stays in. ",""],["Rules","Breaking free","Core Rules › Compulsion","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#compulsion","Breaking free: on its turn, the victim can take 2 Stress to contest the possession again. On a win, the possessor is cast out. ",""],["Rules","Specialties","Core Rules › Specialties","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#specialties","A specialty is a narrow focus within a skill, written as Skill (Specialty): Weaponry (Swords), Survival (Tracking). The skill tables list examples. Requirement: you need at least 1 permanent dot in a skill to choose or buy a specialty in it. Granted specialties: a specialty granted by a talent or trait ignores that requirement. You gain it even with 0 dots in the skill, and it always gives at least +1d, which offsets part of the untrained penalty. Improving it past +1d still follows the cap below. Bonus: when a specialty applies to a roll, add its bonus: +1d for a new specialty. One per roll: a skill can have several specialties, but only one applies to any roll. Use the one with the highest bonus. Improving a specialty: instead of gaining a new specialty, you can improve one you already have. Each improvement adds +1d to its bonus, up to +3d. A specialty's bonus can't be higher than your permanent dots in its skill. An improvement costs the same as gaining another specialty in that skill (see Advancement). Duplicates: if a talent or trait grants a specialty you already have, improve that specialty by one step instead. Scope: a specialty must be narrower than its skill. Swords, not",""],["Rules","Requirement","Core Rules › Specialties","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#specialties","Requirement: you need at least 1 permanent dot in a skill to choose or buy a specialty in it. ",""],["Rules","Granted specialties","Core Rules › Specialties","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#specialties","Granted specialties: a specialty granted by a talent or trait ignores that requirement. You gain it even with 0 dots in the skill, and it always gives at least +1d, which offsets part of the untrained penalty. Improving it past +1d still follows the cap below. ",""],["Rules","Bonus","Core Rules › Specialties","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#specialties","Bonus: when a specialty applies to a roll, add its bonus: +1d for a new specialty. ",""],["Rules","One per roll","Core Rules › Specialties","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#specialties","One per roll: a skill can have several specialties, but only one applies to any roll. Use the one with the highest bonus. ",""],["Rules","Improving a specialty","Core Rules › Specialties","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#specialties","Improving a specialty: instead of gaining a new specialty, you can improve one you already have. Each improvement adds +1d to its bonus, up to +3d. A specialty's bonus can't be higher than your permanent dots in its skill. An improvement costs the same as gaining another specialty in that skill (see Advancement). ",""],["Rules","Duplicates","Core Rules › Specialties","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#specialties","Duplicates: if a talent or trait grants a specialty you already have, improve that specialty by one step instead. ",""],["Rules","Scope","Core Rules › Specialties","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#specialties","Scope: a specialty must be narrower than its skill. Swords, not Melee Weapons; Tracking, not Wilderness. The GM approves each one. ",""],["Rules","Stress","Core Rules › Stress","rules/stress-and-madness.html#stress","Stress track: Your current Tenacity + 2 boxes (see Tenacity and madness). As you take Tenacity damage, your Stress track shrinks. Shrinking track: If your Stress track shrinks below the Stress you already have, the Stress that no longer fits is lost. It doesn't overflow again. Push yourself: Before a roll, take 2 Stress to add +2d to it. Extra reaction: Once your reaction for the round is spent, take 2 Stress to react again. You can repeat this as long as you can pay. Recovery roll: Resolve + Composure. Clear 1 Stress per success. Clearing Stress: Stress doesn't clear on its own with time. It clears at a rest (see Rest), at the end of a week of downtime, by catching your breath (see Actions), through talents, by surrendering while Beaten Down, and when it overflows (below). Overflow: You can still take Stress when your track is full, whether you chose to or an effect forced it on you. When Stress doesn't fit, it overflows: Each point that doesn't fit becomes 1 Tenacity damage. Your Stress track shrinks accordingly, and stays full. You make one madness check for the incident, and on a failure suffer a Bout of Madness (see Tenacity and madness). Unless a Bout killed you, clear half y",""],["Rules","Stress track","Core Rules › Stress","rules/stress-and-madness.html#stress","Stress track: Your current Tenacity + 2 boxes (see Tenacity and madness). As you take Tenacity damage, your Stress track shrinks. ",""],["Rules","Shrinking track","Core Rules › Stress","rules/stress-and-madness.html#stress","Shrinking track: If your Stress track shrinks below the Stress you already have, the Stress that no longer fits is lost. It doesn't overflow again. ",""],["Rules","Push yourself","Core Rules › Stress","rules/stress-and-madness.html#stress","Push yourself: Before a roll, take 2 Stress to add +2d to it. ",""],["Rules","Extra reaction","Core Rules › Stress","rules/stress-and-madness.html#stress","Extra reaction: Once your reaction for the round is spent, take 2 Stress to react again. You can repeat this as long as you can pay. ",""],["Rules","Recovery roll","Core Rules › Stress","rules/stress-and-madness.html#stress","Recovery roll: Resolve + Composure. Clear 1 Stress per success. ",""],["Rules","Clearing Stress","Core Rules › Stress","rules/stress-and-madness.html#stress","Clearing Stress: Stress doesn't clear on its own with time. It clears at a rest (see Rest), at the end of a week of downtime, by catching your breath (see Actions), through talents, by surrendering while Beaten Down, and when it overflows (below). ",""],["Rules","Overflow","Core Rules › Stress","rules/stress-and-madness.html#stress","Overflow: You can still take Stress when your track is full, whether you chose to or an effect forced it on you. When Stress doesn't fit, it overflows: Each point that doesn't fit becomes 1 Tenacity damage. Your Stress track shrinks accordingly, and stays full. You make one madness check for the incident, and on a failure suffer a Bout of Madness (see Tenacity and madness). Unless a Bout killed you, clear half your Stress, rounded up. Any madness still plays out.",""],["Rules","Tenacity and madness","Core Rules › Tenacity and madness","rules/stress-and-madness.html#tenacity-and-madness","Tenacity measures a character's sanity. Your Tenacity track has Resolve + Composure + 3 boxes. Tenacity damage: Comes from Stress overflow and from effects that say so. Madness check: each incident that deals Tenacity damage calls for one check, however many points it cost: Endure against madness, rolling one die per point of Tenacity you have left (after the damage), and needing successes equal to the Tenacity damage from the incident. The more of your mind is gone, the less of it holds on. On a success, you hold together, and there's no Bout. On a failure, you suffer a Bout of Madness. Bout of Madness: roll 3d6, add the dice together, and subtract the total Tenacity you're missing. Some Scars add a further penalty. Look up the result below. Recovery: Tenacity doesn't heal on its own. It heals only through the Recuperate downtime action (see Downtime). Result Effect 0 or less Death. Your heart stops and you die. 1 Catatonia. You fall Prone and can't act or react. At the end of each hour, roll 1d; a success ends the madness. 2 Stricken. You become Stunned. At the end of each minute, roll 1d; a success ends the madness. 3 Sickened. You vomit uncontrollably and are Disoriented until ",""],["Rules","Tenacity","Core Rules › Tenacity and madness","rules/stress-and-madness.html#tenacity-and-madness","Tenacity measures a character's sanity. Your Tenacity track has Resolve + Composure + 3 boxes. ",""],["Rules","Tenacity damage","Core Rules › Tenacity and madness","rules/stress-and-madness.html#tenacity-and-madness","Tenacity damage: Comes from Stress overflow and from effects that say so. ",""],["Rules","Madness check","Core Rules › Tenacity and madness","rules/stress-and-madness.html#tenacity-and-madness","Madness check: each incident that deals Tenacity damage calls for one check, however many points it cost: Endure against madness, rolling one die per point of Tenacity you have left (after the damage), and needing successes equal to the Tenacity damage from the incident. The more of your mind is gone, the less of it holds on. On a success, you hold together, and there's no Bout. On a failure, you suffer a Bout of Madness. ",""],["Rules","Bout of Madness","Core Rules › Tenacity and madness","rules/stress-and-madness.html#tenacity-and-madness","Bout of Madness: roll 3d6, add the dice together, and subtract the total Tenacity you're missing. Some Scars add a further penalty. Look up the result below. ",""],["Rules","Recovery","Core Rules › Tenacity and madness","rules/stress-and-madness.html#tenacity-and-madness","Recovery: Tenacity doesn't heal on its own. It heals only through the Recuperate downtime action (see Downtime). ",""],["Rules","Turns and actions","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#turns-and-actions","Rounds: a round is about 6 seconds of in-world time. Fast and slow turns: Each player chooses at the start of every round. The round runs: fast PCs (2 actions), fast NPCs (2 actions), slow PCs (3 actions), slow NPCs (3 actions). Within a phase, players choose their own order. The GM assigns NPC speeds. Once per turn: Each action type can be taken once per turn, so one attack per turn by default. Talents that allow more say so explicitly. Move (1 action): move up to your Speed in meters (see Character Creation). Move is an exception to the once-per-turn rule: you can take it as many times as you have actions, so spending two actions moves you up to twice your Speed. Free actions cost no actions. Reaction: One per round. It refreshes at the start of your turn. Ambush: A character who is unaware of you when you attack or act against them is Surprised until the end of their next turn. A character is unsuspecting while Surprised or unaware of you.",""],["Rules","Rounds","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#turns-and-actions","Rounds: a round is about 6 seconds of in-world time. ",""],["Rules","Fast and slow turns","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#turns-and-actions","Fast and slow turns: Each player chooses at the start of every round. The round runs: fast PCs (2 actions), fast NPCs (2 actions), slow PCs (3 actions), slow NPCs (3 actions). Within a phase, players choose their own order. The GM assigns NPC speeds. ",""],["Rules","Once per turn","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#turns-and-actions","Once per turn: Each action type can be taken once per turn, so one attack per turn by default. Talents that allow more say so explicitly. ",""],["Rules","Move","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#turns-and-actions","Move (1 action): move up to your Speed in meters (see Character Creation). Move is an exception to the once-per-turn rule: you can take it as many times as you have actions, so spending two actions moves you up to twice your Speed. ",""],["Rules","Free actions","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#turns-and-actions","Free actions cost no actions. ",""],["Rules","Reaction","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#turns-and-actions","Reaction: One per round. It refreshes at the start of your turn. ",""],["Rules","Ambush","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#turns-and-actions","Ambush: A character who is unaware of you when you attack or act against them is Surprised until the end of their next turn. A character is unsuspecting while Surprised or unaware of you. ",""],["Rules","Usage limits","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#usage-limits","Limited abilities (talents, traits, a player's daily stakes raise) and durations are measured in in-world time. Limit Refreshes Once per round At the start of each round Once per minute One minute after use Once per hour One hour after use Once per day At the end of a rest",""],["Rules","Actions","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#actions","Action Cost Effect Attack 1 A weapon or unarmed attack (see Attacks). Once per turn. Move 1 Move up to your Speed. Can be taken more than once per turn. Disengage 1 Move 1 meter without provoking Reactive Strikes. Set up 1 Create an opening against a target (below). Brace 1 +1d to your defense rolls while you're in cover. Use a skill 1+ Pick a lock, give first aid, work a machine. The GM sets how many actions it takes. Interact 1 Draw or stow a weapon, open a door, grab an item. Stand up 1 Stand up from Prone. Grapple 1 Start or act in a grapple; counts as your attack (see Grappling). First aid 2 Stop an adjacent character's Bleeding (below). Catch your breath 2 Roll Composure and clear 1 Stress per success. Once per day. Set up: Describe how you create an opening, then roll an Attribute + Skill that fits, contested by the target. On a success, you get +1d on your next roll against that target before the end of your next turn. The target defends with the contested pool that fits: Intelligence + Resolve against feints, tricks and studying their weaknesses; Wits + Composure against taunts, pressure and intimidation; Dexterity + Stamina against physical maneuvers. First aid: Treat an ",""],["Rules","Success","Core Rules › Actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#actions","Success: the target stops Bleeding. First aid doesn't heal damage, and an unconscious target stays unconscious (see Waking up). ",""],["Rules","Opportunity","Core Rules › Actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#actions","Opportunity: choose a benefit as usual, such as +1d to the next Recover on the target at a rest. ",""],["Rules","Complication","Core Rules › Actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#actions","Complication: the GM adds a setback that fits, such as using one more charge. ",""],["Rules","Searching someone","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#searching-someone","The searcher makes one roll, usually Wits + Investigation. If the person being searched is actively hiding something, it's contested by their Dexterity + Thievery or Subterfuge; otherwise it needs a fixed number of successes set by the GM. Ordinary hidden items are found if the search succeeds. Discreet items need 1 more success: 1 extra success against a fixed difficulty, or at least 1 net success in a contest. One roll can find the pistol in your coat and still miss the knife in your boot.",""],["Rules","Ordinary hidden items","Core Rules › Searching someone","rules/turns-and-actions.html#searching-someone","Ordinary hidden items are found if the search succeeds. ",""],["Rules","Discreet items","Core Rules › Searching someone","rules/turns-and-actions.html#searching-someone","Discreet items need 1 more success: 1 extra success against a fixed difficulty, or at least 1 net success in a contest. ",""],["Rules","Ranges","Core Rules › Ranges","rules/turns-and-actions.html#ranges","On a battlemap, one square is 1 meter. Talents and rules use four fixed ranges: Range Distance Used for Adjacent 1 meter (the next square) Touching, melee, and moving \"a short distance\" Close Within 5 meters Anything \"close to you\": commands, aid and most support talents Near Within 10 meters (about one Move) Anything \"near\" a character, shouted orders, small areas Far Beyond 10 meters Sight and ranged weapons A short distance is 1 meter: one adjacent square. A small area is a 3 × 3 meter square (9 squares on a map). Talents can extend ranges. For example, Authority doubles the range of your Leader talents, so their \"close\" becomes 10 meters.",""],["Rules","A short distance","Core Rules › Ranges","rules/turns-and-actions.html#ranges","A short distance is 1 meter: one adjacent square. ",""],["Rules","A small area","Core Rules › Ranges","rules/turns-and-actions.html#ranges","A small area is a 3 × 3 meter square (9 squares on a map). ",""],["Rules","Talents can extend ranges","Core Rules › Ranges","rules/turns-and-actions.html#ranges","Talents can extend ranges. For example, Authority doubles the range of your Leader talents, so their \"close\" becomes 10 meters. ",""],["Rules","Terrain and forced movement","Core Rules › Ranges","rules/turns-and-actions.html#terrain-and-forced-movement","Difficult terrain (rubble, deep mud, a crowd, an entangling trap): moving through it costs double, so a Move covers half your Speed there. You take −1d to Physical rolls while standing in it: any roll using a Physical attribute or skill, including attacks and defense rolls (Dodge, Parry or rolled Defense). Dangerous terrain (fire, spikes, Dusk-blighted ground): terrain that deals damage when you enter it or end your turn in it. The source sets the damage. Pushed back: the effect that pushes you says how far. You move that distance straight away from the source. If something solid stops you early, you fall Prone. Being pushed doesn't provoke Reactive Strikes. Armor can take 1 Wear to ignore a push (see Armor).",""],["Rules","Difficult terrain","Core Rules › Terrain and forced movement","rules/turns-and-actions.html#terrain-and-forced-movement","Difficult terrain (rubble, deep mud, a crowd, an entangling trap): moving through it costs double, so a Move covers half your Speed there. You take −1d to Physical rolls while standing in it: any roll using a Physical attribute or skill, including attacks and defense rolls (Dodge, Parry or rolled Defense). ",""],["Rules","Dangerous terrain","Core Rules › Terrain and forced movement","rules/turns-and-actions.html#terrain-and-forced-movement","Dangerous terrain (fire, spikes, Dusk-blighted ground): terrain that deals damage when you enter it or end your turn in it. The source sets the damage. ",""],["Rules","Pushed back","Core Rules › Terrain and forced movement","rules/turns-and-actions.html#terrain-and-forced-movement","Pushed back: the effect that pushes you says how far. You move that distance straight away from the source. If something solid stops you early, you fall Prone. Being pushed doesn't provoke Reactive Strikes. Armor can take 1 Wear to ignore a push (see Armor). ",""],["Rules","Attacks","Core Rules › Attacks","rules/attacks.html#attacks","Attack pools: Strength + Weaponry for melee weapons, Strength + Brawl for unarmed attacks, and Dexterity + Ranged for ranged and thrown weapons. Talents can change the attribute for some weapons. Hits: Every attack is contested (see Defense and reactions). It hits if the attacker's successes equal or beat the defender's; a tie is a hit with 0 net successes, dealing the weapon's damage alone. Damage: A hit deals damage equal to the net successes plus the weapon's damage, of the weapon's damage type. Armor reduces it (see Armor). Opportunity on attacks is based on the attacker's total successes, not net successes. Off-hand attack: while holding a Light weapon in your other hand, take 1 Stress to make one extra attack with it (1 action). This is an exception to the one-attack rule. Extra attacks: each attack after your first in a turn takes a cumulative −2d (−2d on the second, −4d on the third), whatever grants it. A single action that attacks several targets, such as Unstoppable Charge, counts as one attack.",""],["Rules","Attack pools","Core Rules › Attacks","rules/attacks.html#attacks","Attack pools: Strength + Weaponry for melee weapons, Strength + Brawl for unarmed attacks, and Dexterity + Ranged for ranged and thrown weapons. Talents can change the attribute for some weapons. ",""],["Rules","Hits","Core Rules › Attacks","rules/attacks.html#attacks","Hits: Every attack is contested (see Defense and reactions). It hits if the attacker's successes equal or beat the defender's; a tie is a hit with 0 net successes, dealing the weapon's damage alone. ",""],["Rules","Damage","Core Rules › Attacks","rules/attacks.html#attacks","Damage: A hit deals damage equal to the net successes plus the weapon's damage, of the weapon's damage type. Armor reduces it (see Armor). ",""],["Rules","Opportunity on attacks","Core Rules › Attacks","rules/attacks.html#attacks","Opportunity on attacks is based on the attacker's total successes, not net successes. ",""],["Rules","Off-hand attack","Core Rules › Attacks","rules/attacks.html#attacks","Off-hand attack: while holding a Light weapon in your other hand, take 1 Stress to make one extra attack with it (1 action). This is an exception to the one-attack rule. ",""],["Rules","Extra attacks","Core Rules › Attacks","rules/attacks.html#attacks","Extra attacks: each attack after your first in a turn takes a cumulative −2d (−2d on the second, −4d on the third), whatever grants it. A single action that attacks several targets, such as Unstoppable Charge, counts as one attack. ",""],["Rules","Called shots","Core Rules › Attacks","rules/attacks.html#called-shots","Before rolling an attack, you can aim at a specific spot. Take the penalty, and on a hit, add the effect: Target Penalty Effect on a hit Arm or hand −1d The target drops what it's holding Leg −1d The target is Slowed Head −2d +1 damage Gap in armor −3d Ignore the target's armor rating; the 1-bashing floor still applies",""],["Rules","Grappling","Core Rules › Grappling","rules/attacks.html#grappling","Grapple (1 action, counts as your attack): start or act in a grapple. Starting a grapple: Roll Strength + Brawl, contested like any attack (Dodge, Parry or Defense). On a hit, you and the target are both Grappled. It deals no damage. Acting in a grapple: everyone in a grapple acts at the same time. Once per round, when the first of them takes the Grapple action, everyone in the grapple rolls Strength + Brawl together, and each spends their Grapple action for the round on it (it's their attack), even if their turn hasn't come yet. The one with the most successes wins, with net successes counted against the best opposing roll, and chooses one move against an opponent in the grapple, or two on an Opportunity. On a tie, no one gains ground. The moves: Break free: you're no longer Grappled. Damage: deal bashing damage equal to your net successes, or lethal damage equal to your net successes + weapon damage if you control a Light weapon. Control weapon: take control of a weapon in the grapple, or draw your own Light weapon. Disarm: a weapon you control drops or is thrown away. Drop Prone: you both fall Prone. You can instead drop only the opponent and break free. Hold: until the grapple'",""],["Rules","Grapple","Core Rules › Grappling","rules/attacks.html#grappling","Grapple (1 action, counts as your attack): start or act in a grapple. ",""],["Rules","Starting a grapple","Core Rules › Grappling","rules/attacks.html#grappling","Starting a grapple: Roll Strength + Brawl, contested like any attack (Dodge, Parry or Defense). On a hit, you and the target are both Grappled. It deals no damage. ",""],["Rules","Acting in a grapple","Core Rules › Grappling","rules/attacks.html#grappling","Acting in a grapple: everyone in a grapple acts at the same time. Once per round, when the first of them takes the Grapple action, everyone in the grapple rolls Strength + Brawl together, and each spends their Grapple action for the round on it (it's their attack), even if their turn hasn't come yet. The one with the most successes wins, with net successes counted against the best opposing roll, and chooses one move against an opponent in the grapple, or two on an Opportunity. On a tie, no one gains ground. The moves: Break free: you're no longer Grappled. Damage: deal bashing damage equal to your net successes, or lethal damage equal to your net successes + weapon damage if you control a Light weapon. Control weapon: take control of a weapon in the grapple, or draw your own Light weapon. Disarm: a weapon you control drops or is thrown away. Drop Prone: you both fall Prone. You can instead drop only the opponent and break free. Hold: until the grapple's next roll, the opponent can't do anything but struggle in it. Restrain: with rope, cuffs or a hold that locks, the opponent is Immobilized even after you let go. Requires Hold first. Take cover: until your next turn, ranged attacks ",""],["Rules","Defense and reactions","Core Rules › Defense and reactions","rules/defense-and-reactions.html#defense-and-reactions","Defense = the lower of Dexterity and Wits. Defending: You contest an attack with a reaction (Dodge or Parry). If you can't or don't react, you still contest it by rolling your Defense. Dodge, Parry and rolled Defense are all defense rolls. Bonuses and penalties: an effect that changes your Defense itself (such as heavy armor's −1) changes every defense roll, twice over on a Dodge. Most bonuses instead add dice to a specific roll and are written that way: \"+1d to Parry\", \"+1d to Dodge\", or \"+1d to defense rolls\" (any of the three, added once). Dodge (reaction): Roll twice your Defense with the stakes die. A Complication leaves you Prone. An Opportunity lets you Disengage as a free action. Dodging spends your Defense: until the start of your next turn, you can't Parry or roll Defense against attacks, and attacks against you are uncontested. Parry (reaction, melee only): Roll Defense + Weaponry if you're holding a weapon, or Defense + Brawl if you're unarmed. Parrying an armed attack unarmed takes −2d. Graze: When an attack misses, take 1 Stress to deal 1 damage of one severity lower than the attack's (lethal becomes bashing, aggravated becomes lethal). Bashing attacks can't graze. Re",""],["Rules","Defense","Core Rules › Defense and reactions","rules/defense-and-reactions.html#defense-and-reactions","Defense = the lower of Dexterity and Wits. ",""],["Rules","Defending","Core Rules › Defense and reactions","rules/defense-and-reactions.html#defense-and-reactions","Defending: You contest an attack with a reaction (Dodge or Parry). If you can't or don't react, you still contest it by rolling your Defense. Dodge, Parry and rolled Defense are all defense rolls. ",""],["Rules","Bonuses and penalties","Core Rules › Defense and reactions","rules/defense-and-reactions.html#defense-and-reactions","Bonuses and penalties: an effect that changes your Defense itself (such as heavy armor's −1) changes every defense roll, twice over on a Dodge. Most bonuses instead add dice to a specific roll and are written that way: \"+1d to Parry\", \"+1d to Dodge\", or \"+1d to defense rolls\" (any of the three, added once). ",""],["Rules","Dodge","Core Rules › Defense and reactions","rules/defense-and-reactions.html#defense-and-reactions","Dodge (reaction): Roll twice your Defense with the stakes die. A Complication leaves you Prone. An Opportunity lets you Disengage as a free action. Dodging spends your Defense: until the start of your next turn, you can't Parry or roll Defense against attacks, and attacks against you are uncontested. ",""],["Rules","Parry","Core Rules › Defense and reactions","rules/defense-and-reactions.html#defense-and-reactions","Parry (reaction, melee only): Roll Defense + Weaponry if you're holding a weapon, or Defense + Brawl if you're unarmed. Parrying an armed attack unarmed takes −2d. ",""],["Rules","Graze","Core Rules › Defense and reactions","rules/defense-and-reactions.html#defense-and-reactions","Graze: When an attack misses, take 1 Stress to deal 1 damage of one severity lower than the attack's (lethal becomes bashing, aggravated becomes lethal). Bashing attacks can't graze. ",""],["Rules","Reactive Strike","Core Rules › Defense and reactions","rules/defense-and-reactions.html#defense-and-reactions","Reactive Strike (reaction): When an enemy leaves your melee reach, make a melee attack against them. ",""],["Rules","Feints and mental attacks","Core Rules › Defense and reactions","rules/defense-and-reactions.html#defense-and-reactions","Feints and mental attacks: Attacks contested by Intelligence + Resolve can't be Dodged or Parried, and the defender doesn't spend a reaction. ",""],["Rules","Endure","Core Rules › Defense and reactions","rules/defense-and-reactions.html#defense-and-reactions","Endure (reaction): your defense against harm that isn't an attack: persistent damage, poison, and any hazard or effect that calls for it (a setting's, like Duskworld's Dusk). Roll Stamina + Resolve, unless the threat names another pool, and compare your successes to what the threat says. Outside combat, Endure costs no reaction. In combat, if your reaction is spent and you don't take Stress for another one, you don't roll, and take the full effect. ",""],["Rules","Endure bonuses","Core Rules › Defense and reactions","rules/defense-and-reactions.html#defense-and-reactions","Endure bonuses are written \"+1d to Endure\", which helps against every threat, or \"+1d to Endure against\" a named threat (poison, the Dusk), which helps only against that one. ",""],["Rules","Armor","Core Rules › Armor","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#armor","Armor rating: When an attack hits or grazes you, subtract your armor rating from the damage. Damage that gets through keeps its type. If armor absorbs all of it, you still take 1 bashing damage. Wear: Armor has a Wear track with boxes equal to its current rating. It takes 1 Wear when a hit gets past it (damage beyond the 1-bashing floor) or when the attack scores an Opportunity, at most 1 Wear per hit. You can also have your armor take 1 Wear to ignore one condition a hit would cause, such as Prone, Bleeding or Stunned, or being pushed back. Battered: When the Wear track fills, the armor's rating drops by 1. A full Wear track takes no more Wear from hits. You can still choose to have it take Wear to ignore a condition; each point that doesn't fit lowers the rating by 1 more. Prepare at a rest clears Wear. If a rest ends with the Wear track still full, the rating drops by 1 more. Lost rating comes back only when the armor is repaired during downtime. Broken: Armor reduced to rating 0 is broken. It gives no protection until repaired during downtime. Armor Rating Penalty Bulk Light (leather, padded coat) 1 None 1 Medium (mail, brigandine) 2 −1d to Stealth and Athletics 2 Heavy (plate)",""],["Rules","Armor rating","Core Rules › Armor","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#armor","Armor rating: When an attack hits or grazes you, subtract your armor rating from the damage. Damage that gets through keeps its type. If armor absorbs all of it, you still take 1 bashing damage. ",""],["Rules","Wear","Core Rules › Armor","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#armor","Wear: Armor has a Wear track with boxes equal to its current rating. It takes 1 Wear when a hit gets past it (damage beyond the 1-bashing floor) or when the attack scores an Opportunity, at most 1 Wear per hit. You can also have your armor take 1 Wear to ignore one condition a hit would cause, such as Prone, Bleeding or Stunned, or being pushed back. ",""],["Rules","Battered","Core Rules › Armor","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#armor","Battered: When the Wear track fills, the armor's rating drops by 1. A full Wear track takes no more Wear from hits. You can still choose to have it take Wear to ignore a condition; each point that doesn't fit lowers the rating by 1 more. Prepare at a rest clears Wear. If a rest ends with the Wear track still full, the rating drops by 1 more. Lost rating comes back only when the armor is repaired during downtime. ",""],["Rules","Broken","Core Rules › Armor","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#armor","Broken: Armor reduced to rating 0 is broken. It gives no protection until repaired during downtime. ",""],["Rules","Weapons","Core Rules › Weapons","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapons","Each setting chooses which weapons exist. Weapon Damage Traits Bulk Unarmed 0 bashing — — Club 1 bashing Stunning 1 Sap 1 bashing Stunning, Discreet — Knife 1 lethal Light, Discreet, Throwable, Precise — Rapier 1 lethal Light, Piercing 1, Precise 1 Short sword, machete 2 lethal — 1 Long sword 2 lethal Versatile 1 Spear 2 lethal Reach, Two-handed 2 Warhammer 3 lethal Heavy, Knockdown 2 Greatsword 4 lethal Heavy, Two-handed 3 Maul, sledgehammer 4 lethal Heavy, Two-handed, Knockdown 3 Small shield 0 bashing Shield (1) 1 Large shield 1 bashing Shield (2), Heavy 2 Bow 2 lethal Range 30 m, Two-handed 2 Crossbow 3 lethal Range 30 m, Slow, Two-handed 2 Pistol 2 lethal Range 20 m 1 Rifle 4 lethal Range 60 m, Slow, Two-handed, Precise 2",""],["Rules","Weapon traits","Core Rules › Weapons","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Deadly: An Opportunity on the attack adds +1 lethal damage. Discreet: When someone searches you, finding this item takes 1 more success than finding anything else (see Searching someone). Heavy: Needs Strength 3; −1d per missing dot. Knockdown: On an Opportunity, the target falls Prone. Light: Small and one-handed. Usable while Grappled. Piercing X: Ignores X points of armor rating. Precise: On called shots, 6s explode. Quickdraw: Drawing or stowing it is a free action. Range: Attacks beyond it take −2d, up to double the range. Reach: Attack a target up to 2 meters away. Shield (X): +X dice to Parry while held. With Shield (2) or higher, it also counts as cover against ranged attacks. Slow: Reloading takes 1 action. Stunning: On an Opportunity, the target is Stunned until the end of their next turn. Throwable: Can be thrown with Dexterity + Ranged, Range 10 m. Two-handed: Needs both hands, so you can't hold a shield, a second weapon or a light source while attacking with it. Versatile: Can be used one- or two-handed; +1 damage when used two-handed.",""],["Traits","Deadly","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Deadly: An Opportunity on the attack adds +1 lethal damage. ",""],["Traits","Discreet","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Discreet: When someone searches you, finding this item takes 1 more success than finding anything else (see Searching someone). ",""],["Traits","Heavy","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Heavy: Needs Strength 3; −1d per missing dot. ",""],["Traits","Knockdown","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Knockdown: On an Opportunity, the target falls Prone. ",""],["Traits","Light","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Light: Small and one-handed. Usable while Grappled. ",""],["Traits","Piercing X","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Piercing X: Ignores X points of armor rating. ",""],["Traits","Precise","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Precise: On called shots, 6s explode. ",""],["Traits","Quickdraw","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Quickdraw: Drawing or stowing it is a free action. ",""],["Traits","Range","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Range: Attacks beyond it take −2d, up to double the range. ",""],["Traits","Reach","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Reach: Attack a target up to 2 meters away. ",""],["Traits","Shield (X)","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Shield (X): +X dice to Parry while held. With Shield (2) or higher, it also counts as cover against ranged attacks. ",""],["Traits","Slow","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Slow: Reloading takes 1 action. ",""],["Traits","Stunning","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Stunning: On an Opportunity, the target is Stunned until the end of their next turn. ",""],["Traits","Throwable","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Throwable: Can be thrown with Dexterity + Ranged, Range 10 m. ",""],["Traits","Two-handed","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Two-handed: Needs both hands, so you can't hold a shield, a second weapon or a light source while attacking with it. ",""],["Traits","Versatile","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Versatile: Can be used one- or two-handed; +1 damage when used two-handed. ",""],["Rules","Kits","Core Rules › Kits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#kits","A kit is a set of tools for one kind of work. It gives +1d to the rolls it covers. Working with no proper tools at all gives −1d. Charges: a kit has 3 charges. Spend 1 each time you take its +1d. A Complication on that roll spends 1 more. Empty kit: at 0 charges you still have the tools (no −1d), but you get no bonus. Refilling: each Prepare at a rest refills 1 charge on each of your kits. In a settlement you can buy extra rest actions (see Rest) to Prepare again. A week of downtime refills every kit to full. Kit Covers Medical kit Medicine: First aid, Recover Tool kit Crafts: repairs, building Thieves' tools Thievery: locks, safes Climbing gear Athletics: climbing, rope work Survival kit Survival: camping, foraging, tracking Disguise kit Subterfuge: disguises Writing case Academics or Expression: records, correspondence, forgery Other consumables (ammunition, lamp oil, rations, bandages beyond a kit) aren't counted. If you've used Prepare, you have them; a Complication can still use them up.",""],["Rules","Charges","Core Rules › Kits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#kits","Charges: a kit has 3 charges. Spend 1 each time you take its +1d. A Complication on that roll spends 1 more. ",""],["Rules","Empty kit","Core Rules › Kits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#kits","Empty kit: at 0 charges you still have the tools (no −1d), but you get no bonus. ",""],["Rules","Refilling","Core Rules › Kits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#kits","Refilling: each Prepare at a rest refills 1 charge on each of your kits. In a settlement you can buy extra rest actions (see Rest) to Prepare again. A week of downtime refills every kit to full. ",""],["Rules","Encumbrance","Core Rules › Encumbrance","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#encumbrance","Every item has a bulk from 1 to 3, or no bulk at all. Add up the bulk of everything you carry, including worn armor, to find your load. Bulk Examples None Papers, money in handfuls, clothing, a knife, a sap and other Discreet items 1 One-handed weapons, a pistol, a small shield, each kit, a lantern, light armor, a bag of wealth 2 Two-handed weapons, one-handed heavy weapons, bows, crossbows, rifles, a large shield, medium armor 3 Two-handed heavy weapons, heavy armor, a chest of wealth The weapon and armor tables list each item's bulk. Load limits depend on Strength: a light load is up to Strength + 1, a normal load up to twice that, and a heavy load up to three times that. Strength Light Normal Heavy 1 2 4 6 2 3 6 9 3 4 8 12 4 5 10 15 5 6 12 18 Light load: +1d to Athletics and Stealth. Normal load: no effect. Heavy load: −1d to Athletics and Stealth, −2 Speed, and you can't take fast turns. Each day of travel under a heavy load gives 1 stack of Exhausted. Over a heavy load: you can't take the Move action, only Disengage, until you drop something.",""],["Rules","Light load","Core Rules › Encumbrance","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#encumbrance","Light load: +1d to Athletics and Stealth. ",""],["Rules","Normal load","Core Rules › Encumbrance","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#encumbrance","Normal load: no effect. ",""],["Rules","Heavy load","Core Rules › Encumbrance","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#encumbrance","Heavy load: −1d to Athletics and Stealth, −2 Speed, and you can't take fast turns. Each day of travel under a heavy load gives 1 stack of Exhausted. ",""],["Rules","Over a heavy load","Core Rules › Encumbrance","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#encumbrance","Over a heavy load: you can't take the Move action, only Disengage, until you drop something. ",""],["Rules","NPCs","Core Rules › NPCs","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#npcs","Each NPC has a single rating from 1 to 5. They roll double the rating for things they're proficient in, the rating as-is for things they know, and a chance die for everything else. Enemy types can add modifiers. When a talent says a roll is \"contested by\" a pool (such as Intelligence + Resolve), an NPC rolls their rating by the rule above instead. Stress: Minions have none. Important NPCs have Stress equal to twice their rating. Bosses have four times their rating. No Tenacity: NPCs have no Tenacity track, so their Stress doesn't overflow. An NPC with a full Stress track can't pay Stress costs. Minions count as having a full Stress track, so they can't pay Stress costs. Health: Important NPCs have Health equal to Size + rating. Bosses have custom Health, usually double an important NPC's. Minions have no Health track (below). Beaten Down: Important NPCs and bosses are Beaten Down when their total damage exceeds half their Health, rounded up. Taking out minions: Any lethal or aggravated damage takes a minion out: they're down and bleeding to death. Bashing damage takes a minion out only if a single hit deals bashing at least equal to its rating; smaller hits are shrugged off. A mini",""],["Rules","Stress","Core Rules › NPCs","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#npcs","Stress: Minions have none. Important NPCs have Stress equal to twice their rating. Bosses have four times their rating. ",""],["Rules","No Tenacity","Core Rules › NPCs","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#npcs","No Tenacity: NPCs have no Tenacity track, so their Stress doesn't overflow. An NPC with a full Stress track can't pay Stress costs. ",""],["Rules","Minions","Core Rules › NPCs","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#npcs","Minions count as having a full Stress track, so they can't pay Stress costs. ",""],["Rules","Health","Core Rules › NPCs","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#npcs","Health: Important NPCs have Health equal to Size + rating. Bosses have custom Health, usually double an important NPC's. Minions have no Health track (below). ",""],["Rules","Beaten Down","Core Rules › NPCs","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#npcs","Beaten Down: Important NPCs and bosses are Beaten Down when their total damage exceeds half their Health, rounded up. ",""],["Rules","Taking out minions","Core Rules › NPCs","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#npcs","Taking out minions: Any lethal or aggravated damage takes a minion out: they're down and bleeding to death. Bashing damage takes a minion out only if a single hit deals bashing at least equal to its rating; smaller hits are shrugged off. A minion taken out by bashing is unconscious, surrenders or flees, whichever fits the scene. ",""],["Rules","Defense","Core Rules › NPCs","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#npcs","Defense: Minions roll their rating to defend against attacks; they don't Dodge or Parry. Important NPCs and bosses have a Defense set by their type and use the same rules as PCs. ",""],["Rules","Armor","Core Rules › NPCs","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#npcs","Armor: A minion's armor is a static rating with no Wear track. Important NPCs and bosses track Wear like PCs. ",""],["Rules","Damage","Core Rules › NPCs","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#npcs","Damage: NPC attacks deal damage like PCs': net successes plus weapon damage. ",""],["Rules","Companions","Core Rules › Companions","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#companions","A companion is an animal that works with you. It's an NPC with a rating (1–5) and rolls like one: double its rating for what it's proficient in, its rating for what it knows, and a chance die for everything else. Health: Size + rating. It heals like a character; Recover on an animal uses Animal Ken instead of Medicine. No Stress: it counts as having a full Stress track, so it can't pay Stress costs. Defense: it rolls its rating to defend, like a minion. It doesn't Dodge or Parry. Acting: it has no turn of its own. On your turn, you can spend your own actions to have it act. Talents such as Animal Bond give it actions for free. Orders: outside a fight, unusual or dangerous orders need an Animal Ken roll. Getting one: Animal Bond gives a rating 2 animal of your choice: one from the table, or another animal you and the GM build at rating 2. Otherwise, a trained animal costs a bag. Companion Rating Size Health Speed Attack Proficient in Hound 2 4 6 13 Bite, 1 lethal Tracking, noticing, biting Wolfhound 3 5 8 12 Bite, 2 lethal Biting, tracking, intimidating Bird of prey 2 2 4 20, flying Talons, 1 lethal Noticing, scouting, harrying Horse 2 7 9 20 Kick, 2 bashing Running, carrying, kicki",""],["Rules","Health","Core Rules › Companions","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#companions","Health: Size + rating. It heals like a character; Recover on an animal uses Animal Ken instead of Medicine. ",""],["Rules","No Stress","Core Rules › Companions","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#companions","No Stress: it counts as having a full Stress track, so it can't pay Stress costs. ",""],["Rules","Defense","Core Rules › Companions","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#companions","Defense: it rolls its rating to defend, like a minion. It doesn't Dodge or Parry. ",""],["Rules","Acting","Core Rules › Companions","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#companions","Acting: it has no turn of its own. On your turn, you can spend your own actions to have it act. Talents such as Animal Bond give it actions for free. ",""],["Rules","Orders","Core Rules › Companions","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#companions","Orders: outside a fight, unusual or dangerous orders need an Animal Ken roll. ",""],["Rules","Getting one","Core Rules › Companions","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#companions","Getting one: Animal Bond gives a rating 2 animal of your choice: one from the table, or another animal you and the GM build at rating 2. Otherwise, a trained animal costs a bag. ",""],["Rules","Damage and Health","Core Rules › Damage and Health","rules/damage-and-health.html#damage-and-health","Health track: Size + Stamina boxes. Damage types: From least to most severe: bashing, lethal and aggravated. Filling the track: The track stays sorted by severity: aggravated damage fills from the left, then lethal, then bashing. New damage that is more severe pushes less severe damage to the right. Overflow: When your Health track is full, new bashing damage becomes lethal, and new lethal damage becomes aggravated. Last box: Bashing in your last Health box: you fall Unconscious. Lethal in your last Health box: you fall Unconscious and start Bleeding. Aggravated can only reach your last box once the whole track is aggravated: you die. Waking up: You wake from damage-caused unconsciousness when your last Health box clears, whether through natural healing or an ally's healing. At the start of your turn, unless you chose to Avoid Death, you can also push yourself to wake up instead of gaining +2d. Waking doesn't heal you: any damage still in your last box keeps affecting you (Bleeding continues), and one more point of damage knocks you out again. Beaten Down threshold: Stamina + Resolve. When your total damage, of any type, exceeds it, you are Beaten Down.",""],["Rules","Health track","Core Rules › Damage and Health","rules/damage-and-health.html#damage-and-health","Health track: Size + Stamina boxes. ",""],["Rules","Damage types","Core Rules › Damage and Health","rules/damage-and-health.html#damage-and-health","Damage types: From least to most severe: bashing, lethal and aggravated. ",""],["Rules","Filling the track","Core Rules › Damage and Health","rules/damage-and-health.html#damage-and-health","Filling the track: The track stays sorted by severity: aggravated damage fills from the left, then lethal, then bashing. New damage that is more severe pushes less severe damage to the right. ",""],["Rules","Overflow","Core Rules › Damage and Health","rules/damage-and-health.html#damage-and-health","Overflow: When your Health track is full, new bashing damage becomes lethal, and new lethal damage becomes aggravated. ",""],["Rules","Last box","Core Rules › Damage and Health","rules/damage-and-health.html#damage-and-health","Last box: Bashing in your last Health box: you fall Unconscious. Lethal in your last Health box: you fall Unconscious and start Bleeding. Aggravated can only reach your last box once the whole track is aggravated: you die.",""],["Rules","Waking up","Core Rules › Damage and Health","rules/damage-and-health.html#damage-and-health","Waking up: You wake from damage-caused unconsciousness when your last Health box clears, whether through natural healing or an ally's healing. At the start of your turn, unless you chose to Avoid Death, you can also push yourself to wake up instead of gaining +2d. Waking doesn't heal you: any damage still in your last box keeps affecting you (Bleeding continues), and one more point of damage knocks you out again. ",""],["Rules","Beaten Down threshold","Core Rules › Damage and Health","rules/damage-and-health.html#damage-and-health","Beaten Down threshold: Stamina + Resolve. When your total damage, of any type, exceeds it, you are Beaten Down. ",""],["Rules","Avoiding death","Core Rules › Avoiding death","rules/damage-and-health.html#avoiding-death","When damage knocks you Unconscious, you choose what happens next. Avoid Death: take a Death's Mark. You stop Bleeding, and further damage can't kill you while you're unconscious. You can't be targeted by attacks unless an opponent specifically targets unconscious creatures (a creature's description says so). You wake only when an ally heals 1 or more of your damage, or when the party finishes a rest. You can't push yourself to wake up: you've chosen to step out of the fight, and the only other way to act is a Blaze of Glory. If you don't avoid death, the normal rules apply: you may keep Bleeding, and further damage can kill you (see Waking up). Blaze of Glory: each time your turn comes while you're still unconscious, whether or not you avoided death, you can choose to go out in a Blaze of Glory. Take one turn as normal (fast or slow). Each roll you make counts as already having one success with Opportunity. At the end of the turn, you cross through the veil of death.",""],["Rules","Avoid Death","Core Rules › Avoiding death","rules/damage-and-health.html#avoiding-death","Avoid Death: take a Death's Mark. You stop Bleeding, and further damage can't kill you while you're unconscious. You can't be targeted by attacks unless an opponent specifically targets unconscious creatures (a creature's description says so). You wake only when an ally heals 1 or more of your damage, or when the party finishes a rest. You can't push yourself to wake up: you've chosen to step out of the fight, and the only other way to act is a Blaze of Glory. ",""],["Rules","If you don't avoid death,","Core Rules › Avoiding death","rules/damage-and-health.html#avoiding-death","If you don't avoid death, the normal rules apply: you may keep Bleeding, and further damage can kill you (see Waking up). ",""],["Rules","Blaze of Glory","Core Rules › Avoiding death","rules/damage-and-health.html#avoiding-death","Blaze of Glory: each time your turn comes while you're still unconscious, whether or not you avoided death, you can choose to go out in a Blaze of Glory. Take one turn as normal (fast or slow). Each roll you make counts as already having one success with Opportunity. At the end of the turn, you cross through the veil of death. ",""],["Rules","Scars","Core Rules › Scars","rules/damage-and-health.html#scars","A Scar is a lasting mark on a character. Scars don't heal with rest or downtime; only the story can remove one. Some Scars build up in stages and are written with their stage, Name N: for example, Duskworld's Dusking 2. Settings define these, including what each stage does and what happens at the last one. They're separate from Death's Marks and don't count toward their limit.",""],["Rules","Death's Marks","Core Rules › Scars","rules/damage-and-health.html#deaths-marks","Death's Marks are the Scars you take when you Avoid Death. Choose one of these penalties. Each Death's Mark must be a different one, so a character can avoid death at most three times; the fourth is always the last. Permanently reduce your Health by 1. Permanently reduce your Tenacity by 1. Your Stress track shrinks with it. Permanently reduce one attribute by 1. Derived values that use it change too. Cross through the veil of death. You die.",""],["Rules","Permanently reduce your Health by 1","Core Rules › Death's Marks","rules/damage-and-health.html#deaths-marks","Permanently reduce your Health by 1. ",""],["Rules","Permanently reduce your Tenacity by 1","Core Rules › Death's Marks","rules/damage-and-health.html#deaths-marks","Permanently reduce your Tenacity by 1. Your Stress track shrinks with it. ",""],["Rules","Permanently reduce one attribute by 1","Core Rules › Death's Marks","rules/damage-and-health.html#deaths-marks","Permanently reduce one attribute by 1. Derived values that use it change too. ",""],["Rules","Cross through the veil of death","Core Rules › Death's Marks","rules/damage-and-health.html#deaths-marks","Cross through the veil of death. You die. ",""],["Rules","Persistent damage","Core Rules › Persistent damage","rules/damage-and-health.html#persistent-damage","Persistent damage (such as Bleeding) is applied at the end of the affected character's turn, so they always get to act first. Enduring it: before taking the damage, you can Endure. If your successes equal or exceed the number of rounds the effect has left, you take no damage that round. The effect still continues.",""],["Rules","Enduring it","Core Rules › Persistent damage","rules/damage-and-health.html#persistent-damage","Enduring it: before taking the damage, you can Endure. If your successes equal or exceed the number of rounds the effect has left, you take no damage that round. The effect still continues. ",""],["Rules","Hazards","Core Rules › Hazards","rules/damage-and-health.html#hazards","Common dangers of the world. Settings add their own, such as Duskworld's Dusk exposure. Armor doesn't reduce hazard damage. Hazard Effect Falling 1 bashing damage per 3 meters fallen, or lethal if you land on something sharp. Roll Dexterity + Athletics as you fall (no action): each success reduces the damage by 1. A fall of 30 meters or more is fatal unless something breaks it, such as deep water or a canopy. Fire Lethal damage at the end of each of your turns in it: 1 for a torch or small fire, 2 for a bonfire, 3 for a burning building or worse. Drowning and suffocation You can hold your breath for rounds equal to 5 × your Stamina. After that, take 1 lethal damage at the end of each of your turns until you can breathe. Extreme cold or heat Without protection, after hours equal to your Stamina, take 1 bashing damage each hour, or 1 lethal in severe conditions (a blizzard, a desert at noon). Poison A poison has a rating from 1 to 5. It deals 1 lethal damage each hour (each round for a fast poison) for intervals equal to its rating. Before each, you can Endure: with successes equal to or above its rating, you take no damage that interval. Hunger, thirst and sleeplessness A day withou",""],["Rules","Natural healing","Core Rules › Natural healing","rules/damage-and-health.html#natural-healing","Wounds heal one at a time, least severe first. Damage Heals Bashing 1 per hour Lethal 1 per rest Aggravated 1 per week of downtime Longer stretches of free time use the Downtime rules: a week of downtime heals all bashing and lethal damage, and 1 aggravated wound.",""],["Rules","Rest","Core Rules › Rest","rules/rest.html#rest","A rest is a night's sleep and the free time around it, or a few hours of real relaxation, usually once per day. At the end of a rest, every character: clears 1 Stress, heals all bashing damage and 1 lethal wound (natural healing), removes 1 stack of Exhausted, refreshes their once-per-day abilities. Each character also takes two rest actions. You can take the same action more than once, but each character can receive Recover only once per rest. In a settlement, a character can pay 2 handfuls for each extra rest action. Recover: Roll Intelligence + Medicine on a patient. On a success, they heal 1 lethal wound. On a success with Opportunity, they heal 2 lethal wounds instead, or 1 aggravated wound becomes lethal. You can treat yourself, but if you're Beaten Down, you take 1 Stress. Prepare: Replenish your consumables, clear all your armor's Wear, and refill 1 charge on each of your kits. Broken gear and lost armor rating must be repaired during downtime. Respite: Make a recovery roll (Resolve + Composure). Clear 1 extra Stress per success when the rest ends. Forage: Roll Wits + Survival. Each success supplies one character for the day. On a success with Opportunity, you also find som",""],["Rules","Recover","Core Rules › Rest","rules/rest.html#rest","Recover: Roll Intelligence + Medicine on a patient. On a success, they heal 1 lethal wound. On a success with Opportunity, they heal 2 lethal wounds instead, or 1 aggravated wound becomes lethal. You can treat yourself, but if you're Beaten Down, you take 1 Stress. ",""],["Rules","Prepare","Core Rules › Rest","rules/rest.html#rest","Prepare: Replenish your consumables, clear all your armor's Wear, and refill 1 charge on each of your kits. Broken gear and lost armor rating must be repaired during downtime. ",""],["Rules","Respite","Core Rules › Rest","rules/rest.html#rest","Respite: Make a recovery roll (Resolve + Composure). Clear 1 extra Stress per success when the rest ends. ",""],["Rules","Forage","Core Rules › Rest","rules/rest.html#rest","Forage: Roll Wits + Survival. Each success supplies one character for the day. On a success with Opportunity, you also find something useful, such as herbs that give +1d to the next Recover. ",""],["Rules","Scout","Core Rules › Rest","rules/rest.html#rest","Scout: Roll Wits + Investigation or Stealth. On a success, learn one fact about the area ahead. On a success with Opportunity, the party can't be Surprised by the first threat they meet in that area. ",""],["Rules","Conditions","Core Rules › Conditions","rules/conditions.html#conditions","A condition doesn't stack unless it says so (Exhausted does). Gaining a condition you already have keeps whichever duration is longer. Condition Effect Beaten Down Making an attack costs 1 Stress, unless you have no chance to surrender (the enemy won't or can't accept it, such as a mindless beast). If you surrender, clear 1 Stress. Ends when your total damage drops to your Beaten Down threshold or below. Bleeding Persistent damage: 1 lethal damage at the end of each of your turns, until the duration runs out or the wound is treated (with First aid, Field Medicine or Avoid Death). Blinded Rolls that rely on sight use a chance die. Attacking or acting against a target you can hear, such as an adjacent enemy, takes −3d instead, as in darkness. You can't Dodge, and your other defense rolls take −2d. Deafened Rolls that rely on hearing use a chance die, and you take −1d to notice anything around you. You can't benefit from anything that needs you to hear someone, such as a shouted order or a command, unless you can see them. Determined Your next Stress cost is reduced by 1. Disoriented You can't use reactions. Exhausted −1d to all rolls. Exhausted can stack, giving −1d for each stack. W",""],["Rules","Steps","Character Creation › Steps","character-creation.html#steps","Race and heritage: gain two racial traits. Community: gain the key trait of the community you grew up in, and put 1 skill dot in one of its skills. Vocation: gain the vocation's key talent, and put 1 skill dot in one of the vocation's skills. Attributes: distribute attribute dots. Skills, specialties and a free pick: spend the rest of your skill dots, choose three specialties, then take one extra talent or community trait. Derived values and gear: work out Health, Tenacity, Stress, Defense and the rest, and pick starting equipment. At the end, a starting character has 2 racial traits, 1 community trait (the key trait), 1 talent (the vocation's key talent), three specialties, and one free pick (a talent or a community trait).",""],["Rules","Race and heritage","Character Creation › Steps","character-creation.html#steps","Race and heritage: gain two racial traits. ",""],["Rules","Community","Character Creation › Steps","character-creation.html#steps","Community: gain the key trait of the community you grew up in, and put 1 skill dot in one of its skills. ",""],["Rules","Vocation","Character Creation › Steps","character-creation.html#steps","Vocation: gain the vocation's key talent, and put 1 skill dot in one of the vocation's skills. ",""],["Rules","Attributes","Character Creation › Steps","character-creation.html#steps","Attributes: distribute attribute dots. ",""],["Rules","Skills, specialties and a free pick","Character Creation › Steps","character-creation.html#steps","Skills, specialties and a free pick: spend the rest of your skill dots, choose three specialties, then take one extra talent or community trait. ",""],["Rules","Derived values and gear","Character Creation › Steps","character-creation.html#steps","Derived values and gear: work out Health, Tenacity, Stress, Defense and the rest, and pick starting equipment. ",""],["Rules","1. Race and heritage","Character Creation › 1. Race and heritage","character-creation.html#1-race-and-heritage","Your race is what you are. Your heritage is another bloodline in you: a parent of a different race, or old ancestry that has resurfaced generations later. Each race has racial traits: a primary trait and usually a secondary trait. Choose a race and take its primary trait. Then choose another or the same race as your heritage and take its secondary trait. Some races have only a primary trait. Their blood is dominant: anyone with that bloodline is of that race. They can be chosen as a race but not as a heritage, so a character of that race always takes a different race as their heritage. Settings add their own races. The core rules include humans.",""],["Rules","Human","Character Creation › 1. Race and heritage","character-creation.html#human","Primary: Driven. When you push yourself, you gain +3d instead of +2d. Secondary: Fortune's Favor. You can raise the stakes on your own rolls twice per day instead of once.",""],["Traits","Driven","Character Creation › Human","character-creation.html#human","Primary: Driven. When you push yourself, you gain +3d instead of +2d. ",""],["Traits","Fortune's Favor","Character Creation › Human","character-creation.html#human","Secondary: Fortune's Favor. You can raise the stakes on your own rolls twice per day instead of once. ",""],["Rules","2. Community","Character Creation › 2. Community","character-creation.html#2-community","A community is a group of people you belong to: where you live, who looks out for you, and what that life teaches. At character creation, you choose the community you grew up in. Communities are defined by each setting. Each community is a small tree of community traits, read like a vocation's talent tree (see Talents): Key trait: every member of that community gains it. Further traits: one or two more, each with an after tag and other prerequisites (skills, attributes, race). At character creation, you gain your community's key trait. Its further traits come later through advancement, or you can take one with your free pick in step 5. Community skills: each community lists two or three skills. Put 1 skill dot in one of them. Key traits are about as strong as a secondary racial trait, and lean toward social and story hooks. A further trait costs the same as a talent, at creation and in advancement.",""],["Rules","Key trait","Character Creation › 2. Community","character-creation.html#2-community","Key trait: every member of that community gains it. ",""],["Rules","Further traits","Character Creation › 2. Community","character-creation.html#2-community","Further traits: one or two more, each with an after tag and other prerequisites (skills, attributes, race). ",""],["Rules","At character creation","Character Creation › 2. Community","character-creation.html#2-community","At character creation, you gain your community's key trait. Its further traits come later through advancement, or you can take one with your free pick in step 5. ",""],["Rules","Community skills","Character Creation › 2. Community","character-creation.html#2-community","Community skills: each community lists two or three skills. Put 1 skill dot in one of them. ",""],["Rules","Joining another community","Character Creation › 2. Community","character-creation.html#joining-another-community","Characters can join other communities during play, the way they can train into another vocation. Gain its key trait through advancement, at the cost of a talent. After that, its further traits are open to you like your own community's. No skill dot. The community skill dot is only for the community you grew up in. You keep your first community. Joining a new one adds to who you are; it doesn't replace it. The cost is the only requirement, but joining a community should mean something in the story. GMs are encouraged to work with the player on an in-world reason and a chance to roleplay it: a season travelling with a caravan, a debt repaid to a guild, a winter sheltered on a Frontier farmstead. A character can also start play as a member of a second community, using the free pick in step 5. Their backstory covers how they came to belong to it.",""],["Rules","Gain its key trait","Character Creation › Joining another community","character-creation.html#joining-another-community","Gain its key trait through advancement, at the cost of a talent. After that, its further traits are open to you like your own community's. ",""],["Rules","No skill dot","Character Creation › Joining another community","character-creation.html#joining-another-community","No skill dot. The community skill dot is only for the community you grew up in. ",""],["Rules","You keep your first community","Character Creation › Joining another community","character-creation.html#joining-another-community","You keep your first community. Joining a new one adds to who you are; it doesn't replace it. ",""],["Rules","3. Vocation","Character Creation › 3. Vocation","character-creation.html#3-vocation","Your vocation is what you do: your calling and your training. Vocations are in the core rules: Agent, Envoy, Hunter, Leader, Scholar and Warrior. Gain your vocation's key talent. Other talents come through advancement, or with your free pick in step 5. Vocation skills: each vocation lists three skills. Put 1 skill dot in one of them.",""],["Rules","Vocation skills","Character Creation › 3. Vocation","character-creation.html#3-vocation","Vocation skills: each vocation lists three skills. Put 1 skill dot in one of them. ",""],["Rules","4. Attributes","Character Creation › 4. Attributes","character-creation.html#4-attributes","All nine attributes start at 1. Rank the Mental, Physical and Social categories. Put 5 dots in the first, 4 in the second and 3 in the third. Cap at creation: no attribute can have more than 4 dots. A 5th dot comes only through advancement.",""],["Rules","Cap at creation","Character Creation › 4. Attributes","character-creation.html#4-attributes","Cap at creation: no attribute can have more than 4 dots. A 5th dot comes only through advancement. ",""],["Rules","Skills","Character Creation › 5. Skills, specialties and a free pick","character-creation.html#skills","You have 22 skill dots, split by category: rank Mental, Physical and Social, and put 11, 7 and 4 dots in them. The community and vocation dots from steps 2 and 3 are part of these 22. Each comes out of the pool for its skill's category, so you have 20 left to spend freely. The community and vocation dots can go in the same skill. Cap at creation: no skill can have more than 3 permanent dots. Higher ratings come through advancement. Temporary dots from talents (such as the Scholar's Erudition) don't count toward the cap, so they can take a skill above 3.",""],["Rules","Cap at creation","Character Creation › Skills","character-creation.html#skills","Cap at creation: no skill can have more than 3 permanent dots. Higher ratings come through advancement. ",""],["Rules","Temporary dots","Character Creation › Skills","character-creation.html#skills","Temporary dots from talents (such as the Scholar's Erudition) don't count toward the cap, so they can take a skill above 3. ",""],["Rules","Specialties","Character Creation › 5. Skills, specialties and a free pick","character-creation.html#specialties","Choose three specialties (see Specialties for the rules and examples): One from your community: in the skill where you put your community dot. One from your vocation: in the skill where you put your vocation dot. One free: in any skill with at least 1 permanent dot. If your community and vocation dots went in the same skill, take two different specialties in it. All three start at +1d; specialties can only be improved through advancement.",""],["Rules","One from your community","Character Creation › Specialties","character-creation.html#specialties","One from your community: in the skill where you put your community dot. ",""],["Rules","One from your vocation","Character Creation › Specialties","character-creation.html#specialties","One from your vocation: in the skill where you put your vocation dot. ",""],["Rules","One free","Character Creation › Specialties","character-creation.html#specialties","One free: in any skill with at least 1 permanent dot. ",""],["Rules","Free pick","Character Creation › 5. Skills, specialties and a free pick","character-creation.html#free-pick","Once your skills are set, take one of the following. Its prerequisites must be met. Another talent from your vocation, to go further along one of its trees. A second vocation's key talent, to start play with two vocations. A further trait of your community. A second community's key trait, to start play as a member of two communities. Your backstory explains how you came to belong to it. You don't get its skill dot. The free pick comes after attributes and skills, so a talent or trait that requires either can be taken as long as your final ratings meet it. With the skill cap at 3, talents that require a skill of 4+ can't be taken at creation.",""],["Rules","Another talent","Character Creation › Free pick","character-creation.html#free-pick","Another talent from your vocation, to go further along one of its trees. ",""],["Rules","A second vocation's key talent","Character Creation › Free pick","character-creation.html#free-pick","A second vocation's key talent, to start play with two vocations. ",""],["Rules","A further trait","Character Creation › Free pick","character-creation.html#free-pick","A further trait of your community. ",""],["Rules","A second community's key trait","Character Creation › Free pick","character-creation.html#free-pick","A second community's key trait, to start play as a member of two communities. Your backstory explains how you came to belong to it. You don't get its skill dot. ",""],["Rules","6. Derived values and gear","Character Creation › 6. Derived values and gear","character-creation.html#6-derived-values-and-gear","Value Formula Size 5 for an adult of any core race. Races or effects can change it. Health Size + Stamina Tenacity Resolve + Composure + 3 Stress Current Tenacity + 2 Defense The lower of Dexterity and Wits Beaten Down threshold Stamina + Resolve Speed Strength + Dexterity + Size + species factor, in meters Speed is how far you can move with one move action. The species factor is 0 by default; a setting can give a race or creature a different one (for example, +2 for a fast runner, −2 for a slow one). Because Size is part of it, smaller characters are slower and larger ones are faster.",""],["Rules","Starting gear","Character Creation › 6. Derived values and gear","character-creation.html#starting-gear","You start with: Your community's gear: clothes, everyday items and anything the setting ties to it (listed with each community). Your vocation's gear: weapons, armor and a kit (listed with each vocation). 2 handfuls of wealth (see Wealth). Only the first vocation and the community you grew up in give gear; a second vocation or community from the free pick doesn't. Settings can add rules about who may carry what (Duskworld: firearms).",""],["Rules","Your community's gear","Character Creation › Starting gear","character-creation.html#starting-gear","Your community's gear: clothes, everyday items and anything the setting ties to it (listed with each community). ",""],["Rules","Your vocation's gear","Character Creation › Starting gear","character-creation.html#starting-gear","Your vocation's gear: weapons, armor and a kit (listed with each vocation). ",""],["Rules","2 handfuls","Character Creation › Starting gear","character-creation.html#starting-gear","2 handfuls of wealth (see Wealth). ",""],["Rules","Wealth","Character Creation › Wealth","character-creation.html#wealth","Money is abstract: nobody counts coins. Wealth comes in three units, each worth 10 of the one below it. Unit Worth What it buys Handful — A night at an inn, a bribe to a clerk, a set of tools Bag 10 handfuls A good weapon, light or medium armor, passage with a caravan, a serious bribe Chest 10 bags Heavy armor, a horse and wagon, a favor from the powerful Each setting names the units to fit its world (Duskworld: handfuls of boxings, slabs and shells; see the Duskworld Overview). The rules always use handful, bag and chest. On your sheet: 9 handful boxes, 9 bag boxes, and a count of chests. When you'd mark a 10th handful, clear your handfuls and mark a bag instead; bags roll up into a chest the same way. Paying: clear the boxes for the price. If you don't have enough of a smaller unit, break a bigger one: clear 1 bag and mark 10 handfuls, then pay. Small purchases: anything worth less than a handful (a meal, a drink, a fare) isn't tracked. Selling: you get about half the price back, at the GM's call. Restocking: Prepare restocks consumables for free. In a settlement, each extra rest action costs 2 handfuls, so you can Prepare again to refill more kit charges (see Rest). Rewards: an ",""],["Rules","On your sheet","Character Creation › Wealth","character-creation.html#wealth","On your sheet: 9 handful boxes, 9 bag boxes, and a count of chests. When you'd mark a 10th handful, clear your handfuls and mark a bag instead; bags roll up into a chest the same way. ",""],["Rules","Paying","Character Creation › Wealth","character-creation.html#wealth","Paying: clear the boxes for the price. If you don't have enough of a smaller unit, break a bigger one: clear 1 bag and mark 10 handfuls, then pay. ",""],["Rules","Small purchases","Character Creation › Wealth","character-creation.html#wealth","Small purchases: anything worth less than a handful (a meal, a drink, a fare) isn't tracked. ",""],["Rules","Selling","Character Creation › Wealth","character-creation.html#wealth","Selling: you get about half the price back, at the GM's call. ",""],["Rules","Restocking","Character Creation › Wealth","character-creation.html#wealth","Restocking: Prepare restocks consumables for free. In a settlement, each extra rest action costs 2 handfuls, so you can Prepare again to refill more kit charges (see Rest). ",""],["Rules","Rewards","Character Creation › Wealth","character-creation.html#wealth","Rewards: an ordinary job pays a handful or a few; a big job pays a bag. Chests are story rewards, not wages. ",""],["Talents","Opportunist","Agent › Key talent","vocations/agent.html#key-talent","Once per round, reroll your stakes die.",""],["Rules","Agent","Vocations","vocations/agent.html","Agents live by their wits and their luck: investigators, spies and thieves who turn risk into opportunity. Their key talent lets them reroll the stakes die, so they thrive when the stakes are high.",""],["Talents","Rousing Presence","Envoy › Key talent","vocations/envoy.html#key-talent","1 action · Take 1 Stress. An ally near you becomes Inspired for one hour.",""],["Rules","Envoy","Vocations","vocations/envoy.html","Envoys inspire, steady and protect the people around them, and turn words into weapons against their enemies. Their key talent makes allies Inspired, so the whole party fails less often.",""],["Talents","Seek Quarry","Hunter › Key talent","vocations/hunter.html#key-talent","1 action · Choose a character you can see, or whose trail or traces you've found, as your quarry. You get +1d on rolls to find, attack or study them. They stay your quarry until you choose another.",""],["Rules","Hunter","Vocations","vocations/hunter.html","Hunters track, study and bring down a chosen quarry. Patient and precise, they're as comfortable in the wild as in a crowded street, as long as they know who they're after.",""],["Talents","Decisive Command","Leader › Key talent","vocations/leader.html#key-talent","1 action · Take 1 Stress. An ally close to you adds your command dice to their next roll.",""],["Rules","Leader","Vocations","vocations/leader.html","Leaders make everyone around them better. Their command dice add to their allies' rolls, and their talents give the party extra actions, protection and direction.",""],["Talents","Erudition","Scholar › Key talent","vocations/scholar.html#key-talent","Gain a temporary specialty and +1 dot in two Mental skills. Reassign them at the end of a rest with access to research (a library, archives, the internet).",""],["Rules","Scholar","Vocations","vocations/scholar.html","Scholars treat knowledge as a tool. Their key talent, Erudition, gives them expertise they can reassign after a rest with access to research, so they can prepare for whatever comes next.",""],["Talents","Vigilant Stance","Warrior › Key talent","vocations/warrior.html#key-talent","1 action · Enter Vigilant Stance. While in it, your first extra reaction each round costs 1 Stress instead of 2 when used to Parry or make a Reactive Strike, and you can enter other stances as a free action.",""],["Rules","Warrior","Vocations","vocations/warrior.html","Masters of weapons and fighting stances, warriors are at their best in the thick of melee. They hold the line, trade blows with the toughest enemies, and make it costly for anyone to get past them.",""],["Talents","Orange Blood","Athrenite › Key talent","vocations/athrenite.html#key-talent","1 action · Gain the Athren power skill and an Ichor pool, and choose your Form: Guardian, Harbinger, Listener, Mawbound, Shardbound or Veilwalker. Your Form never changes. You can take talents only from your Form's tree and from Vessel. Surge: once per round, as a free action, spend 1 Ichor to add +1d to a Physical roll.","duskworld"],["Rules","Athrenite","Vocations","vocations/athrenite.html","Athrenites are humans who carry a power that isn't theirs, and know it by their orange blood. SPEAR infuses its chosen with it in sealed laboratories, so that the power that once served the Allmother's murderers now serves Mendris. Some say it came from the First Followers' blasphemy; some say the Allmother allows it, others that she curses it. No one knows, and few agree. The power is called Athren, and each athrenite belongs to one Form of it. The power is fast and strong, and it's always hungry: use it hard enough and it consumes you, and you become a Transcendent.","duskworld"],["Rules","Athren","Athrenite › Athren","vocations/athrenite.html#athren","Power skill: Athren. Each power names the attribute it's rolled with. Ichor: the pool is 2 + the higher of Stamina or Resolve + twice your Athren dots, so it grows with your power. Running dry is rarely what stops an athrenite; the Athren Stage is. It refills at the end of a rest and at the end of a week of downtime. When it's empty, you can pay with 2 Stress per point, like any power pool. Fast: evoking any power takes 1 action, whatever it costs, unless it says it's a reaction. Paid in full: you pay a power's cost whether or not the roll succeeds. Athren doesn't give back what you spend. Duration: a power lasts one minute per success unless it says otherwise. You can sustain it for 1 Ichor per extra unit; its initial cost stays reserved while it lasts. Attacks: an attack a power gives you (a bite, claws, a crystal weapon, Deathtouch) works like a weapon: its listed damage is the weapon's damage, added to your net successes as usual (see Attacks). Overcharge: before rolling, you can spend extra Ichor on a power: +1d per extra point. Your threshold is your Athren dots. Spending more Ichor than that on a single power, Overcharge included, risks your Athren Stage.","duskworld"],["Rules","Power skill","Athrenite › Athren","vocations/athrenite.html#athren","Power skill: Athren. Each power names the attribute it's rolled with. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Ichor","Athrenite › Athren","vocations/athrenite.html#athren","Ichor: the pool is 2 + the higher of Stamina or Resolve + twice your Athren dots, so it grows with your power. Running dry is rarely what stops an athrenite; the Athren Stage is. It refills at the end of a rest and at the end of a week of downtime. When it's empty, you can pay with 2 Stress per point, like any power pool. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Fast","Athrenite › Athren","vocations/athrenite.html#athren","Fast: evoking any power takes 1 action, whatever it costs, unless it says it's a reaction. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Paid in full","Athrenite › Athren","vocations/athrenite.html#athren","Paid in full: you pay a power's cost whether or not the roll succeeds. Athren doesn't give back what you spend. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Duration","Athrenite › Athren","vocations/athrenite.html#athren","Duration: a power lasts one minute per success unless it says otherwise. You can sustain it for 1 Ichor per extra unit; its initial cost stays reserved while it lasts. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Attacks","Athrenite › Athren","vocations/athrenite.html#athren","Attacks: an attack a power gives you (a bite, claws, a crystal weapon, Deathtouch) works like a weapon: its listed damage is the weapon's damage, added to your net successes as usual (see Attacks). ","duskworld"],["Rules","Overcharge","Athrenite › Athren","vocations/athrenite.html#athren","Overcharge: before rolling, you can spend extra Ichor on a power: +1d per extra point. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Your threshold","Athrenite › Athren","vocations/athrenite.html#athren","Your threshold is your Athren dots. Spending more Ichor than that on a single power, Overcharge included, risks your Athren Stage. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Born and made","Athrenite › Athren","vocations/athrenite.html#born-and-made","Most athrenites are made: infused in SPEAR's laboratories, registered, and bound to SPEAR's service for as long as they're useful. Some are born, carrying orange blood from birth and awakening to their power later, often violently. SPEAR denies they exist. Officially, an athrenite SPEAR didn't make is unregistered, and so illegal; in practice, SPEAR finds them and either recruits them or makes them disappear. Both use the same rules. Choose at character creation: Made: you have a SPEAR record, a handler, and a debt the Houses won't let you forget. Your papers say what you are. Born: you have a secret. Orange blood is hard to hide when you bleed, and your power will draw attention sooner or later. Mawbound are the exception: a made athrenite who turns out Mawbound is a failure, and SPEAR destroys its failures early. Almost every Mawbound is born, or a fugitive who escaped the laboratories (see Mawbound).","duskworld"],["Rules","Made","Athrenite › Born and made","vocations/athrenite.html#born-and-made","Made: you have a SPEAR record, a handler, and a debt the Houses won't let you forget. Your papers say what you are. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Born","Athrenite › Born and made","vocations/athrenite.html#born-and-made","Born: you have a secret. Orange blood is hard to hide when you bleed, and your power will draw attention sooner or later. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Athren Stage","Athrenite › Athren","vocations/athrenite.html#athren-stage","The Athren Stage is a Scar written with its stage, from 0 to 4, such as Athren Stage 2. It goes down only through the ways in Settling the blood, and never from stage 4. Risking the stage: when an Athren roll shows a Complication, or when you spend more Ichor on one power than your threshold, roll Resolve + Composure. You need successes equal to the stage you'd reach: 1 to reach stage 1, up to 4 to reach stage 4. On a failure, your Athren Stage rises by 1. Stories can raise it too: a failed infusion, a SPEAR \"treatment\", drinking something you shouldn't. Stage Effect 1 Surging: once per hour, evoke a power for 1 Ichor less (minimum 1). 2 Evolved: every power you evoke uses its Evolved effect, and costs 1 more Ichor. 3 Your Form's struggle turns: your Form's struggle gains its stage 3 effect (see each Form). 4 Transcendence: you become your Form's Transcendent. The character leaves play and becomes the GM's.","duskworld"],["Rules","Risking the stage","Athrenite › Athren Stage","vocations/athrenite.html#athren-stage","Risking the stage: when an Athren roll shows a Complication, or when you spend more Ichor on one power than your threshold, roll Resolve + Composure. You need successes equal to the stage you'd reach: 1 to reach stage 1, up to 4 to reach stage 4. On a failure, your Athren Stage rises by 1. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Stories can raise it too","Athrenite › Athren Stage","vocations/athrenite.html#athren-stage","Stories can raise it too: a failed infusion, a SPEAR \"treatment\", drinking something you shouldn't. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Settling the blood","Athrenite › Athren","vocations/athrenite.html#settling-the-blood","The power can be pushed back down, but only slowly, and never for free. Only in downtime: your Athren Stage can drop by at most 1 per week of downtime, whatever ways you use. Nothing brings anyone back from stage 4. Stillblood: SPEAR's elixir, brewed in its laboratories. Drink a dose at the start of a week of downtime: at the end of the week, your Athren Stage drops by 1. That week your Ichor doesn't refill and you can't evoke powers. Made athrenites get doses from their handlers, as reward for good service or withheld as leverage. Anyone else needs a favor with SPEAR or House Callarain, or the black market. Black-market stillblood: stolen or diluted doses, 2 slabs each, found with a Manipulation + Streetwise roll. Roll a die when you drink one: on a 1, it was cut with something else and does nothing. Bloodletting: the oldest trick of the born. A helper drains the orange blood from you, as a downtime action: they roll Intelligence + Medicine, needing successes equal to your Athren Stage. You take lethal damage equal to your Athren Stage, which heals normally. On a success, your Athren Stage drops by 1 at the end of the week. On a Complication, someone notices the orange blood, or k","duskworld"],["Rules","Only in downtime","Athrenite › Settling the blood","vocations/athrenite.html#settling-the-blood","Only in downtime: your Athren Stage can drop by at most 1 per week of downtime, whatever ways you use. Nothing brings anyone back from stage 4. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Stillblood","Athrenite › Settling the blood","vocations/athrenite.html#settling-the-blood","Stillblood: SPEAR's elixir, brewed in its laboratories. Drink a dose at the start of a week of downtime: at the end of the week, your Athren Stage drops by 1. That week your Ichor doesn't refill and you can't evoke powers. Made athrenites get doses from their handlers, as reward for good service or withheld as leverage. Anyone else needs a favor with SPEAR or House Callarain, or the black market. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Black-market stillblood","Athrenite › Settling the blood","vocations/athrenite.html#settling-the-blood","Black-market stillblood: stolen or diluted doses, 2 slabs each, found with a Manipulation + Streetwise roll. Roll a die when you drink one: on a 1, it was cut with something else and does nothing. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Bloodletting","Athrenite › Settling the blood","vocations/athrenite.html#settling-the-blood","Bloodletting: the oldest trick of the born. A helper drains the orange blood from you, as a downtime action: they roll Intelligence + Medicine, needing successes equal to your Athren Stage. You take lethal damage equal to your Athren Stage, which heals normally. On a success, your Athren Stage drops by 1 at the end of the week. On a Complication, someone notices the orange blood, or keeps it. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Burning off","Athrenite › Settling the blood","vocations/athrenite.html#settling-the-blood","Burning off: go somewhere no one will see and empty the power, again and again, for a week. It takes the whole week, and you can take no other downtime actions. At the end of it, roll Resolve + Composure, needing successes equal to your Athren Stage + 1. On a success, it drops by 1. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Struggles","Athrenite › Athren","vocations/athrenite.html#struggles","Each Form carries a struggle, a drawback that never goes away. At Athren Stage 3, it turns into something you can use. Form Struggle At stage 3 Guardian Hollow: the power hardens the body and hollows the heart. You can't push yourself on Empathy, Expression or Persuasion rolls. You can add your Athren dots to any roll to resist a mental effect, fear or compulsion. Harbinger Spirit Anchor: not all the dead are willing to depart, and you're an easy anchor for the restless. −2d to resist possession and the influence of spirits. Attacks by the undead and spirits deal half damage to you, rounded down, and whenever you resist a possession, the possessor takes 1 aggravated damage. Listener Overwhelming Voices: the more minds around you, the louder the Speaker's whispers. Near a crowd of more people than twice your Athren dots, −1d to Mental rolls and Athren rolls. In a crowd, you can let the voices in: take −1d to every roll except Athren rolls, and your Athren rolls get +2d instead of −1d. Mawbound Unmoored Form: when you're hurt, your body starts to twist on its own. While you're Beaten Down, Bleeding or Exhausted, −2d to Social rolls. The wrongness becomes a weapon: in those states, yo","duskworld"],["Rules","Transcendents","Athrenite › Athren","vocations/athrenite.html#transcendents","What an athrenite becomes when the power finally takes them. Everyone in Mendris has seen at least one: the Vakens, at least, are hired out by the rich. Form Transcendent Guardian Vaken: a body near indestructible, like bark, and a mind gone numb. Vakens belong to the Allmother and are lent or rented out as guards, police and beasts of burden. They act only on orders, and only those trained to reach them, the puppeteers, can give them reliably. Harbinger Unpassed: they reached the threshold of death and couldn't cross. Mindless undead, stuck between life and death. Listener The Speaker: their mind drowns in the torrent of other Listeners' thoughts, and they join the great hive mind known as the Speaker. Mawbound Mawkin: the body keeps mutating beyond recognition until nothing of the mind is left. Shardbound Shardsoul: the body becomes a crystal weapon of their choosing: a powerful, sentient weapon that can be wielded but never again walks. Veilwalker Lost: the Otherside takes what it's owed. They lose their way beyond the Veil, or something finds them there. Either way, they never come back.","duskworld"],["Talents","Dreamer's Blood","Dreamshaper › Key talent","vocations/dreamshaper.html#key-talent","1 action · Gain the Dreamshaping power skill and a Reverie pool. Waking Dream: spend 1 Reverie to create a brief image or sound no bigger than you, or a small object with no bulk and no moving parts that's plainly unreal to the touch. It lasts until the end of your next turn. Waking Dream is a nudge, not a full dreamcraft: you don't roll.","duskworld"],["Rules","Dreamshaper","Vocations","vocations/dreamshaper.html","Some kehars carry the blood of the First Dreamers and can reach into the Everdream, the dream that dreamed the world, to make their own dreams real. Their gift is Dreamshaping, and the effects they create are called dreamcrafts.","duskworld"],["Rules","Dreamcrafts","Dreamshaper › Dreamcrafts","vocations/dreamshaper.html#dreamcrafts","Power skill: Dreamshaping. Each dreamcraft names the attribute it's rolled with. Reverie: the pool is 2 + the higher of Wits or Presence. It refills at the end of a rest only if you slept, and at the end of a week of downtime. Daydreaming: an hour of quiet rest (no travel, fighting or hard work) lets you roll Composure and regain 1 Reverie per success, at least 1. When it's empty, you can pay with 2 Stress per point, like any power pool. Reverie paid for a dreamcraft that's still active stays reserved until it ends. Always a gamble: every dreamcraft roll raises the stakes, and the stakes die replaces one die of your pool: the dream is never fully yours to control. If your pool is down to a chance die, roll the chance die alone.","duskworld"],["Rules","Power skill","Dreamshaper › Dreamcrafts","vocations/dreamshaper.html#dreamcrafts","Power skill: Dreamshaping. Each dreamcraft names the attribute it's rolled with. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Reverie","Dreamshaper › Dreamcrafts","vocations/dreamshaper.html#dreamcrafts","Reverie: the pool is 2 + the higher of Wits or Presence. It refills at the end of a rest only if you slept, and at the end of a week of downtime. Daydreaming: an hour of quiet rest (no travel, fighting or hard work) lets you roll Composure and regain 1 Reverie per success, at least 1. When it's empty, you can pay with 2 Stress per point, like any power pool. Reverie paid for a dreamcraft that's still active stays reserved until it ends. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Always a gamble","Dreamshaper › Dreamcrafts","vocations/dreamshaper.html#dreamcrafts","Always a gamble: every dreamcraft roll raises the stakes, and the stakes die replaces one die of your pool: the dream is never fully yours to control. If your pool is down to a chance die, roll the chance die alone. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Control","Dreamshaper › Dreamcrafts","vocations/dreamshaper.html#control","The roll decides what the dreamcraft costs, not just whether it works. Result Effect Reverie cost Success with Opportunity Works Half, rounded up Success Works Full Failure with Opportunity Doesn't work None: the Everdream lets you go cleanly Failure Doesn't work 1 Failure with Complication Doesn't work Half, rounded up, and a dream phenomenon breaks loose A Complication on a successful dreamcraft is an ordinary setback, chosen by the GM.","duskworld"],["Rules","Materializing","Dreamshaper › Dreamcrafts","vocations/dreamshaper.html#materializing","A dreamcraft can be cast in the moment or materialized slowly. The time you take sets the unit it's measured in: it lasts one unit per success. Way Time Unit Or instead In the moment The actions it lists Rounds — Meditation 1 minute Minutes +1d, lasting rounds Deep meditation 1 hour Hours +2d, lasting rounds Dreamed A night's sleep Days +3d, lasting rounds Never quick: casting a dreamcraft in the moment takes at least 2 actions. Shaping a dream is slower than swinging a sword. Longer or stronger: when you materialize slowly, choose either the longer unit or the bonus dice. A dreamcraft with an instant effect always takes the bonus. Sustaining: when a dreamcraft would end, you can pay 1 Reverie to keep it for one more unit, as often as you like. That Reverie is spent as usual; only the dreamcraft's initial cost stays reserved. Dreamed dreamcrafts are paid when you wake, from the Reverie the rest just refilled, and stay reserved for as long as they last: a Dreamveil kept over a hideout for days costs its dreamshaper that much Reverie every day. Some dreamcrafts can only be dreamed, or only be dreamed or deep-meditated.","duskworld"],["Rules","Never quick","Dreamshaper › Materializing","vocations/dreamshaper.html#materializing","Never quick: casting a dreamcraft in the moment takes at least 2 actions. Shaping a dream is slower than swinging a sword. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Longer or stronger","Dreamshaper › Materializing","vocations/dreamshaper.html#materializing","Longer or stronger: when you materialize slowly, choose either the longer unit or the bonus dice. A dreamcraft with an instant effect always takes the bonus. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Sustaining","Dreamshaper › Materializing","vocations/dreamshaper.html#materializing","Sustaining: when a dreamcraft would end, you can pay 1 Reverie to keep it for one more unit, as often as you like. That Reverie is spent as usual; only the dreamcraft's initial cost stays reserved. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Dreamed dreamcrafts","Dreamshaper › Materializing","vocations/dreamshaper.html#materializing","Dreamed dreamcrafts are paid when you wake, from the Reverie the rest just refilled, and stay reserved for as long as they last: a Dreamveil kept over a hideout for days costs its dreamshaper that much Reverie every day. Some dreamcrafts can only be dreamed, or only be dreamed or deep-meditated. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Phenomena, nightmares and nexuses","Dreamshaper › Dreamcrafts","vocations/dreamshaper.html#phenomena-nightmares-and-nexuses","Dream phenomena: odd breezes, strange noises, objects moving on their own, a sudden chill, runetech flickering out for a few minutes, small things from the Everdream appearing or vanishing. The GM chooses, scaled to the dreamcraft. Nightmares: in Heavy or Deep Dusk exposure, any Complication on a dreamcraft roll turns it into a nightmare, even on a success. The GM twists the effect against you or your allies. Dream Nexuses: some places touch the Everdream. A minor nexus gives +1d to dreamcraft rolls, a major one +2d. At a nexus, any Complication also releases a phenomenon.","duskworld"],["Rules","Dream phenomena","Dreamshaper › Phenomena, nightmares and nexuses","vocations/dreamshaper.html#phenomena-nightmares-and-nexuses","Dream phenomena: odd breezes, strange noises, objects moving on their own, a sudden chill, runetech flickering out for a few minutes, small things from the Everdream appearing or vanishing. The GM chooses, scaled to the dreamcraft. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Nightmares","Dreamshaper › Phenomena, nightmares and nexuses","vocations/dreamshaper.html#phenomena-nightmares-and-nexuses","Nightmares: in Heavy or Deep Dusk exposure, any Complication on a dreamcraft roll turns it into a nightmare, even on a success. The GM twists the effect against you or your allies. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Dream Nexuses","Dreamshaper › Phenomena, nightmares and nexuses","vocations/dreamshaper.html#phenomena-nightmares-and-nexuses","Dream Nexuses: some places touch the Everdream. A minor nexus gives +1d to dreamcraft rolls, a major one +2d. At a nexus, any Complication also releases a phenomenon. ","duskworld"],["Talents","Forefather's Blessing","Shadowcaller › Key talent","vocations/shadowcaller.html#key-talent","1 action · Gain the Shadowcalling power skill and an Umbra pool, and choose your first bloodline. Fangs: you can grow fangs at will; your bite is an unarmed attack dealing 1 lethal damage. When you bite a creature that you're grappling, or one that's Unconscious or willing, you can spend 1 action to drain its blood: it gains 1 stack of Exhausted, and you either regain Umbra equal to its Stamina (an NPC's rating) or heal 1 lethal damage (or 2 bashing).","duskworld"],["Rules","Shadowcaller","Vocations","vocations/shadowcaller.html","Shanats are the children of Rakshan, the Shadow Hunter, and some carry enough of his blood to call on his gifts: the powers of a hunter of the night. Their gift is Shadowcalling, and its powers are called blessings. The stronger the blessing, the louder the forefather whispers.","duskworld"],["Rules","Shadowcalling","Shadowcaller › Shadowcalling","vocations/shadowcaller.html#shadowcalling","Power skill: Shadowcalling. Each blessing names the attribute it's rolled with. Umbra: the pool is 2 + the higher of Resolve or Presence. A rest refills only half of it, rounded up; a week of downtime refills it fully. Blood is the real refill: draining with your Fangs, or drinking a redvial. When it's empty, you can pay with 2 Stress per point, like any power pool. Calling a blessing takes actions equal to its Umbra cost. Duration: a blessing lasts one minute per success unless it says otherwise. You can sustain it for 1 Umbra per extra unit; its initial cost stays reserved while it lasts.","duskworld"],["Rules","Power skill","Shadowcaller › Shadowcalling","vocations/shadowcaller.html#shadowcalling","Power skill: Shadowcalling. Each blessing names the attribute it's rolled with. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Umbra","Shadowcaller › Shadowcalling","vocations/shadowcaller.html#shadowcalling","Umbra: the pool is 2 + the higher of Resolve or Presence. A rest refills only half of it, rounded up; a week of downtime refills it fully. Blood is the real refill: draining with your Fangs, or drinking a redvial. When it's empty, you can pay with 2 Stress per point, like any power pool. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Calling a blessing","Shadowcaller › Shadowcalling","vocations/shadowcaller.html#shadowcalling","Calling a blessing takes actions equal to its Umbra cost. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Duration","Shadowcaller › Shadowcalling","vocations/shadowcaller.html#shadowcalling","Duration: a blessing lasts one minute per success unless it says otherwise. You can sustain it for 1 Umbra per extra unit; its initial cost stays reserved while it lasts. ","duskworld"],["Rules","The forefather's price","Shadowcaller › Shadowcalling","vocations/shadowcaller.html#the-forefathers-price","The roll decides what the blessing costs. Result Effect Umbra cost Success with Opportunity Works Half, rounded up Success Works Full Failure Doesn't work 1 Failure with Complication Doesn't work 1, and Rakshan's whispers press in: take 1 Stress A Complication on a successful blessing is an ordinary setback, chosen by the GM. Light: shadowcalling is hardest in the light. Blessing rolls take −3d in bright light (direct daylight, a Beacon's glare) and −1d in ordinary light (indoors by day, a cloudy sky). Dim light and darkness carry no penalty. Forefather's Curse: you count as a kren for any effect aimed at krens. It's the shadowcallers' curse, not every shanat's: Rakshan's blood answers only in those who call on it. Forefather's Whispers: when you resist an effect aimed at krens, take −1d per two Shadowcalling dots, rounded up.","duskworld"],["Rules","Light","Shadowcaller › The forefather's price","vocations/shadowcaller.html#the-forefathers-price","Light: shadowcalling is hardest in the light. Blessing rolls take −3d in bright light (direct daylight, a Beacon's glare) and −1d in ordinary light (indoors by day, a cloudy sky). Dim light and darkness carry no penalty. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Forefather's Curse","Shadowcaller › The forefather's price","vocations/shadowcaller.html#the-forefathers-price","Forefather's Curse: you count as a kren for any effect aimed at krens. It's the shadowcallers' curse, not every shanat's: Rakshan's blood answers only in those who call on it. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Forefather's Whispers","Shadowcaller › The forefather's price","vocations/shadowcaller.html#the-forefathers-price","Forefather's Whispers: when you resist an effect aimed at krens, take −1d per two Shadowcalling dots, rounded up. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Bloodlines","Shadowcaller › Shadowcalling","vocations/shadowcaller.html#bloodlines","Each bloodline is a talent tree. The bloodlines were separate once, in the First Children, but the years have muddled them, and few shanats keep to one. Your first bloodline comes with the key talent. Its blessings are ordinary talents. Opening another bloodline means taking its first blessing. It costs a talent and either the blood price (spend one blood price) or the bloodless way (also spend one choice from the advancement pool). After that, its blessings are ordinary talents too. The blood price: you gain one when you kill a krenblooded creature by draining it with your Fangs and give up the drain's benefit, or when you drink a kren redvial. Redvials: sealed vials of blood, the civilized way for a shanat to drink. House Shantari makes them, and they're a taboo: never sold openly, passed from shanat to shanat, and of no use to anyone but a shanat. Drinking one takes 1 action: regain Umbra by the blood it holds (1 for an animal's, 2 for most people's, 3 for a strong or rich source), or heal 1 lethal damage (or 2 bashing) instead. A kren redvial holds a krenblooded creature's dying blood instead: rare and illegal, and drinking it pays one blood price rather than giving the drain's","duskworld"],["Rules","Your first bloodline","Shadowcaller › Bloodlines","vocations/shadowcaller.html#bloodlines","Your first bloodline comes with the key talent. Its blessings are ordinary talents. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Opening another bloodline","Shadowcaller › Bloodlines","vocations/shadowcaller.html#bloodlines","Opening another bloodline means taking its first blessing. It costs a talent and either the blood price (spend one blood price) or the bloodless way (also spend one choice from the advancement pool). After that, its blessings are ordinary talents too. ","duskworld"],["Rules","The blood price","Shadowcaller › Bloodlines","vocations/shadowcaller.html#bloodlines","The blood price: you gain one when you kill a krenblooded creature by draining it with your Fangs and give up the drain's benefit, or when you drink a kren redvial. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Redvials","Shadowcaller › Bloodlines","vocations/shadowcaller.html#bloodlines","Redvials: sealed vials of blood, the civilized way for a shanat to drink. House Shantari makes them, and they're a taboo: never sold openly, passed from shanat to shanat, and of no use to anyone but a shanat. Drinking one takes 1 action: regain Umbra by the blood it holds (1 for an animal's, 2 for most people's, 3 for a strong or rich source), or heal 1 lethal damage (or 2 bashing) instead. A kren redvial holds a krenblooded creature's dying blood instead: rare and illegal, and drinking it pays one blood price rather than giving the drain's benefit. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Pure or mixed","Shadowcaller › Bloodlines","vocations/shadowcaller.html#bloodlines","Pure or mixed: a shadowcaller who keeps to one bloodline reaches its strongest blessings sooner; one who mixes them has more tools. Both are true to Rakshan's blood. ","duskworld"],["Talents","Watchful Eye","Agent › Investigator","vocations/agent.html#investigator-watchful-eye","reaction · Investigation 2+ · Use Opportunist on the stakes die of a willing ally close to you. This counts as your use of Opportunist this round.",""],["Talents","Get 'Em Talking","Agent › Investigator","vocations/agent.html#investigator-get-em-talking","2 actions · Empathy 3+ · Take 1 Stress and roll Wits + Investigation, contested by Wits + Composure, to learn the target's motivation. For the next hour, you can raise the stakes on rolls that leverage it.",""],["Talents","Quick Analysis","Agent › Investigator","vocations/agent.html#investigator-quick-analysis","free action · Investigation 3+ · Once per round, take 2 Stress to gain 2 extra actions this turn, usable only for Mental rolls to use a skill, take a set-up action or use an Agent talent that isn't an attack. These can repeat actions you've already taken, but set-ups against the same target don't stack.",""],["Talents","Baleful","Agent › Investigator","vocations/agent.html#investigator-baleful","passive · When you win a contested Mental or Social roll against a target, they also take 1 Stress.",""],["Talents","Gather Evidence","Agent › Investigator","vocations/agent.html#investigator-gather-evidence","Empathy 3+ · Gain an Academics (Law) specialty. Once per round, when you succeed on a Mental roll against a target, you become Determined.",""],["Talents","Hardy","Agent › Investigator","vocations/agent.html#investigator-hardy","passive · +2 Health.",""],["Talents","Sleuth's Instincts","Agent › Investigator","vocations/agent.html#investigator-sleuths-instincts","Investigation 4+ · +1d on Mental rolls against characters whose motivation you know. You know when those characters lie to you.",""],["Talents","Close the Case","Agent › Investigator","vocations/agent.html#investigator-close-the-case","3 actions · Investigation 4+ · Take 3 Stress and roll Intelligence + Investigation, contested by Intelligence + Resolve, with +1d if you know the target's motivation. On a success, they must answer one question truthfully or concede the point publicly, their choice, and they take −2d on rolls against you for one hour. On a failure, they get +1d on their next roll against you.",""],["Talents","Sure Outcome","Agent › Spy","vocations/agent.html#spy-sure-outcome","Empathy 3+ · When you use Opportunist, take 2 Stress to turn an Opportunity into a Complication that counts as 2 successes instead of 1, or turn any Complication into an Opportunity.",""],["Talents","Plausible Excuse","Agent › Spy","vocations/agent.html#spy-plausible-excuse","reaction · Subterfuge 2+ · Gain a Thievery (Sleight of Hand) specialty. When you're caught skulking, take 2 Stress to feign innocence.",""],["Talents","Collected","Agent › Spy","vocations/agent.html#spy-collected","passive · +1d to resist Mental and Social rolls.",""],["Talents","Cover Story","Agent › Spy","vocations/agent.html#spy-cover-story","passive · Gain a false identity and a Socialize specialty in its culture or group.",""],["Talents","Subtle Takedown","Agent › Spy","vocations/agent.html#spy-subtle-takedown","2 actions · Empathy 4+ · Make an unarmed attack with Dexterity + Empathy against an unsuspecting target, raising the stakes. On a hit, you and the target are Grappled (see Grappling), and while you hold them, they can't speak or call out.",""],["Talents","Mighty","Agent › Spy","vocations/agent.html#spy-mighty","passive · When you hit with a weapon or unarmed attack, deal +1 damage per action spent on the attack.",""],["Talents","Mercurial Facade","Agent › Spy","vocations/agent.html#spy-mercurial-facade","Subterfuge 4+ · Disguise yourself with Subterfuge, without physical supplies. The first character to see through the disguise is Surprised.",""],["Talents","High Society Contacts","Agent › Spy","vocations/agent.html#spy-high-society-contacts","patron in high society · Gain a Socialize (High Society) specialty. Take 2 Stress to gain an Opportunity on a roll to interact in high society.",""],["Talents","Risky Behavior","Agent › Thief","vocations/agent.html#thief-risky-behavior","Empathy 3+ · Take 1 Stress to raise the stakes on your roll.",""],["Talents","Cheap Shot","Agent › Thief","vocations/agent.html#thief-cheap-shot","1 action · Take 1 Stress and make an unarmed attack with Dexterity + Thievery, raising the stakes. On a hit, the target is Disoriented until the end of their next turn; on an Opportunity, they're Stunned instead.",""],["Talents","Double Down","Agent › Thief","vocations/agent.html#thief-double-down","You can use Opportunist a second time on the same roll. This is an exception to the one-reroll rule. If that reroll shows a Complication, take 2 Stress.",""],["Talents","Surefooted","Agent › Thief","vocations/agent.html#thief-surefooted","passive · +3 Speed. Reduce damage from falls and dangerous terrain by your Athletics dots.",""],["Talents","Underworld Contacts","Agent › Thief","vocations/agent.html#thief-underworld-contacts","patron in the criminal underworld · Gain a Streetwise (Criminal Groups) specialty. Take 2 Stress to gain an Opportunity on a Social roll against criminals.",""],["Talents","Shadow Step","Agent › Thief","vocations/agent.html#thief-shadow-step","free action · Thievery 4+ · After you disengage, take 2 Stress and roll Dexterity + Thievery against each enemy's Wits + Composure to hide from them. +1d if you're in cover or obscured.",""],["Talents","Fast Talker","Agent › Thief","vocations/agent.html#thief-fast-talker","free action · Empathy 4+ · Once per round, take 2 Stress to gain 2 extra actions this turn, usable only for Social rolls to use a skill, take a set-up action or use an Agent talent that isn't an attack. These can repeat actions you've already taken, but set-ups against the same target don't stack.",""],["Talents","Trickster's Hand","Agent › Thief","vocations/agent.html#thief-tricksters-hand","free action · Thievery 4+ · Once per round, take 2 Stress to gain 2 extra actions this turn, usable only for Physical rolls to use a skill, take a set-up action or use an Agent talent that isn't an attack. These can repeat actions you've already taken, but set-ups against the same target don't stack.",""],["Talents","Steadfast Challenge","Envoy › Diplomat","vocations/envoy.html#diplomat-steadfast-challenge","1 action · Resolve 2+ · Take 1 Stress and roll Resolve + Intimidation, contested by Wits + Composure. On a success, the enemy is Disoriented until the end of their next turn, and has −1d on rolls against you for a number of rounds equal to your successes.",""],["Talents","Collected","Envoy › Diplomat","vocations/envoy.html#diplomat-collected","passive · +1d to resist Mental and Social rolls.",""],["Talents","Withering Retort","Envoy › Diplomat","vocations/envoy.html#diplomat-withering-retort","Resolve 3+ · When you react to an attack, you can use Steadfast Challenge as a free action. If you succeed, you get +2d on your reaction, but the attacker doesn't suffer Steadfast Challenge's other effects.",""],["Talents","Well Dressed","Envoy › Diplomat","vocations/envoy.html#diplomat-well-dressed","passive · Gain a Socialize (Fashion) specialty. While dressed for the occasion, +1d on your first Subterfuge, Expression or Persuasion roll each hour.",""],["Talents","Calm Appeal","Envoy › Diplomat","vocations/envoy.html#diplomat-calm-appeal","Resolve 3+ · When Steadfast Challenge leaves a target Disoriented, take 1 Stress to calm them: for as long as the challenge's −1d lasts, they also take −1d on all attacks and hostile actions, not only against you. A target who wins the contest against your Steadfast Challenge still takes 1 Stress.",""],["Talents","High Society Contacts","Envoy › Diplomat","vocations/envoy.html#diplomat-high-society-contacts","patron in high society · Gain a Socialize (High Society) specialty. Take 2 Stress to gain an Opportunity on a roll to interact in high society.",""],["Talents","Peaceful Solution","Envoy › Diplomat","vocations/envoy.html#diplomat-peaceful-solution","passive · Resolve 4+ · Attacks made by enemies you've calmed against allies close to you take a further −1d.",""],["Talents","Practiced Oratory","Envoy › Diplomat","vocations/envoy.html#diplomat-practiced-oratory","Persuasion 4+ · When you use Rousing Presence or Steadfast Challenge, take Stress up to your Persuasion dots. Each point adds one more target. For Steadfast Challenge, make one roll; each target contests it separately.",""],["Talents","Customary Garb","Envoy › Faithful","vocations/envoy.html#faithful-customary-garb","passive · While dressed appropriately, +1d to Social rolls and to resist Social rolls.",""],["Talents","Galvanize","Envoy › Faithful","vocations/envoy.html#faithful-galvanize","2 actions · An ally close to you makes a recovery roll. Each ally can benefit from Galvanize once per day.",""],["Talents","Devoted Presence","Envoy › Faithful","vocations/envoy.html#faithful-devoted-presence","Academics 2+ · When you use Rousing Presence, take 1 Stress to remove Prone, Slowed, Stunned and Surprised from the target.",""],["Talents","Composed","Envoy › Faithful","vocations/envoy.html#faithful-composed","passive · +2 maximum Stress.",""],["Talents","Stalwart Presence","Envoy › Faithful","vocations/envoy.html#faithful-stalwart-presence","Resolve 3+ · When you use Rousing Presence, take 1 Stress to give the target +1d to defense rolls, or +1d to resist, for one hour.",""],["Talents","Applied Motivation","Envoy › Faithful","vocations/envoy.html#faithful-applied-motivation","passive · Academics 3+ · When you use Galvanize, the ally clears 1 extra Stress.",""],["Talents","Sage Counsel","Envoy › Faithful","vocations/envoy.html#faithful-sage-counsel","Academics 4+ · When you help a character with teamwork, take 1 Stress to also make them Inspired.",""],["Talents","Inspired Zeal","Envoy › Faithful","vocations/envoy.html#faithful-inspired-zeal","Resolve 4+ · When an ally uses Inspired, choose other allies, up to your Resolve dots. Each becomes Determined.",""],["Talents","Sound Advice","Envoy › Mentor","vocations/envoy.html#mentor-sound-advice","reaction · Use Rousing Presence as a reaction on an ally near you who just failed a roll. They can use Inspired on that roll right away.",""],["Talents","Practical Demonstration","Envoy › Mentor","vocations/envoy.html#mentor-practical-demonstration","free action · Expression 2+ · When you take a set-up action or hit with an attack, use Rousing Presence as a free action.",""],["Talents","Lessons in Patience","Envoy › Mentor","vocations/envoy.html#mentor-lessons-in-patience","passive · Resolve 3+ · Gain an Expression (Motivational Speech) specialty. The target of your Rousing Presence also clears 1 Stress.",""],["Talents","Mighty","Envoy › Mentor","vocations/envoy.html#mentor-mighty","passive · When you hit with a weapon or unarmed attack, deal +1 damage per action spent on the attack.",""],["Talents","Instill Confidence","Envoy › Mentor","vocations/envoy.html#mentor-instill-confidence","Rousing Presence can make an ally Determined instead of Inspired.",""],["Talents","Guiding Oration","Envoy › Mentor","vocations/envoy.html#mentor-guiding-oration","passive · Expression 3+ · After you take a set-up action against a target, an ally close to that target gets +1d on their next roll against it.",""],["Talents","Foresight","Envoy › Mentor","vocations/envoy.html#mentor-foresight","passive · Resolve 4+ · You get 2 reactions per round.",""],["Talents","Rallying Shout","Envoy › Mentor","vocations/envoy.html#mentor-rallying-shout","Expression 4+ · Rousing Presence can wake an Unconscious ally: they heal 2 damage, least severe first.",""],["Talents","Tagging Shot","Hunter › Archer","vocations/hunter.html#archer-tagging-shot","2 actions · Wits 3+ · Move a short distance and make a ranged attack. On a hit or a graze, the target becomes your quarry.",""],["Talents","Combat Training","Hunter › Archer","vocations/hunter.html#archer-combat-training","passive · Gain a weapon specialty and a Socialize (Military Life) specialty, and ignore the penalties of one armor type. Once per round, you can graze without taking Stress.",""],["Talents","Sharp Eye","Hunter › Archer","vocations/hunter.html#archer-sharp-eye","1 action · Observe a target and roll Wits + Investigation, contested by Intelligence + Resolve. On a success, learn their rating, one of their proficiencies, or whether their Health, Stress or power pool is below half.",""],["Talents","Steady Aim","Hunter › Archer","vocations/hunter.html#archer-steady-aim","1 action · Dexterity 2+ · Your next ranged attack this turn has its range increased by half and deals +2 damage.",""],["Talents","Exploit Weakness","Hunter › Archer","vocations/hunter.html#archer-exploit-weakness","free action · Wits 4+ · Take a set-up action against your quarry without spending an action.",""],["Talents","Backstep","Hunter › Archer","vocations/hunter.html#archer-backstep","free action · Dexterity 3+ · After a ranged attack, take 2 Stress to disengage without spending an action. Brace if you reach cover or an obscured area.",""],["Talents","Unrelenting Salvo","Hunter › Archer","vocations/hunter.html#archer-unrelenting-salvo","passive · Dexterity 4+ · You can attack your quarry more than once per turn with the same ranged weapon. Each attack costs an action and takes the usual penalty for extra attacks.",""],["Talents","Hardy","Hunter › Archer","vocations/hunter.html#archer-hardy","passive · +2 Health.",""],["Talents","Startling Blow","Hunter › Assassin","vocations/hunter.html#assassin-startling-blow","1 action · Stealth 2+ · Take 2 Stress and make an attack. On a hit, the target is Surprised until the end of their next turn, as if ambushed. A character can be startled this way once per minute.",""],["Talents","Killing Edge","Hunter › Assassin","vocations/hunter.html#assassin-killing-edge","passive · Wits 3+ · Gain Weaponry (Knives) and Ranged (Thrown Weapons) specialties. These weapons gain the Deadly and Quickdraw traits, and you can attack with them using Dexterity instead of Strength.",""],["Talents","Fatal Thrust","Hunter › Assassin","vocations/hunter.html#assassin-fatal-thrust","2 actions · Wits 4+ · Attack an unsuspecting target with a Light melee weapon. Deal +2 damage, and gain +2d if the weapon is Discreet. On an Opportunity, choose one: the target is Bleeding for 3 turns, Slowed for one minute, or Stunned until the end of their next turn.",""],["Talents","Shadowing","Hunter › Assassin","vocations/hunter.html#assassin-shadowing","Stealth 3+ · Your quarry gets −1d to notice you, and you get +1d to avoid their notice. When your Stealth beats someone's Wits + Composure while you're in cover or obscured, take 3 Stress to make them your quarry.",""],["Talents","Mighty","Hunter › Assassin","vocations/hunter.html#assassin-mighty","passive · When you hit with a weapon or unarmed attack, deal +1 damage per action spent on the attack.",""],["Talents","Cold Eyes","Hunter › Assassin","vocations/hunter.html#assassin-cold-eyes","passive · When you defeat your quarry, choose a new quarry without spending an action. Once per day, you also clear 1 Stress.",""],["Talents","Swift Strikes","Hunter › Assassin","vocations/hunter.html#assassin-swift-strikes","1 action · Weaponry, Brawl or Ranged 3+ (the weapon's skill) · Take 2 Stress to make an extra attack with the same weapon (see Extra attacks). This is an exception to the one-attack rule.",""],["Talents","Sidestep","Hunter › Assassin","vocations/hunter.html#assassin-sidestep","Stealth 4+ · Your first Dodge each round doesn't spend your Defense, while you're not wearing armor rated 2 or higher.",""],["Talents","Deadly Trap","Hunter › Tracker","vocations/hunter.html#tracker-deadly-trap","2 actions · Survival 2+ · Conceal a trap covering a small area (see Ranges). You can have one trap set at a time, and its effects last 1 turn.",""],["Talents","Animal Bond","Hunter › Tracker","vocations/hunter.html#tracker-animal-bond","Gain a rating 2 animal companion of your choice. While your companion is close, you get +1d to defense rolls. As a free action on your turn, give your companion 1 or 2 actions. Your companion can track (2 actions): one target near it becomes your quarry.",""],["Talents","Experienced Trapper","Hunter › Tracker","vocations/hunter.html#tracker-experienced-trapper","Wits 3+ · You can forage for as many people as your Survival dots and make natural tools with Survival. You can have two traps set at once. Your traps deal 2 damage and their effects last 2 turns.",""],["Talents","Protective Bond","Hunter › Tracker","vocations/hunter.html#tracker-protective-bond","1 action · Your companion protects an ally close to you. That ally gets the Animal Bond bonus instead of you.",""],["Talents","Surefooted","Hunter › Tracker","vocations/hunter.html#tracker-surefooted","passive · +3 Speed. Reduce damage from falls and dangerous terrain by your Athletics dots.",""],["Talents","Feral Connection","Hunter › Tracker","vocations/hunter.html#tracker-feral-connection","passive · Survival 3+ · Gain an Animal Ken specialty. Your companion gains +3 Health and +1d to its defense rolls, and extra dice on its rolls equal to half your Survival, rounded up.",""],["Talents","Hunter's Edge","Hunter › Tracker","vocations/hunter.html#tracker-hunters-edge","passive · Survival 4+ · Your companion gets +1d against your quarry. Your traps deal 3 damage and their effects last 3 turns.",""],["Talents","Pack Hunting","Hunter › Tracker","vocations/hunter.html#tracker-pack-hunting","reaction · Wits 4+ · Take 1 Stress to give an ally +2d on their attack against your quarry.",""],["Talents","Combat Coordination","Leader › Champion","vocations/leader.html#champion-combat-coordination","free action · Expression 3+ · Once per turn, after you attack, use Decisive Command without spending an action. If the attack missed, it also costs no Stress.",""],["Talents","Valiant Intervention","Leader › Champion","vocations/leader.html#champion-valiant-intervention","1 action · Athletics 2+ · Take 1 Stress, move up to half your Speed, then roll Presence + Athletics against an enemy in your reach, contested by its Wits + Composure. On a success, it gets −1d on rolls against your allies until the end of its next turn.",""],["Talents","Imposing Posture","Leader › Champion","vocations/leader.html#champion-imposing-posture","passive · Athletics 3+ · When an enemy within your weapon's reach wins a contested Social roll against you, they still become Disoriented until the end of their next turn.",""],["Talents","Hardy","Leader › Champion","vocations/leader.html#champion-hardy","passive · +2 Health.",""],["Talents","Mighty","Leader › Champion","vocations/leader.html#champion-mighty","passive · When you hit with a weapon or unarmed attack, deal +1 damage per action spent on the attack.",""],["Talents","Resolute Stand","Leader › Champion","vocations/leader.html#champion-resolute-stand","Athletics 2+ · Valiant Intervention doesn't provoke Reactive Strikes. Take Stress up to your Expression dots to add that many targets to it.",""],["Talents","Resilient Hero","Leader › Champion","vocations/leader.html#champion-resilient-hero","reaction · Athletics 4+ · Once per day, when damage would fill your last Health box, instead leave empty boxes equal to your Athletics dots.",""],["Talents","Demonstrative Command","Leader › Champion","vocations/leader.html#champion-demonstrative-command","Expression 3+ · +1d to your command dice. Take 1 Stress to add your command dice to your own Athletics or Expression roll, or to any roll using Dexterity.",""],["Talents","Composed","Leader › Officer","vocations/leader.html#officer-composed","passive · +2 maximum Stress.",""],["Talents","Through the Fray","Leader › Officer","vocations/leader.html#officer-through-the-fray","1 action · Persuasion 2+ · An ally close to you can disengage or take a set-up action as a reaction.",""],["Talents","Well Supplied","Leader › Officer","vocations/leader.html#officer-well-supplied","Persuasion 3+ · Gain a Politics (Military Logistics) specialty. Take 2 Stress to gain an Opportunity on a roll to requisition resources.",""],["Talents","Customary Garb","Leader › Officer","vocations/leader.html#officer-customary-garb","passive · While dressed appropriately, +1d to Social rolls and to resist Social rolls.",""],["Talents","Relentless March","Leader › Officer","vocations/leader.html#officer-relentless-march","passive · Persuasion 4+ · Decisive Command also gives the target +3 Speed. Until their next turn, they ignore Exhausted, Slowed and Surprised.",""],["Talents","Confident Command","Leader › Officer","vocations/leader.html#officer-confident-command","Expression 3+ · +1d to your command dice. Take 1 Stress to add your command dice to your own Intimidation, Expression or Persuasion roll.",""],["Talents","Synchronized Assault","Leader › Officer","vocations/leader.html#officer-synchronized-assault","3 actions · Expression 4+ · Roll Presence + Expression, contested by the target's Intelligence + Resolve. On a success, choose allies up to your Expression dots and take 1 Stress for each; on their next turns, each gains an extra action for one more attack against the target (see Extra attacks). This is an exception to the one-attack rule. On a failure, only one ally gains it, for 1 Stress.",""],["Talents","Authority","Leader › Officer","vocations/leader.html#officer-authority","rank commanding 5+ people · Double the range of your Leader talents that affect allies, and the number of allies they affect.",""],["Talents","Cutthroat Tactics","Leader › Politico","vocations/leader.html#politico-cutthroat-tactics","Subterfuge 2+ · An ally can raise the stakes instead of using your command dice. If the stakes die shows a Complication, you clear 1 Stress.",""],["Talents","Tactical Ploy","Leader › Politico","vocations/leader.html#politico-tactical-ploy","1 action · Take 1 Stress and roll Manipulation + Subterfuge, contested by Intelligence + Resolve. On a success, the target loses their reaction and gets −1d on their next Mental or Social roll.",""],["Talents","Rumormonger","Leader › Politico","vocations/leader.html#politico-rumormonger","requires a patron · Gain a Politics (Scandal) specialty. Take 2 Stress to gain an Opportunity on a roll to spread misinformation or gather rumors.",""],["Talents","Well Dressed","Leader › Politico","vocations/leader.html#politico-well-dressed","passive · Gain a Socialize (Fashion) specialty. While dressed for the occasion, +1d on your first Subterfuge, Expression or Persuasion roll each hour.",""],["Talents","Baleful","Leader › Politico","vocations/leader.html#politico-baleful","passive · When you win a contested Mental or Social roll against a target, they also take 1 Stress.",""],["Talents","Set at Odds","Leader › Politico","vocations/leader.html#politico-set-at-odds","2 actions · Expression 4+ · Take Stress equal to the number of targets. Roll Presence + Expression, contested by the highest Wits + Composure among them. On a success, until the end of their next turn, they're Disoriented, can't use teamwork or aid each other, and take −1d on rolls that would help one another. Outside a fight, they distrust each other for one hour instead.",""],["Talents","Shrewd Command","Leader › Politico","vocations/leader.html#politico-shrewd-command","Expression 3+ · +1d to your command dice. Take 1 Stress to add your command dice to your own Subterfuge, Empathy or Expression roll.",""],["Talents","Grand Deception","Leader › Politico","vocations/leader.html#politico-grand-deception","3 actions · Subterfuge 4+ · Take 3 Stress and roll Manipulation + Subterfuge, needing 3 successes. On a success, reveal a ruse you set up earlier that changes one detail of the situation.",""],["Talents","Efficient Engineer","Scholar › Engineer","vocations/scholar.html#engineer-efficient-engineer","Crafts 2+ · Gain a Crafts (Weapon Crafting, Armor Crafting or Equipment Crafting) specialty. When crafting, your Opportunity range expands by 1 and material costs are halved.",""],["Talents","Field Repair","Scholar › Engineer","vocations/scholar.html#engineer-field-repair","2 actions · Crafts 2+ · Once per minute, roll Intelligence + Crafts (or Technology, for a Device), needing 2 successes, to clear Wear from an ally's armor up to your Crafts dots, make a broken item work until the end of the fight, or restore 1 charge to a Device.",""],["Talents","Deep Study","Scholar › Engineer","vocations/scholar.html#engineer-deep-study","passive · Erudition gives you one more specialty and two more skill dots.",""],["Talents","Inventive Design","Scholar › Engineer","vocations/scholar.html#engineer-inventive-design","Technology 3+ · Gain a Crafts (Device Crafting) specialty. You can craft Devices rated 1 higher than usual.",""],["Talents","Fine Handiwork","Scholar › Engineer","vocations/scholar.html#engineer-fine-handiwork","When crafting, advanced upgrades cost the same as ordinary upgrades.",""],["Talents","Overcharge","Scholar › Engineer","vocations/scholar.html#engineer-overcharge","Technology 4+ · Once per turn, as a free action, push a Device close to you: raise the stakes on its next roll. On an Opportunity, it acts again right away at −2d; that roll can't trigger Overcharge.",""],["Talents","Master Artisan","Scholar › Engineer","vocations/scholar.html#engineer-master-artisan","When crafting, you get 2 extra attempts and the interval is halved.",""],["Talents","Overwhelm with Details","Scholar › Engineer","vocations/scholar.html#engineer-overwhelm-with-details","Academics 4+ · Take 2 Stress to use Academics in place of another skill on a Mental or Social roll.",""],["Talents","Strategize","Scholar › Strategist","vocations/scholar.html#strategist-strategize","Investigation 2+ · After a set-up action using an Erudition skill, you can give the +1d to an ally instead. Take 2 Stress to also stop the target from reacting against that ally.",""],["Talents","Mind and Body","Scholar › Strategist","vocations/scholar.html#strategist-mind-and-body","passive · Gain a weapon specialty and one more Erudition skill. Erudition can now pick Physical skills.",""],["Talents","Composed","Scholar › Strategist","vocations/scholar.html#strategist-composed","passive · +2 maximum Stress.",""],["Talents","Know Your Moment","Scholar › Strategist","vocations/scholar.html#strategist-know-your-moment","passive · Investigation 3+ · +1d to defense rolls from the start of each round until you take your turn.",""],["Talents","Deep Contemplation","Scholar › Strategist","vocations/scholar.html#strategist-deep-contemplation","2 actions · Academics 3+ · Reassign up to 2 of your Erudition skills or specialties.",""],["Talents","Keen Insight","Scholar › Strategist","vocations/scholar.html#strategist-keen-insight","passive · When your set-up action against a target succeeds, they also get −1d on their next roll.",""],["Talents","Contingency","Scholar › Strategist","vocations/scholar.html#strategist-contingency","reaction · Academics 4+ · Take 2 Stress to remove a Complication from the roll of an ally close to you.",""],["Talents","Turning Point","Scholar › Strategist","vocations/scholar.html#strategist-turning-point","2 actions · Investigation 4+ · Once per minute, take 2 Stress and roll Wits + Investigation, contested by the enemy leader's Intelligence + Resolve (or the strongest enemy's, if there's no leader), with +1d if you took a slow turn. On a success, you and your allies gain 1 extra action on your next turns.",""],["Talents","Field Medicine","Scholar › Surgeon","vocations/scholar.html#surgeon-field-medicine","1 action · Medicine 2+ · Take 2 Stress and roll Intelligence + Medicine, needing 2 successes. On a success, a conscious character close to you heals 2 damage, least severe first, and stops Bleeding.",""],["Talents","Emotional Intelligence","Scholar › Surgeon","vocations/scholar.html#surgeon-emotional-intelligence","passive · Gain a Medicine (Diagnosis) specialty and one more Erudition skill. Erudition can now pick Social skills.",""],["Talents","Anatomical Insight","Scholar › Surgeon","vocations/scholar.html#surgeon-anatomical-insight","Once per round, when you hit with an unarmed attack, take 1 Stress or spend an Opportunity to give the target 1 stack of Exhausted. Stacks from this talent wear off after one minute.",""],["Talents","Collected","Scholar › Surgeon","vocations/scholar.html#surgeon-collected","passive · +1d to resist Mental and Social rolls.",""],["Talents","Swift Healer","Scholar › Surgeon","vocations/scholar.html#surgeon-swift-healer","Medicine 3+ · Field Medicine becomes a free action and heals 1 extra damage.",""],["Talents","Applied Medicine","Scholar › Surgeon","vocations/scholar.html#surgeon-applied-medicine","passive · Academics 3+ · Your Field Medicine heals 1 extra damage.",""],["Talents","Ongoing Care","Scholar › Surgeon","vocations/scholar.html#surgeon-ongoing-care","Academics 4+ · Gain a Medicine (Mental Health Care) specialty. When you Recover a patient, they also lose one condition caused by injury, such as Bleeding, Slowed or 1 stack of Exhausted.",""],["Talents","Resuscitation","Scholar › Surgeon","vocations/scholar.html#surgeon-resuscitation","Medicine 4+ · Field Medicine can revive a character who is Unconscious, or who died from damage within the last round, for 3 Stress instead of 2. They heal the usual amount, aggravated damage first if their track is full of it, and wake up. It can't undo death from a Bout of Madness or a Death's Mark.",""],["Talents","Practiced Kata","Warrior › Duelist","vocations/warrior.html#duelist-practiced-kata","You know subtle ways to use your stances outside of combat, and gain their bonuses there too. Unless Surprised or already in a stance, you start every fight in Vigilant Stance.",""],["Talents","Challenger's Stance","Warrior › Duelist","vocations/warrior.html#duelist-challengers-stance","1 action · Intimidation 2+ · Enter Challenger's Stance: +1d to Intimidation. When only one enemy is in your reach and no allies are, you can either attack or take a set-up action without spending an action. The one-attack rule still applies.",""],["Talents","Counter Stance","Warrior › Duelist","vocations/warrior.html#duelist-counter-stance","1 action · Athletics 3+ · Enter Counter Stance: +1d to Empathy. When an attacker misses or only grazes you, you can make a Reactive Strike against them.",""],["Talents","Signature Weapon","Warrior › Duelist","vocations/warrior.html#duelist-signature-weapon","passive · Gain a weapon specialty. Choose one of your weapon specialties: attacks with that weapon get an Opportunity on 4+ successes. If it's a Light weapon, you can attack with Dexterity instead of Strength.",""],["Talents","Surefooted","Warrior › Duelist","vocations/warrior.html#duelist-surefooted","passive · +3 Speed. Reduce damage from falls and dangerous terrain by your Athletics dots.",""],["Talents","Feinting Strike","Warrior › Duelist","vocations/warrior.html#duelist-feinting-strike","1 action · Intimidation 3+ · Take 2 Stress and make a melee weapon attack, contested by Intelligence + Resolve. On a hit, the target loses their reaction and takes Stress equal to your net successes, up to your Intimidation. On a graze, they take 1 Stress and keep their reaction.",""],["Talents","Windstance","Warrior › Duelist","vocations/warrior.html#duelist-windstance","1 action · Wits 3+ · Enter Windstance: +1d to rolls using Dexterity. While multiple enemies are in your reach, you can either disengage or attack without spending an action. The one-attack rule still applies.",""],["Talents","Wit's End","Warrior › Duelist","vocations/warrior.html#duelist-wits-end","2 actions · Intimidation 4+ · Take 1 Stress, move up to half your Speed, then make a melee weapon attack against a target with a full Stress track. The attack ignores armor, deals +3 damage and can't graze.",""],["Talents","Heavy Training","Warrior › Juggernaut","vocations/warrior.html#juggernaut-heavy-training","requires Heavy armor and a Heavy weapon · Gain an Athletics (Heavy Armor) specialty and a weapon specialty in one heavy weapon. Your armor's Wear track has 2 extra boxes. When you attack with the heavy weapon, you also graze extra targets in reach, up to your weapon skill dots.",""],["Talents","Stonestance","Warrior › Juggernaut","vocations/warrior.html#juggernaut-stonestance","1 action · Enter Stonestance: +1 armor rating. Enemies in your reach must spend 1 extra action to attack an ally who isn't in Stonestance.",""],["Talents","Repelling Stance","Warrior › Juggernaut","vocations/warrior.html#juggernaut-repelling-stance","1 action · Athletics 3+ · Enter Repelling Stance: +1d to Parry and +1d to resist Mental rolls. After you're hit or grazed in melee, use your reaction to roll Strength + Athletics, contested by Dexterity + Stamina. On a success, the attacker takes Stress equal to your net successes, up to your Athletics, and is pushed back 1 meter.",""],["Talents","Mighty","Warrior › Juggernaut","vocations/warrior.html#juggernaut-mighty","passive · When you hit with a weapon or unarmed attack, deal +1 damage per action spent on the attack.",""],["Talents","Shattering Blow","Warrior › Juggernaut","vocations/warrior.html#juggernaut-shattering-blow","Wits 3+ · When you hit with a melee attack, take 2 Stress. The target's armor takes 1 Wear (or loses 1 rating for one hour if its Wear track is already full), and every target you damage is pushed back a number of meters equal to the damage it took, of any type (bashing, lethal or aggravated). Count the hit's damage once, even if it lands across several severities. A graze or a hit stopped by armor still pushes 1 meter.",""],["Talents","Bloodstance","Warrior › Juggernaut","vocations/warrior.html#juggernaut-bloodstance","1 action · Athletics 3+ · Enter Bloodstance: your attacks get an Opportunity on 4+ successes, but your Defense is 0 and you take −1d to resist Mental and Social rolls.",""],["Talents","Shrug It Off","Warrior › Juggernaut","vocations/warrior.html#juggernaut-shrug-it-off","free action · Wits 4+ · When a melee attack hits you, whether or not you tried to Parry, take Stress to ignore that much damage, up to your Weaponry dots.",""],["Talents","Unstoppable Charge","Warrior › Juggernaut","vocations/warrior.html#juggernaut-unstoppable-charge","2 actions · Athletics 4+ · Take 2 Stress and move up to your Speed in a straight line. Make a heavy-weapon attack against each enemy you pass within reach; each defends separately. This is an exception to the one-attack rule. Each enemy you hit is pushed aside 1 meter or falls Prone, your choice. Reactive Strikes don't stop your movement. +1d in heavy armor.",""],["Talents","Cautious Advance","Warrior › Soldier","vocations/warrior.html#soldier-cautious-advance","2 actions · Resolve 2+ · Move up to half your Speed, ignoring difficult terrain. Then Brace or take a set-up action without spending an action.",""],["Talents","Combat Training","Warrior › Soldier","vocations/warrior.html#soldier-combat-training","passive · Gain a weapon specialty and a Socialize (Military Life) specialty, and ignore the penalties of one armor type. Once per round, you can graze without taking Stress.",""],["Talents","Defensive Position","Warrior › Soldier","vocations/warrior.html#soldier-defensive-position","passive · Athletics 3+ · Your Brace gives +2d instead of +1d, and allies can Brace behind you.",""],["Talents","Devastating Blow","Warrior › Soldier","vocations/warrior.html#soldier-devastating-blow","2 actions · Athletics 4+ · Make a melee weapon attack that deals +2 damage.",""],["Talents","Formation Drills","Warrior › Soldier","vocations/warrior.html#soldier-formation-drills","passive · Resolve 3+ · Allies close to you who Brace also get your Defensive Position bonus.",""],["Talents","Hardy","Warrior › Soldier","vocations/warrior.html#soldier-hardy","passive · +2 Health.",""],["Talents","Wary","Warrior › Soldier","vocations/warrior.html#soldier-wary","passive · Resolve 4+ · You can't be Surprised while your Stress track isn't full. When an effect forces Stress on you, take 1 less, to a minimum of 1.",""],["Talents","Swift Strikes","Warrior › Soldier","vocations/warrior.html#soldier-swift-strikes","1 action · Weaponry, Brawl or Ranged 3+ (the weapon's skill) · Take 2 Stress to make an extra attack with the same weapon (see Extra attacks). This is an exception to the one-attack rule.",""],["Talents","Armor of the Empty","Athrenite › Guardian","vocations/athrenite.html#guardian-armor-of-the-empty","1 action · 1 Ichor. Roll Stamina + Athren. Your skin hardens into dense, unyielding armor: +1 armor rating, or +2 at Athren 4+, on top of anything you wear. It has no Wear track, and Piercing doesn't reduce it. Evolved: when an attack hits you, you can double this armor against that attack as a free action; the power then ends.","duskworld"],["Talents","Fortitude of the Empty","Athrenite › Guardian","vocations/athrenite.html#guardian-fortitude-of-the-empty","1 action · 1 Ichor. Roll Stamina + Athren. +2d to Endure, against anything, the Dusk included. You can evoke it as part of an Endure reaction, but then it lasts only for that roll. Evolved: being Beaten Down doesn't make your attacks cost Stress while it lasts.","duskworld"],["Talents","Nullifying Pulse","Athrenite › Guardian","vocations/athrenite.html#guardian-nullifying-pulse","1 action · 1 Ichor, lasting one round per success. Roll Resolve + Athren. The Otherside pulses around you, within 10 meters, and moves with you. At the start of each of their turns, everyone else in it contests your roll with Intelligence + Resolve. On a failure, they can't use any magic that costs your Athren dots or less, and their active magic of that cost or less is suppressed (not ended) while they're in it. A Device used in it contests with its own roll. Evolved: when a power fails to work because of your pulse, regain Ichor equal to half its cost, rounded up.","duskworld"],["Talents","Pillar of Might","Athrenite › Guardian","vocations/athrenite.html#guardian-pillar-of-might","1 action · Athren 3+ · 2 Ichor. Roll Strength + Athren. You draw the Otherside into your body: +1 Strength and +1 Stamina, even beyond your normal maximum. Evolved: also +1 Size, and +2 instead of +1 to one of them.","duskworld"],["Talents","Pillar of Resolve","Athrenite › Guardian","vocations/athrenite.html#guardian-pillar-of-resolve","1 action · Athren 3+ · 2 Ichor. Roll Resolve + Athren. Your mind closes like a fist: +2d to resist mental effects, fear and compulsion. Evolved: you're immune to mental effects while it lasts, helpful ones included.","duskworld"],["Talents","Pain to Power","Athrenite › Guardian","vocations/athrenite.html#guardian-pain-to-power","1 action · Athren 4+ · 3 Ichor. Roll Resolve + Athren. While it lasts, when a creature within 10 meters takes damage, you can use your reaction to take half of it yourself, rounded up, ignoring your armor; the creature takes the rest. Each time you do, choose one: reduce the damage you take by 1; +5 Speed until the end of your next turn; +1 armor until the end of your next turn; or pass your half on to another creature within 10 meters, which contests with Stamina + Resolve (if it wins, you take it after all). Evolved: choose two.","duskworld"],["Talents","Deathtouch","Athrenite › Harbinger","vocations/athrenite.html#harbinger-deathtouch","1 action · 1 Ichor, instant. The Otherside withers what you touch. Make an unarmed attack with Presence + Athren: it deals 1 lethal damage, ignores non-magical armor, and halves magical armor, rounded down. Evolved: you can make it against a target within 10 meters instead of by touch.","duskworld"],["Talents","Lifetouch","Athrenite › Harbinger","vocations/athrenite.html#harbinger-lifetouch","1 action · 1 Ichor, instant. Touch a creature and roll Presence + Athren. It heals 1 bashing damage per success, or 1 lethal damage instead (2 at Athren 4+). Life given must pass through you: for each lethal damage you heal, you take 1 bashing damage. You can't Lifetouch yourself, and it can't heal aggravated damage, stop Bleeding or restore what's lost. Evolved: you can heal a creature within 10 meters instead of by touch.","duskworld"],["Talents","Soul Echo","Athrenite › Harbinger","vocations/athrenite.html#harbinger-soul-echo","1 action · 1 Ichor. Roll Intelligence + Athren. Where someone died within as many days as your Athren dots, or touching their body, you feel their last strong emotion or memory. You learn nothing they didn't know. Evolved: you live through their last moments clearly, and get +1d to rolls about that death or that place while it lasts.","duskworld"],["Talents","Empty Breath","Athrenite › Harbinger","vocations/athrenite.html#harbinger-empty-breath","1 action · Athren 3+ · 2 Ichor, lasting one hour per success. Breathe a memory of life into a corpse dead no longer than as many days as your Athren dots. It rises as a mindless undead under your control: a companion with a rating of your Athren − 1, proficient in Strength and Stamina. You can control one at a time; raising another frees the first, which stays in its unlife, uncontrolled, until the power ends. Evolved: it's also proficient in Dexterity, and you can control two, raised separately.","duskworld"],["Talents","Final Breath","Athrenite › Harbinger","vocations/athrenite.html#harbinger-final-breath","1 action · Athren 3+ · 2 Ichor, instant. Roll Resolve + Athren, contested by Intelligence + Resolve (or rating) of every undead within 20 meters. You force them across the threshold: each one you beat takes 1 aggravated damage per net success, doubled against mindless undead. Evolved: undead who win still take 1 aggravated damage.","duskworld"],["Talents","Psychopomp","Athrenite › Harbinger","vocations/athrenite.html#harbinger-psychopomp","1 action · Athren 4+ · 3 Ichor. Roll Presence + Athren. While it lasts, the souls of the dying pass through you. Each time a creature within 20 meters dies, choose one: you or another creature within range heals 2 bashing or 1 lethal damage; you regain 1 Ichor; you or another creature within range gets +1d on their next roll; or another creature within range takes −1d on its next roll, shaken by the passing. Evolved: choose two.","duskworld"],["Talents","Knowing Whispers","Athrenite › Listener","vocations/athrenite.html#listener-knowing-whispers","1 action · 1 Ichor. Roll Wits + Athren, contested by Intelligence + Resolve, against someone within 10 meters. You hear their surface thoughts: +1d to Social rolls against them. After a minute of listening, you can dig deeper for 1 action: contest again, and on a success learn their strongest hidden desire or something important to them about current events. You can dig again, each time contested. Evolved: +2d to Empathy against them, and your first dig needs no minute of listening.","duskworld"],["Talents","Mental Seed","Athrenite › Listener","vocations/athrenite.html#listener-mental-seed","1 action · 1 Ichor, lasting one hour per success. Roll Manipulation + Athren, contested by Intelligence + Resolve, against someone within 10 meters. You plant a seed in their mind: while it lasts, you can target them with your powers from any distance, as long as they're within 10 kilometers per Athren dot; doing so uses up the seed. Or you can end it early to give them −1d to Mental rolls for one round per success. Evolved: reaching them through the seed doesn't use it up; it loses one hour instead.","duskworld"],["Talents","Thread Whispers","Athrenite › Listener","vocations/athrenite.html#listener-thread-whispers","1 action · 1 Ichor, lasting one hour per success. Roll Intelligence + Athren and link minds with up to one person per Athren dot within 500 meters (an unwilling one contests with Intelligence + Resolve). Linked minds can speak to each other silently while they stay in range. You choose whether everyone hears everyone, each speaks only to you, or the link runs one way. Evolved: the linked can share memories and mental images, and you can link animals too.","duskworld"],["Talents","Distant Eyes","Athrenite › Listener","vocations/athrenite.html#listener-distant-eyes","1 action · Athren 3+ · 2 Ichor. Roll Intelligence + Athren and choose someone within 10 kilometers per Athren dot. You see and hear what happens within a few meters of them, blurred: −2d to rolls to notice details. An unwilling target contests with Wits + Composure. You get +1d if you hold a piece of them or something of great meaning to them, and −1d if you only know of them. It fails on the dead and on targets warded by stronger magic, and magic that reveals hidden effects can detect it. Evolved: you can choose a place you know, or hold something of, instead of a person.","duskworld"],["Talents","Distant Hand","Athrenite › Listener","vocations/athrenite.html#listener-distant-hand","1 action · Athren 3+ · 2 Ichor. Roll Intelligence + Athren. You move things within 10 meters with your mind: an object or creature of up to twice your Athren dots in Size (an unwilling creature contests with Dexterity + Stamina). You can move it at up to 2 meters per success each turn, or use a skill through it at a distance (picking a lock, swinging a dropped knife) at −1d. Holding something in the air counts its Size as double. Evolved: skills used through it take no penalty, and something held in the air counts its Size as one and a half times instead.","duskworld"],["Talents","Maddening Whispers","Athrenite › Listener","vocations/athrenite.html#listener-maddening-whispers","1 action · Athren 4+ · 3 Ichor, lasting one round per success. Roll Manipulation + Athren, contested by Intelligence + Resolve, against up to one creature per Athren dot within 20 meters. You let the Speaker's whispers into their minds. At the start of each of their turns while it lasts, each one you beat contests again: on a success they shake it off, and on a failure they roll a die: 1 −1d to all rolls this turn; 2 attack the nearest creature, or themselves if there's none; 3 stand babbling, Stunned this turn; 4 move away from you; 5 attack you or your allies; 6 take 2 Stress. Results 2, 4 and 5 follow the compulsion rules. Someone who leaves its range stays affected for one more turn. Evolved: they roll twice, and you choose the result.","duskworld"],["Talents","Twisted Limbs","Athrenite › Mawbound","vocations/athrenite.html#mawbound-twisted-limbs","1 action · 1 Ichor. Roll Dexterity + Athren. Your arms and legs grow long and sinewy: your melee and unarmed attacks gain Reach, and your jumps go twice as far. Evolved: also +1d to grapple, and +2 Speed per Athren dot.","duskworld"],["Talents","Twisted Skin","Athrenite › Mawbound","vocations/athrenite.html#mawbound-twisted-skin","1 action · 1 Ichor. Roll Dexterity + Athren. Your skin takes on a chameleon's hide: +1d to Stealth. Evolved: you can bend the light completely instead, for one round per success: rolls to see you take −2d.","duskworld"],["Talents","Voideyes","Athrenite › Mawbound","vocations/athrenite.html#mawbound-voideyes","1 action · 1 Ichor, lasting one hour per success. Roll Wits + Athren. Dozens of small eyes open across your face and body, and your own turn inky black. You ignore dice penalties from dim light and darkness, like Night Sight, but not from total darkness. You see in every direction at once, and you can't be ambushed by anyone you could see. Evolved: you also see in total darkness and the deep Dusklands as if in dim light.","duskworld"],["Talents","Voidmaws","Athrenite › Mawbound","vocations/athrenite.html#mawbound-voidmaws","1 action · Athren 3+ · 2 Ichor. Roll Strength + Athren. One part of your body swells into a monstrous limb lined with jagged maws. Choose one: arm (+1d to Athletics, and its bite is an unarmed attack dealing 2 lethal); head (its bite is an unarmed attack dealing 2 lethal, Deadly); body (+1 armor rating, or +2 at Athren 4+, with no Wear track, and anyone who rolls a Complication attacking you in melee is Grappled by the maws); or legs (you climb any surface at your full Speed without a roll). Evolved: choose two.","duskworld"],["Talents","Mawmouth","Athrenite › Mawbound","vocations/athrenite.html#mawbound-mawmouth","1 action · Athren 3+ · 2 Ichor. A proboscis lunges from your chest: make an unarmed attack with Strength + Athren against a creature within 2 meters. On a hit, it latches on while the power lasts: the target is Grappled by it, so it can't move away, but you aren't, so you can act and move freely as long as you stay within 2 meters (moving farther tears it loose and ends the power). The target can break free with the Grapple action, its Strength + Brawl against your Strength + Athren, or by dealing 3 damage to the proboscis. Once per turn, you can spend 1 action to drink: the target takes 1 lethal damage, and you choose one: heal 1 lethal or 2 bashing damage; lose 1 stack of Exhausted; +1d to Mental rolls until your next turn; or +1d to Physical rolls until your next turn. It also drinks from bodies dead no longer than as many hours as your Athren dots. Evolved: choose two.","duskworld"],["Talents","Twisted Ascension","Athrenite › Mawbound","vocations/athrenite.html#mawbound-twisted-ascension","1 action · Athren 4+ · 3 Ichor. Roll Stamina + Athren. You become a terrifying amalgam of writhing limbs, eyes and mouths. While it lasts, you gain +1 Strength, +1 Stamina and +1 Dexterity, even beyond your normal maximum, and +2d to Intimidation. Your body shifts around every blow: damage from each hit against you is reduced by 1. You squeeze through any gap half your size without a roll. Evolved: reduce the damage by 2 instead, and also gain +1 Size.","duskworld"],["Talents","Voidcrystal Weapons","Athrenite › Shardbound","vocations/athrenite.html#shardbound-voidcrystal-weapons","1 action · 1 Ichor. Roll Presence + Athren. A crystal weapon forms in your hand: any melee weapon from the weapon table of up to bulk 1, bulk 2 at Athren 3+, or bulk 3 at Athren 4+. You always know how to wield it: attack with Dexterity or Strength + the higher of Weaponry or Athren, and ignore Heavy's Strength requirement. Choose the emotion that shapes it, and change it for 1 action: Despair (Knockdown); Fury (+1 damage, loses Reach, and you can't Parry with it); Fear (Reach and Throwable); Grief (Piercing 1); Resolve (Shield (1)); Vengeance (each hit on the same target deals +1 damage more than the last, up to +2); Hope (it becomes a crystal bow: 2 lethal, Range 30 m, Piercing 1); or Longing (a crystal bow as Hope, and a target it hits takes −1d to Physical rolls until the end of its next turn). If it leaves your hand, it shatters at the end of the round. Evolved: you form two weapons at once, each with its own emotion, or one that lasts a minute out of your hand.","duskworld"],["Talents","Voidcrystal Armor","Athrenite › Shardbound","vocations/athrenite.html#shardbound-voidcrystal-armor","1 action · 1 Ichor. Roll Stamina + Athren. Voidcrystal grows around you: +1 armor rating, or +2 at Athren 4+, on top of anything you wear, with no Wear track. Evolved: when a melee attack hits you, the attacker takes 1 lethal damage from the shards, even if your armor absorbs the whole hit.","duskworld"],["Talents","Crystalforging","Athrenite › Shardbound","vocations/athrenite.html#shardbound-crystalforging","1 action · 1 Ichor. Touch a non-living object of up to bulk 1 per Athren dot and roll Presence + Athren. Voidcrystal threads it invisibly, giving it one enchantment while it lasts: Darkness (black crystal drinks the light: +1d to hide it, or to hide with it); Durable (it can't be broken, and armor gains +1 rating); Jagged (Athren 3+: a weapon deals +1 damage); or Void (Athren 3+: it's coated in ice: −2d to grab it or keep your footing on it, and a weapon's hits leave the target Slowed until the end of its next turn). Evolved: two enchantments on the same object.","duskworld"],["Talents","Shatterstep","Athrenite › Shardbound","vocations/athrenite.html#shardbound-shatterstep","1 action · Athren 3+ · 2 Ichor, instant. Roll Dexterity + Athren. You shatter into dust and reform anywhere within 10 meters, or 20 meters if you reform beside one of your crystal weapons. For 4 Ichor, you can do it as a reaction to an attack, moving only 1 meter: a melee attack without Reach misses. Evolved: you can call your crystal weapons back to your hand instead, from within 20 meters.","duskworld"],["Talents","Voidcrystal Echoes","Athrenite › Shardbound","vocations/athrenite.html#shardbound-voidcrystal-echoes","1 action · Athren 3+ · 2 Ichor, lasting one round per success. Roll Presence + Athren. Your crystals sing, within 10 meters of you. At the start of each round while it lasts, choose one: allies get a free defense reaction this round; allies get +2 Speed; no sound can be made in the area; allies gain +1 armor this round; enemies take −1d to all rolls this round; or enemies contest your roll with Wits + Composure or are Disoriented this round. Evolved: choose two each round.","duskworld"],["Talents","Voidcrystal Form","Athrenite › Shardbound","vocations/athrenite.html#shardbound-voidcrystal-form","1 action · Athren 4+ · 3 Ichor. Roll Stamina + Athren. You fill with voidcrystal and become a living weapon. While it lasts, you gain +1 armor rating with no Wear track, +1 Strength and +1 Dexterity, even beyond your normal maximum, and your unarmed attacks deal 1 lethal. Each turn, you gain one extra action that can only be used to attack or Move. Evolved: instead, you can split off your crystal echo: a companion with a rating of your Athren − 1, proficient in Weaponry, Brawl and Athletics, carrying a copy of any crystal weapon you hold. It acts on your turn without costing your actions.","duskworld"],["Talents","Veilsight","Athrenite › Veilwalker","vocations/athrenite.html#veilwalker-veilsight","1 action · 1 Ichor. Roll Wits + Athren. You see the aura of anyone you look at: whether they have a power, roughly how full its pool is, and the nature of any power or Device at work on them. You see through illusions that hide a creature's true form (figments, dreamguises, shadowplay), and into the Otherside where the Veil is thin, which isn't always a good thing. Evolved: you also glimpse their instinctive feelings: +1d to Social rolls and defense rolls against them.","duskworld"],["Talents","Magical Fracture","Athrenite › Veilwalker","vocations/athrenite.html#veilwalker-magical-fracture","1 action · 1 Ichor, lasting one round per success. Roll Resolve + Athren, contested by Intelligence + Resolve, against a creature within 20 meters. If you win, you cut it off from its powers while yours lasts: it can't use any magic that costs your Athren dots or less, its active magic of that cost or less is suppressed (not ended), and its other magic takes −2d. It contests again at the start of each of its turns. Evolved: when a power fails to work because of the fracture, regain Ichor equal to half its cost, rounded up.","duskworld"],["Talents","Magical Adoption","Athrenite › Veilwalker","vocations/athrenite.html#veilwalker-magical-adoption","1 action · 1 Ichor. Roll Intelligence + Athren, contested by Intelligence + Resolve, against a creature within 10 meters, and name one of its powers: one with no skill requirement, one requiring 3+ at Athren 3+, or one requiring 4+ at Athren 4+. If you win, you can use that power while yours lasts, rolling Athren in place of its power skill and paying in Ichor, and the creature takes −2d to use it. If it doesn't have that power, your power fails. Evolved: if it doesn't have the power you named, you take a random one it has that you could have named.","duskworld"],["Talents","Voidstep","Athrenite › Veilwalker","vocations/athrenite.html#veilwalker-voidstep","1 action · Athren 3+ · 2 Ichor, instant. Roll Wits + Athren. You step through the Veil and out of it anywhere within 20 meters. You must be able to move: you physically walk through the Otherside. For 4 Ichor, you can do it as a reaction to an attack, moving only 1 meter: a melee attack without Reach misses. Evolved: the range becomes 50 meters.","duskworld"],["Talents","Void Flicker","Athrenite › Veilwalker","vocations/athrenite.html#veilwalker-void-flicker","1 action · Athren 3+ · 2 Ichor, lasting one round per success. Roll Resolve + Athren. You flicker in and out of the Otherside. Attacks and powers aimed at you take −1d, and those that still hit deal half damage, rounded down, or have half their effect. You slip free of any grapple, bonds or restraint. For 4 Ichor, you can evoke it as a reaction to a single attack, lasting only for that attack. Evolved: attacks and powers aimed at you take −2d instead of −1d.","duskworld"],["Talents","Lift the Veil","Athrenite › Veilwalker","vocations/athrenite.html#veilwalker-lift-the-veil","1 action · Athren 4+ · 3 Ichor, lasting one round per success. Roll Resolve + Athren. You drag the Otherside through the Veil over an area 20 meters across, centered within 50 meters of you. At the start of each round while it lasts, roll a die and choose that result or one next to it: 1 everyone in it gains an extra action this round; 2 it's difficult terrain; 3 everyone in it heals 1 bashing damage; 4 everyone in it takes 1 bashing damage; 5 everyone moving through it moves at double Speed; 6 everyone in it loses one action this round. Evolved: roll twice and choose from either.","duskworld"],["Talents","Steady Blood","Athrenite › Vessel","vocations/athrenite.html#vessel-steady-blood","passive · +2d to the Resolve + Composure roll to resist your Athren Stage rising.","duskworld"],["Talents","Tempered Vessel","Athrenite › Vessel","vocations/athrenite.html#vessel-tempered-vessel","passive · Your threshold is your Athren dots + 1.","duskworld"],["Talents","Wide Threshold","Athrenite › Vessel","vocations/athrenite.html#vessel-wide-threshold","passive · Athren 3+ · Your threshold is your Athren dots + 3.","duskworld"],["Talents","Unbroken Vessel","Athrenite › Vessel","vocations/athrenite.html#vessel-unbroken-vessel","passive · Athren 4+ · Once per day, when your Athren Stage would rise, it doesn't.","duskworld"],["Talents","Figments","Dreamshaper › Mesmer","vocations/dreamshaper.html#mesmer-figments","2 actions · 1 Reverie. Roll Wits + Dreamshaping to create an illusion of sight and sound within 10 meters, filling up to one small area per Dreamshaping dot. Moving it takes 1 action. Anyone who touches it, or has a reason to doubt it, rolls Wits + Composure contested by your roll to see through it. A figment can hide you as if you were Obscured, and you can use it to set up anyone it fools.","duskworld"],["Talents","Mental Awakening","Dreamshaper › Mesmer","vocations/dreamshaper.html#mesmer-mental-awakening","2 actions · 1 Reverie. Roll Intelligence + Dreamshaping. Your mind opens to the Everdream: +2d to Mental skill rolls while it lasts.","duskworld"],["Talents","Dreamguise","Dreamshaper › Mesmer","vocations/dreamshaper.html#mesmer-dreamguise","2 actions · Dreamshaping 2+ · 2 Reverie. Roll Intelligence + Dreamshaping to disguise yourself, or a willing creature you touch, as anyone of about the same size. Anyone who doubts the disguise contests it with Wits + Composure. The disguised character gets +1d to Subterfuge to pass as that person.","duskworld"],["Talents","Dread","Dreamshaper › Mesmer","vocations/dreamshaper.html#mesmer-dread","2 actions · Dreamshaping 2+ · 2 Reverie. Push a nightmare into the mind of someone within 10 meters: roll Manipulation + Dreamshaping, contested by Wits + Composure. On a success, they're Frightened of you while the dreamcraft lasts.","duskworld"],["Talents","Dreamwalk","Dreamshaper › Mesmer","vocations/dreamshaper.html#mesmer-dreamwalk","Dreamshaping 3+ · Dreamed only. 2 Reverie. Roll Resolve + Dreamshaping and choose up to one sleeper per Dreamshaping dot, each someone whose face and name you know, within 10 kilometers per dot. While it lasts, whenever you sleep you can enter one of their dreams and speak with them freely; joining another means leaving the first. An unwilling dreamer contests with Intelligence + Resolve each time you enter. Your presence lingers: +1d to your next Social roll against them.","duskworld"],["Talents","Dreamsteal","Dreamshaper › Mesmer","vocations/dreamshaper.html#mesmer-dreamsteal","Dreamshaping 3+ · Dreamed or deep meditation only. 3 Reverie, instant. Choose someone you know within 10 kilometers per Dreamshaping dot: roll Manipulation + Dreamshaping, contested by Intelligence + Resolve. On a success, their next rest gives them nothing (no Stress cleared, no healing, nothing refreshed) and they gain 1 stack of Exhausted. Most dreamshapers find it vile.","duskworld"],["Talents","Dreamveil","Dreamshaper › Mesmer","vocations/dreamshaper.html#mesmer-dreamveil","Dreamshaping 4+ · Dreamed or deep meditation only. 4 Reverie. Roll Intelligence + Dreamshaping. A building, or a camp up to 20 meters across, looks and sounds empty and ordinary from outside. Finding it means beating your roll with Wits + Investigation. Hidden kehar settlements are kept this way.","duskworld"],["Talents","Enthrall","Dreamshaper › Mesmer","vocations/dreamshaper.html#mesmer-enthrall","2 actions · Dreamshaping 4+ · 3 Reverie. Roll Manipulation + Dreamshaping, contested by Intelligence + Resolve. On a success, the target obeys one short command while the dreamcraft lasts, following the compulsion rules.","duskworld"],["Talents","Dreamstuff","Dreamshaper › Maker","vocations/dreamshaper.html#maker-dreamstuff","2 actions · 1 Reverie. Roll Intelligence + Dreamshaping to shape a simple object of up to bulk 1: a rope, a tool, a key blank, or a weapon that counts as Light. Nothing with intricate moving parts. A tool works as a kit with charges equal to your successes, up to 3.","duskworld"],["Talents","Reshape","Dreamshaper › Maker","vocations/dreamshaper.html#maker-reshape","2 actions · Dreamshaping 2+ · 2 Reverie. Roll Intelligence + Dreamshaping to change one property of a non-living object you touch, up to bulk 1 per Dreamshaping dot: its shape, hardness or texture. Make a broken item work, soften a lock until it opens (your roll against the lock), or raise handholds in a wall.","duskworld"],["Talents","Dream Companion","Dreamshaper › Maker","vocations/dreamshaper.html#maker-dream-companion","2 actions · Dreamshaping 3+ · 3 Reverie. Roll Presence + Dreamshaping to call a creature from the Everdream. It's a companion with a rating of your Dreamshaping − 1, proficient in what you choose. It dissolves when the dreamcraft ends or its Health fills. You can have only one at a time.","duskworld"],["Talents","Shape Ground","Dreamshaper › Maker","vocations/dreamshaper.html#maker-shape-ground","2 actions · Dreamshaping 3+ · 2 Reverie. Roll Presence + Dreamshaping. Within 10 meters, turn a small area into difficult terrain, smooth difficult terrain away, or raise a wall that gives full cover.","duskworld"],["Talents","Unmake","Dreamshaper › Maker","vocations/dreamshaper.html#maker-unmake","2 actions · Dreamshaping 4+ · 3 Reverie, instant. Roll Intelligence + Dreamshaping to return a non-living object of up to bulk 1 per Dreamshaping dot to the Everdream. A Device contests with its own roll. Against another dreamshaper's dreamcraft or a creature of the Everdream, it's contested by their dreamcraft roll or rating; if you win, it ends.","duskworld"],["Talents","Rewrite","Dreamshaper › Maker","vocations/dreamshaper.html#maker-rewrite","3 actions · Dreamshaping 4+ · 5 Reverie, once per day. The world is only another dream. Roll Presence + Dreamshaping to change one thing about your surroundings within 10 meters, with the GM's approval: a fall becomes a slow drift, a door becomes a wall, a fire becomes cold smoke. Creatures it affects contest with Dexterity + Stamina. When the dreamcraft ends, so does the change.","duskworld"],["Talents","Listening Shadows","Shadowcaller › Ashtai","vocations/shadowcaller.html#ashtai-listening-shadows","1 action · 1 Umbra. Roll Wits + Shadowcalling and choose a willing creature within 10 meters. While it lasts, you can speak mind to mind through your shadows at any distance, as long as both of you cast a shadow or stand in darkness. The speaker's shadow takes on the other's shape, and the voice carries no accent or other telltale sign.","duskworld"],["Talents","Shadowcloak","Shadowcaller › Ashtai","vocations/shadowcaller.html#ashtai-shadowcloak","1 action · 1 Umbra. Roll Dexterity + Shadowcalling. While you move no more than one Move a turn, you get +1d to Stealth, and rolls to spot you take −1d.","duskworld"],["Talents","Umbral Sight","Shadowcaller › Ashtai","vocations/shadowcaller.html#ashtai-umbral-sight","1 action · 1 Umbra. Roll Wits + Shadowcalling. You ignore penalties from darkness within 10 meters per Shadowcalling dot, even total darkness and the deep Dusklands.","duskworld"],["Talents","Eyes of the Night","Shadowcaller › Ashtai","vocations/shadowcaller.html#ashtai-eyes-of-the-night","2 actions · Shadowcalling 3+ · 2 Umbra. Roll Intelligence + Shadowcalling and choose someone within 10 kilometers per Shadowcalling dot. You see and hear what happens within a few meters of them, blurred: −2d to rolls to notice details. An unwilling target contests with Wits + Composure. You get +1d if you hold a piece of them (hair, blood) or something of great meaning to them, and −1d if you only know of them. It fails on the dead and on targets warded by stronger magic, and magic that reveals hidden effects can detect it.","duskworld"],["Talents","Shadowstep","Shadowcaller › Ashtai","vocations/shadowcaller.html#ashtai-shadowstep","2 actions · Shadowcalling 3+ · 2 Umbra, instant. Roll Dexterity + Shadowcalling. Step into a shadow large and dark enough to hold you, and out of another within 10 meters. For 4 Umbra, you can do it as a reaction. Until the end of your turn, defense rolls against your attacks take −2d.","duskworld"],["Talents","Nightform","Shadowcaller › Ashtai","vocations/shadowcaller.html#ashtai-nightform","3 actions · Shadowcalling 4+ · 3 Umbra. Roll Resolve + Shadowcalling. You become the night itself. While it lasts, you're Obscured, attacks against you take −2d, you make no sound, and you pass through any gap a shadow could. You can't attack, cast other blessings or hold anything. In bright light it ends at once, and you're Stunned until the end of your next turn.","duskworld"],["Talents","False Reflection","Shadowcaller › Kalestri","vocations/shadowcaller.html#kalestri-false-reflection","1 action · 1 Umbra, lasting one round per success. Roll Manipulation + Shadowcalling. In at least dim light, a shadow double mimics your movements, slightly wrong. Each round, the first roll made to read your actions or react to them, including a defense roll against your attack, takes −2d, or −1d if it's made by someone adjacent to you. When such a roll fails, you choose what they mistook your action for.","duskworld"],["Talents","Glibness of Shadow","Shadowcaller › Kalestri","vocations/shadowcaller.html#kalestri-glibness-of-shadow","1 action · 1 Umbra. Roll Presence + Shadowcalling. In at least dim light, you get +1d to Social rolls to influence others, and rolls to read your emotions or catch your lies take −1d.","duskworld"],["Talents","Umbral Temptation","Shadowcaller › Kalestri","vocations/shadowcaller.html#kalestri-umbral-temptation","1 action · 1 Umbra. Roll Manipulation + Shadowcalling, contested by Intelligence + Resolve, against someone within 10 meters. On a success, you plant a powerful but wholly artificial desire: adoration of you, hatred of a friend, hunger for something they never wanted. It's a nudge, not a compulsion: they feel the desire as their own and take −2d to resist acting on it while it lasts, but what they do is still their choice.","duskworld"],["Talents","Shadow Possession","Shadowcaller › Kalestri","vocations/shadowcaller.html#kalestri-shadow-possession","2 actions · Shadowcalling 3+ · 2 Umbra. Touch a creature's shadow and roll Manipulation + Shadowcalling, contested by Intelligence + Resolve. On a success, you melt into its shadow and possess it: your body vanishes, you share its senses, and you move it with your own actions. Bright light on the creature ends it and casts you out beside it.","duskworld"],["Talents","Shadowplay","Shadowcaller › Kalestri","vocations/shadowcaller.html#kalestri-shadowplay","2 actions · Shadowcalling 3+ · 2 Umbra. Roll Manipulation + Shadowcalling. The shadows over an area or a creature within 10 meters take on a false appearance: a dog looks like a monster, sounds like it roars, feels like silk. You change one sense, plus one more per two Shadowcalling dots, and can't change it again once set. Anyone who interacts with it contests with Wits + Composure to shrug off its effects (pain, fear, nausea), but it can't harm anyone. Bright light dispels it.","duskworld"],["Talents","Form of the Whisperer","Shadowcaller › Kalestri","vocations/shadowcaller.html#kalestri-form-of-the-whisperer","3 actions · Shadowcalling 4+ · 3 Umbra. Roll Presence + Shadowcalling. The shadows around you rise and become real, showing the secrets and fears of those nearby. While it lasts, you gain +1 Dexterity, your unarmed attacks are claws dealing 1 lethal, and enemies within 10 meters take −1d on rolls against you. In total darkness, non-magical damage against you is halved, rounded down. Once per turn, for 1 action, a shadow within 20 meters (in at least dim light) rises and takes one action of yours with your dice, then sinks back. If it attacks, that's your attack for the turn.","duskworld"],["Talents","Umbral Sight","Shadowcaller › Meneren","vocations/shadowcaller.html#meneren-umbral-sight","1 action · 1 Umbra. Roll Wits + Shadowcalling. You ignore penalties from darkness within 10 meters per Shadowcalling dot, even total darkness and the deep Dusklands.","duskworld"],["Talents","Umbral Speed","Shadowcaller › Meneren","vocations/shadowcaller.html#meneren-umbral-speed","1 action · 1 Umbra. Roll Dexterity + Shadowcalling. Your Speed increases by 1 per Shadowcalling dot.","duskworld"],["Talents","Shadow Limbs","Shadowcaller › Meneren","vocations/shadowcaller.html#meneren-shadow-limbs","1 action · 1 Umbra. Roll Dexterity + Shadowcalling. Your limbs stretch and bend like shadows: your unarmed attacks gain Reach, and you get +1d to climb and to grapple.","duskworld"],["Talents","Form of the Hunter","Shadowcaller › Meneren","vocations/shadowcaller.html#meneren-form-of-the-hunter","2 actions · Shadowcalling 3+ · 2 Umbra, lasting ten minutes per success. Roll Stamina + Shadowcalling. You take the shape of a predator beast made of wisps of shadow, built to run down prey and kill it. You gain +1 Strength and +1 Dexterity, and your bite and claws are unarmed attacks dealing 1 lethal. Choose one: Leaper (after a Move of at least 3 meters, +1d to your next attack this turn) or Many-legged (+4 Speed, +2d to resist being knocked Prone). In this form you can't speak or use weapons and tools; if you're knocked out, you return to your own shape.","duskworld"],["Talents","Form of the Stalker","Shadowcaller › Meneren","vocations/shadowcaller.html#meneren-form-of-the-stalker","2 actions · Shadowcalling 3+ · 2 Umbra, lasting ten minutes per success. Roll Dexterity + Shadowcalling. You take the shape of an insect the size of a child, made of wisps of shadow, built to creep, spy and get in where no one else can. You gain +1 Dexterity. You move without a sound, climb any surface at your full Speed without a roll, get +2d to Stealth, and slip through any gap a child could. Your bite is an unarmed attack dealing 1 lethal; against an unsuspecting target, it also leaves them Stunned until the end of their next turn. Your many legs give +4 Speed and +2d to resist being knocked Prone. In this form you can't speak or use weapons and tools; if you're knocked out, you return to your own shape.","duskworld"],["Talents","Form of the Formless","Shadowcaller › Meneren","vocations/shadowcaller.html#meneren-form-of-the-formless","3 actions · Shadowcalling 4+ · 3 Umbra. Roll Resolve + Shadowcalling. You merge the Hunter and the Stalker into the Formless, a terrifying creature of smoke, silence and entropy. While it lasts, you gain +1 Strength and +1 Dexterity, your claws are unarmed attacks dealing 1 lethal, you fly at your Speed, attacks against you take −1d as your shape flickers, and you slip through narrow gaps and under doors without a roll. In total darkness, non-magical damage against you is halved, rounded down.","duskworld"],["Talents","Umbral Armor","Shadowcaller › Vash'arai","vocations/shadowcaller.html#vasharai-umbral-armor","1 action · 1 Umbra. Roll Stamina + Shadowcalling. Darkness hardens around you: +1 armor rating, or +2 at Shadowcalling 4+, on top of anything you wear. It has no Wear track, and it vanishes while you're in bright light.","duskworld"],["Talents","Umbral Fortitude","Shadowcaller › Vash'arai","vocations/shadowcaller.html#vasharai-umbral-fortitude","1 action · 1 Umbra. Roll Stamina + Shadowcalling. Darkness steadies your flesh: you gain 2 extra Health boxes while it lasts (damage in them is lost when it ends), +2d to Endure against persistent damage, poison and hazards, and in dim light or darkness, being Beaten Down doesn't make your attacks cost Stress. You can call it as part of an Endure reaction, but then it lasts only for that roll, and gives no extra Health.","duskworld"],["Talents","Umbral Strength","Shadowcaller › Vash'arai","vocations/shadowcaller.html#vasharai-umbral-strength","1 action · 1 Umbra. Roll Strength + Shadowcalling. Your melee and unarmed attacks deal +1 damage, or +2 at Shadowcalling 4+.","duskworld"],["Talents","Shadow Mend","Shadowcaller › Vash'arai","vocations/shadowcaller.html#vasharai-shadow-mend","2 actions · Shadowcalling 3+ · 2 Umbra. Roll Stamina + Shadowcalling. Shadows knit your flesh. While it lasts, you stop Bleeding and can't start, and you heal 1 bashing damage at the end of each minute. When it ends, you heal 1 lethal damage.","duskworld"],["Talents","Shadow Strike","Shadowcaller › Vash'arai","vocations/shadowcaller.html#vasharai-shadow-strike","2 actions · Shadowcalling 3+ · 2 Umbra. Roll Strength + Shadowcalling. Claws of darkness grow from your arms: your unarmed attacks deal 1 lethal and ignore half of any non-magical armor rating, rounded up.","duskworld"],["Talents","Form of the Beast","Shadowcaller › Vash'arai","vocations/shadowcaller.html#vasharai-form-of-the-beast","3 actions · Shadowcalling 4+ · 3 Umbra. Roll Strength + Shadowcalling. Shadows coil around you and harden into the Form of the Beast. While it lasts, you gain +1 Strength, +1 Dexterity and +1 Stamina, +1d to Intimidation, and claws dealing 1 lethal; non-magical damage against you is halved, rounded down. For 1 action, you can roar: everyone within 10 meters contests your Presence + Intimidation with Wits + Composure or becomes Frightened of you while the blessing lasts. On the turn you call the blessing, the roar is a free action and uses your Shadowcalling roll.","duskworld"],["Talents","Shadow Trap","Shadowcaller › Velkhar","vocations/shadowcaller.html#velkhar-shadow-trap","1 action · 1 Umbra, lasting one round per success. Roll Wits + Shadowcalling, contested by Dexterity + Stamina, against a creature within 10 meters that casts a shadow. On a success, you pin its shadow to the ground: it's Immobilized while the blessing lasts. Breaking free takes 1 action and Strength + Athletics against your roll, and ends the blessing. It ends at once if the creature is in total darkness.","duskworld"],["Talents","Sense Warmth","Shadowcaller › Velkhar","vocations/shadowcaller.html#velkhar-sense-warmth","1 action · 1 Umbra. Roll Wits + Shadowcalling. You sense every living creature within 10 meters per Shadowcalling dot by its warmth, even through walls and in total darkness, and you know roughly how hurt each one is.","duskworld"],["Talents","Shadow Veil","Shadowcaller › Velkhar","vocations/shadowcaller.html#velkhar-shadow-veil","1 action · 1 Umbra. Roll Resolve + Shadowcalling. Within 10 meters, the light drops one step: bright light becomes ordinary, ordinary becomes dim, dim becomes darkness. Candles snuff out, and each lantern goes out on a roll of 1–3 on a die. It can't dim the Allmother's light.","duskworld"],["Talents","Umbral Drain","Shadowcaller › Velkhar","vocations/shadowcaller.html#velkhar-umbral-drain","2 actions · Shadowcalling 3+ · 2 Umbra, lasting one round per success. Roll Presence + Shadowcalling, contested by Dexterity + Stamina, against up to one creature per Shadowcalling dot within 10 meters. Choose Strength, Dexterity or Stamina: each creature you beat loses 1 dot of it (to a minimum of 1) while the blessing lasts, and you gain 1 dot of it. Victims of a drained Stamina look wasted and skeletal.","duskworld"],["Talents","Cold Touch","Shadowcaller › Velkhar","vocations/shadowcaller.html#velkhar-cold-touch","2 actions · Shadowcalling 3+ · 2 Umbra, instant. Touch a creature and roll Presence + Shadowcalling, contested by Stamina + Resolve. On a success, you draw the warmth out of it: it gains 1 stack of Exhausted, and you either regain 1 Umbra or clear 2 Stress.","duskworld"],["Talents","Devour","Shadowcaller › Velkhar","vocations/shadowcaller.html#velkhar-devour","3 actions · Shadowcalling 4+ · 3 Umbra, lasting one round per success. Roll Presence + Shadowcalling, contested by Stamina + Resolve, against up to one creature per Shadowcalling dot within 10 meters. Shadow tendrils tether each one you beat to you. While it lasts, at the end of each of your turns, each tethered creature takes 1 bashing damage, and for each one you heal 1 bashing damage, or 1 lethal damage if that creature's damage became lethal (because its Health was already full), up to 2 healed per turn however many are tethered. A tether breaks if its creature gets more than 20 meters away or steps into bright light.","duskworld"],["Rules","Reading a talent","Talents › Reading a talent","talents/index.html#reading-a-talent","Each talent is written as Name followed by tags, then its effect: after: the talent you need before you can take this one. Talents tagged after key talent start a tree and need only the vocation's key talent. Where two talents are listed (\"after A or B\"), either one is enough. Cost: 1 action, 2 actions, 3 actions, free action, reaction or passive. Prerequisites: skills, attributes, community traits or gear the talent requires, such as Wits 3+ or requires an animal companion. They apply on top of the after tag. Prerequisites count only permanent dots. Temporary dots from talents (such as the Scholar's Erudition) help your rolls but never meet a talent's or trait's prerequisite.",""],["Rules","after","Talents › Reading a talent","talents/index.html#reading-a-talent","after: the talent you need before you can take this one. Talents tagged after key talent start a tree and need only the vocation's key talent. Where two talents are listed (\"after A or B\"), either one is enough. ",""],["Rules","Cost","Talents › Reading a talent","talents/index.html#reading-a-talent","Cost: 1 action, 2 actions, 3 actions, free action, reaction or passive. ",""],["Rules","Prerequisites","Talents › Reading a talent","talents/index.html#reading-a-talent","Prerequisites: skills, attributes, community traits or gear the talent requires, such as Wits 3+ or requires an animal companion. They apply on top of the after tag. ",""],["Rules","Talent trees","Talents › Talent trees","talents/index.html#talent-trees","Each vocation has a key talent and three trees of talents. You can have more than one vocation by gaining another vocation's key talent through advancement or your free pick at character creation. After that, its trees are open to you too. Communities use the same tree structure for their traits (see Character Creation).",""],["Rules","Traits","Talents › Traits","talents/index.html#traits","Traits are where you come from; talents are what you trained. There are three kinds in the core rules: Racial traits: a race's primary and secondary trait (see Character Creation). Community traits: the key and further traits of a community (see Character Creation). Weapon traits: properties of a weapon, such as Deadly or Quickdraw (see Weapon traits). Settings can add their own kinds, always named with what they belong to, such as Duskworld's Dusk traits, which come with the stages of the Dusking Scar (see The Dusk).",""],["Traits","Racial traits","Talents › Traits","talents/index.html#traits","Racial traits: a race's primary and secondary trait (see Character Creation). ",""],["Traits","Community traits","Talents › Traits","talents/index.html#traits","Community traits: the key and further traits of a community (see Character Creation). ",""],["Traits","Weapon traits","Talents › Traits","talents/index.html#traits","Weapon traits: properties of a weapon, such as Deadly or Quickdraw (see Weapon traits). ",""],["Rules","Terms","Talents › Terms","talents/index.html#terms","Specialties from talents and traits always name their skill, written Skill (Specialty): Socialize (Military Life), Athletics (Heavy Armor). They work even with 0 dots in the skill (see Specialties). Weapon specialty: a specialty in Weaponry, Ranged or Brawl that names a weapon type, such as Weaponry (Swords), Ranged (Crossbows) or Brawl (Boxing). Usage limits (once per round, minute, hour or day) are measured in in-world time (see Core Rules). Unsuspecting: Surprised or unaware of you (see Ambush). Quarry: one character at a time, chosen with Seek Quarry and related talents. Stances: entering one takes 1 action. You're in one stance at a time; it lasts until you change it or one hour passes. Patron: contacts, allies, a mentor or standing in the relevant circle, usually from a community trait that says you count as having a patron. Wear: armor's damage track, used by the heavy-armor talents (see Armor).",""],["Rules","Specialties from talents and traits","Talents › Terms","talents/index.html#terms","Specialties from talents and traits always name their skill, written Skill (Specialty): Socialize (Military Life), Athletics (Heavy Armor). They work even with 0 dots in the skill (see Specialties). ",""],["Rules","Weapon specialty","Talents › Terms","talents/index.html#terms","Weapon specialty: a specialty in Weaponry, Ranged or Brawl that names a weapon type, such as Weaponry (Swords), Ranged (Crossbows) or Brawl (Boxing). ",""],["Rules","Usage limits","Talents › Terms","talents/index.html#terms","Usage limits (once per round, minute, hour or day) are measured in in-world time (see Core Rules). ",""],["Rules","Unsuspecting","Talents › Terms","talents/index.html#terms","Unsuspecting: Surprised or unaware of you (see Ambush). ",""],["Rules","Quarry","Talents › Terms","talents/index.html#terms","Quarry: one character at a time, chosen with Seek Quarry and related talents. ",""],["Rules","Stances","Talents › Terms","talents/index.html#terms","Stances: entering one takes 1 action. You're in one stance at a time; it lasts until you change it or one hour passes. ",""],["Rules","Patron","Talents › Terms","talents/index.html#terms","Patron: contacts, allies, a mentor or standing in the relevant circle, usually from a community trait that says you count as having a patron. ",""],["Rules","Wear","Talents › Terms","talents/index.html#terms","Wear: armor's damage track, used by the heavy-armor talents (see Armor). ",""],["Rules","Making an item","Crafting › Making an item","crafting.html#making-an-item","Choose what you're making, gather the materials, and roll at the interval below until you reach the target. Pool: Intelligence + Crafts. Use Technology for the setting's most advanced machines, and Science for chemicals and compounds. A tool kit gives +1d as usual (see Kits). Target: set by the item's complexity, plus any upgrades you declare (below). Interval: an hour for small items, a day for weapons and armor, a week for large or heavy work. Attempts: you can make a number of rolls up to your dice pool when you start (Intelligence + Crafts, plus a kit or specialty). Unlike other extended actions, crafting has this limit as well as the Stress cost below. If you run out before reaching the target, see Defects. Materials: half the item's price in wealth (see Wealth), or salvaged and found materials at the GM's call. Complexity Target Examples Simple 3 Knife, club, rope ladder, a simple trap Standard 6 Sword, spear, light armor, a kit Complex 10 Crossbow, medium armor, a lock, a rating 1–2 Device Masterwork 15 Heavy armor, a rifle, a rating 3+ Device As in any extended action, a roll with no successes costs 1 Stress to press on; if you don't pay, you stop there, as if you'd run out",""],["Rules","Pool","Crafting › Making an item","crafting.html#making-an-item","Pool: Intelligence + Crafts. Use Technology for the setting's most advanced machines, and Science for chemicals and compounds. A tool kit gives +1d as usual (see Kits). ",""],["Rules","Target","Crafting › Making an item","crafting.html#making-an-item","Target: set by the item's complexity, plus any upgrades you declare (below). ",""],["Rules","Interval","Crafting › Making an item","crafting.html#making-an-item","Interval: an hour for small items, a day for weapons and armor, a week for large or heavy work. ",""],["Rules","Attempts","Crafting › Making an item","crafting.html#making-an-item","Attempts: you can make a number of rolls up to your dice pool when you start (Intelligence + Crafts, plus a kit or specialty). Unlike other extended actions, crafting has this limit as well as the Stress cost below. If you run out before reaching the target, see Defects. ",""],["Rules","Materials","Crafting › Making an item","crafting.html#making-an-item","Materials: half the item's price in wealth (see Wealth), or salvaged and found materials at the GM's call. ",""],["Rules","Defects","Crafting › Defects","crafting.html#defects","If you run out of attempts with at least half the target (counting declared upgrades), the item is finished but defective: it gets no upgrades and one defect from the table below. With less than half, the work fails and the materials are lost. A defect can be fixed later as a repair, like broken gear: the target is half the item's crafting target, rounded up. Item Defect Weapon Unreliable: a Complication on an attack with it breaks it Armor Ill-fitting: its Stealth and Athletics penalty is 1 die worse (light armor gains −1d) Kit 2 charges instead of 3 Device 1 charge fewer, and a Complication when using it spends all its charges Other Crude: it works, but anyone who knows the craft can tell (−1d to Socialize where it's seen)",""],["Rules","Upgrades","Crafting › Upgrades","crafting.html#upgrades","An upgrade improves an item beyond the ordinary. An item can hold up to your Crafts dots in upgrades, counting an advanced upgrade as two.",""],["Rules","Declared upgrades","Crafting › Upgrades","crafting.html#declared-upgrades","Before you start, declare the upgrades you want. Each one adds 2 to the target, and each advanced upgrade adds 4. That's cheaper than refining, but you risk more: if you run out of attempts before reaching the full target, you lose every declared upgrade and the item has a defect or fails (see Defects).",""],["Rules","Refining","Crafting › Upgrades","crafting.html#refining","Once you reach the target, you can push for more with the attempts you have left: Every 3 successes from these rolls add one upgrade (an advanced upgrade needs 6). That costs more than declaring it, but the item itself is already finished and safe. A roll with no successes, or a Complication, ends refining, and the Complication costs you one upgrade gained by refining. You can stop after any roll and keep what you've gained.",""],["Rules","Upgrade list","Crafting › Upgrades","crafting.html#upgrade-list","The GM can approve others that fit the item. Item Upgrades Advanced upgrades Weapon Add Quickdraw, Discreet, Throwable or Precise Add Deadly or Piercing 1; +1 damage (once) Armor +2 Wear boxes; remove its Stealth and Athletics penalty +1 rating (once) Kit +1 charge Masterwork: +2d instead of +1d Device +1 charge; +1 to its range or area +1 rating (once) Any Fine make: +1d to Socialize rolls where it's seen Sturdy: it only breaks through deliberate effort, and repairing it takes half the usual target Weapon traits are defined in Core Rules.",""],["Rules","Repair","Crafting › Repair","crafting.html#repair","Repairing uses the same rules, including the attempt limit, at the interval for the item. Lost armor rating: target 3 for each point of rating restored. Broken gear: half the item's crafting target, rounded up. Materials: a quarter of the item's price, or salvage. Repair is how armor regains lost rating and how broken gear works again (see Armor and Rest).",""],["Rules","Lost armor rating","Crafting › Repair","crafting.html#repair","Lost armor rating: target 3 for each point of rating restored. ",""],["Rules","Broken gear","Crafting › Repair","crafting.html#repair","Broken gear: half the item's crafting target, rounded up. ",""],["Rules","Materials","Crafting › Repair","crafting.html#repair","Materials: a quarter of the item's price, or salvage. ",""],["Rules","Devices","Crafting › Devices","crafting.html#devices","A Device is a self-operating mechanism: a trap, an alarm, a launcher, an automaton. Once it's set or started, it works on its own, so the user's skill doesn't matter. Weapons, armor and tools whose use depends on the wielder's skill aren't Devices: make them as ordinary items and improve them with upgrades. What powers a Device depends on the setting: clockwork, alchemy, runes, lightning, or something stranger. Each Device has a rating from 1 to 5 and a single function. Rolling: when a Device makes a roll, it rolls twice its rating in dice, like an NPC rolling something it's proficient in (see NPCs). Setting it or starting it takes 1 action unless it says otherwise. Charges: a Device has charges equal to its rating. Each use spends 1. Each Prepare at a rest refills 1 charge, like a kit. Who can build one: Devices are complex work. You need Crafts 3+ and Technology 2+ to build one, and its rating can't be higher than your Crafts dots − 2 (rating 1 at Crafts 3, 2 at Crafts 4, 3 at Crafts 5). Crafting: a rating 1–2 Device is Complex (target 10); rating 3 or more is Masterwork (target 15). Each Device also needs a core component the setting defines, such as a power source or a focus. W",""],["Rules","Rolling","Crafting › Devices","crafting.html#devices","Rolling: when a Device makes a roll, it rolls twice its rating in dice, like an NPC rolling something it's proficient in (see NPCs). Setting it or starting it takes 1 action unless it says otherwise. ",""],["Rules","Charges","Crafting › Devices","crafting.html#devices","Charges: a Device has charges equal to its rating. Each use spends 1. Each Prepare at a rest refills 1 charge, like a kit. ",""],["Rules","Who can build one","Crafting › Devices","crafting.html#devices","Who can build one: Devices are complex work. You need Crafts 3+ and Technology 2+ to build one, and its rating can't be higher than your Crafts dots − 2 (rating 1 at Crafts 3, 2 at Crafts 4, 3 at Crafts 5). ",""],["Rules","Crafting","Crafting › Devices","crafting.html#devices","Crafting: a rating 1–2 Device is Complex (target 10); rating 3 or more is Masterwork (target 15). Each Device also needs a core component the setting defines, such as a power source or a focus. Without one, the Device can't be built. ",""],["Rules","Disabling or bypassing","Crafting › Devices","crafting.html#devices","Disabling or bypassing: roll Dexterity or Intelligence + Technology, contested by the Device's roll. On a success, it's disarmed, jammed or slipped past; on a failure, it triggers or alerts its owner, as fits the Device. ",""],["Rules","Settings","Crafting › Devices","crafting.html#devices","Settings add their own Devices, and decide what core components are and where to find them. Higher-rated Devices can also be found, bought or salvaged. ",""],["Rules","Common Devices","Crafting › Devices","crafting.html#common-devices","Device Function Grapnel launcher Fires an anchored line up to 5 meters per rating. Climbing the line needs no roll. Smoke canister Set it off, or rig it to a tripwire: it fills a small area with smoke, which is Obscured for one minute. Flash mine Set it off, or rig it to a tripwire. It rolls against the Dexterity + Stamina of everyone in a small area; each one it beats is Disoriented and takes −2d on sight-based rolls until the end of their next turn. Sentry crossbow Set it to watch a doorway or a path. Once per round, it attacks the first creature to enter, rolling its dice, for 2 lethal damage. Each shot spends a charge. Alarm ward Set it over a doorway or a path. It sounds when anyone passes; noticing and avoiding it takes a Stealth roll against the Device's roll. Lock breaker Clamp it to a lock. Over one minute, it opens the lock using its roll in place of Dexterity + Thievery.",""],["Rules","Milestones","Advancement › Milestones","advancement.html#milestones","The GM calls a milestone when a story arc closes or at a major turning point, usually once per session in a short story and every one or two sessions in a longer one. Every character advances at the same time. At each milestone, every character: Gains 1 talent. Makes 2 choices from the advancement pool.",""],["Rules","Gains 1 talent","Advancement › Milestones","advancement.html#milestones","Gains 1 talent. ",""],["Rules","Makes 2 choices","Advancement › Milestones","advancement.html#milestones","Makes 2 choices from the advancement pool. ",""],["Rules","The talent","Advancement › Milestones","advancement.html#the-talent","Each milestone gives one of: a talent from any vocation you have (its after tag and other prerequisites must be met), a new vocation's key talent, after which its trees are open to you, a further community trait from any community you belong to (its prerequisites must be met), or a new community's key trait (see Joining another community). The GM and player should find an in-world reason for it.",""],["Rules","The advancement pool","Advancement › Milestones","advancement.html#the-advancement-pool","Each choice marks one box on an option. Option Boxes You gain Skills ☐ ☐ ☐ ☐ 2 skill dots in different skills rated below 3, or 1 dot in a skill rated 3 or higher Specialties ☐ ☐ 1 specialty, or 1 improvement to a specialty you have Training ☐ ☐ Another talent (as above) Toughness ☐ +1 Health Nerve ☐ +1 Stress box Attribute [☐ ☐ ☐] [☐ ☐ ☐] +1 attribute dot Single boxes (☐) give their benefit as soon as you mark them. Chained boxes ([☐ ☐ ☐]) give their benefit only when the last box of the chain is marked. You can spread a chain across milestones: for example, mark two attribute boxes at one milestone and the third at the next. Full options: once every box of an option is marked, you can't choose it again this cycle. New cycle: the pool has 16 boxes, but a cycle is only 10 choices (5 milestones). After your 10th choice, clear the pool and start again. You'll never fill everything in one cycle, so each cycle you decide what to focus on. An unfinished chain keeps its marked boxes into the next cycle.",""],["Rules","Single boxes","Advancement › The advancement pool","advancement.html#the-advancement-pool","Single boxes (☐) give their benefit as soon as you mark them. ",""],["Rules","Chained boxes","Advancement › The advancement pool","advancement.html#the-advancement-pool","Chained boxes ([☐ ☐ ☐]) give their benefit only when the last box of the chain is marked. You can spread a chain across milestones: for example, mark two attribute boxes at one milestone and the third at the next. ",""],["Rules","Full options","Advancement › The advancement pool","advancement.html#the-advancement-pool","Full options: once every box of an option is marked, you can't choose it again this cycle. ",""],["Rules","New cycle","Advancement › The advancement pool","advancement.html#the-advancement-pool","New cycle: the pool has 16 boxes, but a cycle is only 10 choices (5 milestones). After your 10th choice, clear the pool and start again. You'll never fill everything in one cycle, so each cycle you decide what to focus on. An unfinished chain keeps its marked boxes into the next cycle. ",""],["Rules","Limits","Advancement › Milestones","advancement.html#limits","Skills: the creation cap of 3 no longer applies; skills can reach 5. Specialties: each improvement adds +1d, up to +3d and no higher than the skill's permanent dots (see Specialties). Attributes: no attribute can go above 5. Tiers stay informative only. For example, each 5-milestone cycle could mark a new tier.",""],["Rules","Skills","Advancement › Limits","advancement.html#limits","Skills: the creation cap of 3 no longer applies; skills can reach 5. ",""],["Rules","Specialties","Advancement › Limits","advancement.html#limits","Specialties: each improvement adds +1d, up to +3d and no higher than the skill's permanent dots (see Specialties). ",""],["Rules","Attributes","Advancement › Limits","advancement.html#limits","Attributes: no attribute can go above 5. ",""],["Rules","Weeks of downtime","Downtime › Weeks of downtime","downtime.html#weeks-of-downtime","Downtime is counted in weeks. The GM decides how many weeks the party has before the next adventure starts, or before something interrupts them. A week of downtime includes its nights' rests; don't run them separately. Each week, every character takes two downtime actions, then the week ends. Repeating actions: you can take the same action more than once in a week, but each character can receive Treat only once per week. Teamwork works on downtime rolls as usual. No Stress: downtime rolls span days of steady work, not a burst of effort. You can't push yourself on them or pay any other Stress cost on them, including talents and racial traits. Complications in downtime can follow the characters into the next adventure: a rival, a debt, a rumor, an unhappy client.",""],["Rules","Repeating actions","Downtime › Weeks of downtime","downtime.html#weeks-of-downtime","Repeating actions: you can take the same action more than once in a week, but each character can receive Treat only once per week. ",""],["Rules","Teamwork","Downtime › Weeks of downtime","downtime.html#weeks-of-downtime","Teamwork works on downtime rolls as usual. ",""],["Rules","No Stress","Downtime › Weeks of downtime","downtime.html#weeks-of-downtime","No Stress: downtime rolls span days of steady work, not a burst of effort. You can't push yourself on them or pay any other Stress cost on them, including talents and racial traits. ",""],["Rules","Complications","Downtime › Weeks of downtime","downtime.html#weeks-of-downtime","Complications in downtime can follow the characters into the next adventure: a rival, a debt, a rumor, an unhappy client. ",""],["Rules","Lifestyle","Downtime › Weeks of downtime","downtime.html#lifestyle","Each week, every character chooses how they live and pays its upkeep. A character with a home in the settlement (their community lives there, or a patron houses them) lives Modestly for free, and pays only the difference to live better. Lifestyle Upkeep per week Effect Rough Free A stable loft, a doorway, a friend's floor. The week clears only 1 Stress, and refills only 1 charge on each kit and Device. Modest 1 handful A boarding house or a cheap inn. The week ends as normal. Comfortable 3 handfuls A good inn or rented rooms, with quiet and space to work. +1d to Project rolls. If it's your last week of downtime, you start the next adventure Determined. Lavish 1 bag A fine house, servants, the best tables in town. Everything Comfortable gives, and +1 downtime action that week: servants and hired hands take care of what would otherwise eat your days. You can also pay 1 more bag for one more extra action that week, but no more: money can hire help, but it can't add days to the week. +1d to Cultivate and Gather information among the wealthy and powerful.",""],["Rules","End of the week","Downtime › Weeks of downtime","downtime.html#end-of-the-week","At the end of each week of downtime, every character: clears all Stress (only 1 when living Rough), heals all bashing and lethal damage and 1 aggravated wound (natural healing), removes all stacks of Exhausted, gets everything Prepare gives, and more: consumables restocked, all armor Wear cleared, and every kit and Device refilled to full charges, refreshes their once-per-day abilities. Lost armor rating, broken gear and defects aren't fixed on their own: they need a Project (below).",""],["Rules","Downtime actions","Downtime › Downtime actions","downtime.html#downtime-actions","Treat: Roll Intelligence + Medicine on a patient, giving them long-term care. On a success, they heal 1 aggravated wound. On a success with Opportunity, they heal 2 aggravated wounds instead. A medical kit gives +1d as usual. You can treat yourself. Recuperate: Roll Resolve + Composure as you let go of what you've been through. On a success, heal 1 Tenacity. On a success with Opportunity, heal 2 Tenacity instead. This is the only way Tenacity heals (see Tenacity and madness). It never reduces a Scar, including Dusking. Project: Work on an extended action: crafting or repairing an item, research in an archive, forging papers, training an animal. Each Project action gives rolls according to the task's interval (below). All the extended action's rules apply, including crafting's attempt limit, except one: a roll with no successes doesn't cost Stress. It ends this action's rolls instead, and you can pick the work up again with a later Project action. Work: Roll an Attribute + Skill that fits a trade you can ply: Crafts for selling goods, Expression for performing, Medicine for tending the sick, Streetwise for running errands no one asks about. Each success earns 1 handful. On a success",""],["Rules","Treat","Downtime › Downtime actions","downtime.html#downtime-actions","Treat: Roll Intelligence + Medicine on a patient, giving them long-term care. On a success, they heal 1 aggravated wound. On a success with Opportunity, they heal 2 aggravated wounds instead. A medical kit gives +1d as usual. You can treat yourself. ",""],["Rules","Recuperate","Downtime › Downtime actions","downtime.html#downtime-actions","Recuperate: Roll Resolve + Composure as you let go of what you've been through. On a success, heal 1 Tenacity. On a success with Opportunity, heal 2 Tenacity instead. This is the only way Tenacity heals (see Tenacity and madness). It never reduces a Scar, including Dusking. ",""],["Rules","Project","Downtime › Downtime actions","downtime.html#downtime-actions","Project: Work on an extended action: crafting or repairing an item, research in an archive, forging papers, training an animal. Each Project action gives rolls according to the task's interval (below). All the extended action's rules apply, including crafting's attempt limit, except one: a roll with no successes doesn't cost Stress. It ends this action's rolls instead, and you can pick the work up again with a later Project action. ",""],["Rules","Work","Downtime › Downtime actions","downtime.html#downtime-actions","Work: Roll an Attribute + Skill that fits a trade you can ply: Crafts for selling goods, Expression for performing, Medicine for tending the sick, Streetwise for running errands no one asks about. Each success earns 1 handful. On a success with Opportunity, you also make a useful contact or hear a lead, at the GM's call. ",""],["Rules","Gather information","Downtime › Downtime actions","downtime.html#downtime-actions","Gather information: Roll Wits or Intelligence + Investigation, Academics, Streetwise or Socialize, whichever fits how you ask around. On a success, the GM answers one question about a person, place, group or threat truthfully. On a success with Opportunity, also gain an edge: +1d on your first roll that acts on what you learned. ",""],["Rules","Cultivate","Downtime › Downtime actions","downtime.html#downtime-actions","Cultivate: Roll Manipulation or Presence + Socialize, Persuasion or Streetwise, spending time with a person or group: a patron, a guild, your community. On a success, gain 1 favor with them. On a success with Opportunity, gain 2 favors, or 1 favor and a lasting contact. ",""],["Rules","Project intervals","Downtime › Downtime actions","downtime.html#project-intervals","Task's interval Rolls per Project action Hour As many as the task needs Day Up to 3 Week 1",""],["Rules","Favors","Downtime › Downtime actions","downtime.html#favors","A favor is something a person or group owes you. Spend it, with the GM's approval, for one piece of help they can reasonably give: an introduction, a loan of gear, a door opened, a blind eye turned, a night's shelter on the run. A favor doesn't make anyone act against their own interests. Favors last until they're spent, unless the story breaks the relationship.",""],["Rules","Rolling","Cheat Sheet › Rolling","cheatsheet.html#rolling","Pool: Attribute + Skill in d6. Each 5–6 is a success. 5+ successes is a success with Opportunity. Untrained: −1d Physical or Social, −3d Mental. Chance die: a pool of 0 or less rolls one die. 6 succeeds; 1 is a Complication. Contested: more successes wins; ties go to the side that acts. Net successes = the margin. Fixed difficulty: 2 successes is hard, 3 is very hard. Teamwork: helper rolls first; 1 success gives +1d, Opportunity +2d, at most +2d in total. Their Complication carries over. Group action: everyone doing the same thing rolls; the best roll counts for all. The leader takes 1 Stress per failed roll. Stakes die (raised stakes) Result 5–6 Opportunity, even on a failed roll (no success) 3–4 Nothing 1–2 Complication, which also counts as a success The GM raises the stakes when a roll could change the situation in a big way. Each player can raise them on their own roll once per day. See Rolls and results.",""],["Rules","Pool","Cheat Sheet › Rolling","cheatsheet.html#rolling","Pool: Attribute + Skill in d6. Each 5–6 is a success. 5+ successes is a success with Opportunity. ",""],["Rules","Untrained","Cheat Sheet › Rolling","cheatsheet.html#rolling","Untrained: −1d Physical or Social, −3d Mental. ",""],["Rules","Chance die","Cheat Sheet › Rolling","cheatsheet.html#rolling","Chance die: a pool of 0 or less rolls one die. 6 succeeds; 1 is a Complication. ",""],["Rules","Contested","Cheat Sheet › Rolling","cheatsheet.html#rolling","Contested: more successes wins; ties go to the side that acts. Net successes = the margin. ",""],["Rules","Fixed difficulty","Cheat Sheet › Rolling","cheatsheet.html#rolling","Fixed difficulty: 2 successes is hard, 3 is very hard. ",""],["Rules","Teamwork","Cheat Sheet › Rolling","cheatsheet.html#rolling","Teamwork: helper rolls first; 1 success gives +1d, Opportunity +2d, at most +2d in total. Their Complication carries over. ",""],["Rules","Group action","Cheat Sheet › Rolling","cheatsheet.html#rolling","Group action: everyone doing the same thing rolls; the best roll counts for all. The leader takes 1 Stress per failed roll. ",""],["Rules","Modifiers","Cheat Sheet › Modifiers","cheatsheet.html#modifiers","Situation Modifier Dim light / darkness / total darkness −1d / −3d / chance die Cover (against ranged) −1d; full cover can't be targeted Obscured −1d to see or target you Prone Melee against you +1d, ranged −1d Difficult terrain −1d to Physical rolls (attacks and defense too), half Speed Push yourself +2d for 2 Stress Specialty +1d to +3d (one per roll) Set up +1d on your next roll against that target Kit / no tools +1d (spends a charge) / −1d Beyond weapon range −2d (up to double the range) Heavy weapon without Strength 3 −1d per missing dot Exhausted −1d per stack See Modifiers.",""],["Rules","Turns and actions","Cheat Sheet › Turns and actions","cheatsheet.html#turns-and-actions","Round: about 6 seconds. Order: fast PCs (2 actions), fast NPCs, slow PCs (3 actions), slow NPCs. Players choose each round. One reaction per round. Take 2 Stress for an extra reaction, as many times as you can pay. One attack per turn; extra attacks take a cumulative −2d. Action Cost Effect Attack 1 Once per turn Move 1 Up to your Speed; can repeat Disengage 1 Move 1 m without Reactive Strikes Set up 1 +1d on your next roll against the target Brace 1 +1d to defense rolls while in cover Interact / Stand up 1 Draw a weapon, open a door / stand from Prone Grapple 1 Counts as your attack First aid 2 Dexterity + Medicine, 1 success: stop Bleeding Catch your breath 2 Composure, clear 1 Stress per success; once per day Ranges: adjacent 1 m · close 5 m · near 10 m · far beyond. A small area is 3 × 3 m. See Turns and actions.",""],["Rules","Round","Cheat Sheet › Turns and actions","cheatsheet.html#turns-and-actions","Round: about 6 seconds. Order: fast PCs (2 actions), fast NPCs, slow PCs (3 actions), slow NPCs. Players choose each round. ",""],["Rules","One reaction","Cheat Sheet › Turns and actions","cheatsheet.html#turns-and-actions","One reaction per round. Take 2 Stress for an extra reaction, as many times as you can pay. ",""],["Rules","One attack","Cheat Sheet › Turns and actions","cheatsheet.html#turns-and-actions","One attack per turn; extra attacks take a cumulative −2d. ",""],["Rules","Attacks and defense","Cheat Sheet › Attacks and defense","cheatsheet.html#attacks-and-defense","Attack Pool Melee Strength + Weaponry Unarmed Strength + Brawl Ranged or thrown Dexterity + Ranged Hit: your successes equal or beat the defender's. Damage = net successes + weapon damage. A tie hits for the weapon's damage alone. Armor: subtract its rating; if it absorbs everything, still 1 bashing. A hit that gets past it, or an Opportunity, adds 1 Wear. Graze: on a miss, take 1 Stress to deal 1 damage one severity lower (not with bashing attacks). Defense (reaction) Roll Dodge 2 × Defense, with the stakes die. Spends your Defense until your next turn. Complication: Prone. Opportunity: free Disengage. Parry (melee) Defense + Weaponry, or + Brawl unarmed (−2d against an armed attack) No reaction Roll your Defense Endure Stamina + Resolve (or the threat's pool) against harm that isn't an attack. Free outside combat; in combat, no reaction means no roll. Grapple: start it with Strength + Brawl as an attack. Then, once a round, everyone in it rolls Strength + Brawl at the same time; the winner makes one move (two on an Opportunity), and a tie changes nothing. Defense = the lower of Dexterity and Wits. Attacks contested by Intelligence + Resolve can't be Dodged or Parried and cost no ",""],["Rules","Hit","Cheat Sheet › Attacks and defense","cheatsheet.html#attacks-and-defense","Hit: your successes equal or beat the defender's. Damage = net successes + weapon damage. A tie hits for the weapon's damage alone. ",""],["Rules","Armor","Cheat Sheet › Attacks and defense","cheatsheet.html#attacks-and-defense","Armor: subtract its rating; if it absorbs everything, still 1 bashing. A hit that gets past it, or an Opportunity, adds 1 Wear. ",""],["Rules","Graze","Cheat Sheet › Attacks and defense","cheatsheet.html#attacks-and-defense","Graze: on a miss, take 1 Stress to deal 1 damage one severity lower (not with bashing attacks). ",""],["Rules","Contested pools","Cheat Sheet › Contested pools","cheatsheet.html#contested-pools","Against Contested by Feints, tricks, lies, mental attacks, commands Intelligence + Resolve Pressure, taunts, intimidation, fear Wits + Composure Physical effects that aren't attacks (shoves, traps) Dexterity + Stamina Noticing something hidden Wits + Composure Persistent damage, poison, hazards Endure (Stamina + Resolve)",""],["Rules","Damage and Health","Cheat Sheet › Damage and Health","cheatsheet.html#damage-and-health","Health: Size + Stamina. Fills aggravated → lethal → bashing, left to right. A full track upgrades new damage (bashing → lethal → aggravated). Beaten Down: total damage above Stamina + Resolve. Attacking costs 1 Stress unless you can't surrender; surrendering clears 1 Stress. Last box: bashing knocks you Unconscious; lethal also makes you Bleed. Avoid Death: take a Death's Mark, stop Bleeding, stay down until healed or a rest. Blaze of Glory: one last turn where every roll counts as a success with Opportunity, then death. Persistent damage lands at the end of your turn; Endure (successes ≥ rounds left) to skip it this round. Healing Bashing Lethal Aggravated Natural 1 per hour 1 per rest 1 per week of downtime Rest All 1 (+ Recover: 1, or 2 on an Opportunity) — See Damage and Health.",""],["Rules","Health","Cheat Sheet › Damage and Health","cheatsheet.html#damage-and-health","Health: Size + Stamina. Fills aggravated → lethal → bashing, left to right. A full track upgrades new damage (bashing → lethal → aggravated). ",""],["Rules","Beaten Down","Cheat Sheet › Damage and Health","cheatsheet.html#damage-and-health","Beaten Down: total damage above Stamina + Resolve. Attacking costs 1 Stress unless you can't surrender; surrendering clears 1 Stress. ",""],["Rules","Last box","Cheat Sheet › Damage and Health","cheatsheet.html#damage-and-health","Last box: bashing knocks you Unconscious; lethal also makes you Bleed. Avoid Death: take a Death's Mark, stop Bleeding, stay down until healed or a rest. Blaze of Glory: one last turn where every roll counts as a success with Opportunity, then death. ",""],["Rules","Persistent damage","Cheat Sheet › Damage and Health","cheatsheet.html#damage-and-health","Persistent damage lands at the end of your turn; Endure (successes ≥ rounds left) to skip it this round. ",""],["Rules","Stress and madness","Cheat Sheet › Stress and madness","cheatsheet.html#stress-and-madness","Stress track: current Tenacity + 2. Tenacity: Resolve + Composure + 3. Spend Stress: push yourself (+2d for 2), an extra reaction (2), a graze (1), breaking a compulsion (2). Overflow: Stress that doesn't fit becomes Tenacity damage → madness check → clear half your Stress. Madness check: after any Tenacity damage, Endure against madness: roll your remaining Tenacity in dice, needing successes equal to the damage. Fail: a Bout of Madness. Bout of Madness: 3d6 − missing Tenacity (− Scar penalties). 0 or less is death; 1–4 is crippling; 5–11 is violence, panic or confusion; 12–14 Rage; 15+ is a boon. Clearing Stress: 1 at each rest (+ Respite), Catch your breath, downtime, surrendering while Beaten Down. See Stress and Tenacity and madness.",""],["Rules","Stress track","Cheat Sheet › Stress and madness","cheatsheet.html#stress-and-madness","Stress track: current Tenacity + 2. Tenacity: Resolve + Composure + 3. ",""],["Rules","Spend Stress","Cheat Sheet › Stress and madness","cheatsheet.html#stress-and-madness","Spend Stress: push yourself (+2d for 2), an extra reaction (2), a graze (1), breaking a compulsion (2). ",""],["Rules","Overflow","Cheat Sheet › Stress and madness","cheatsheet.html#stress-and-madness","Overflow: Stress that doesn't fit becomes Tenacity damage → madness check → clear half your Stress. ",""],["Rules","Madness check","Cheat Sheet › Stress and madness","cheatsheet.html#stress-and-madness","Madness check: after any Tenacity damage, Endure against madness: roll your remaining Tenacity in dice, needing successes equal to the damage. Fail: a Bout of Madness. ",""],["Rules","Bout of Madness","Cheat Sheet › Stress and madness","cheatsheet.html#stress-and-madness","Bout of Madness: 3d6 − missing Tenacity (− Scar penalties). 0 or less is death; 1–4 is crippling; 5–11 is violence, panic or confusion; 12–14 Rage; 15+ is a boon. ",""],["Rules","Clearing Stress","Cheat Sheet › Stress and madness","cheatsheet.html#stress-and-madness","Clearing Stress: 1 at each rest (+ Respite), Catch your breath, downtime, surrendering while Beaten Down. ",""],["Rules","NPCs","Cheat Sheet › NPCs","cheatsheet.html#npcs","Minion Important Boss Rolls 2 × rating proficient, rating if known, chance die otherwise Same Same Health None: any lethal takes them out; bashing only if one hit ≥ rating Size + rating Usually double Stress None (can't pay costs) 2 × rating 4 × rating Defense Roll rating Dodge and Parry like PCs Like PCs Armor Static, no Wear Wear Wear Beaten Down when damage exceeds half their Health. See NPCs.",""],["Rules","Extended actions","Cheat Sheet › Extended actions","cheatsheet.html#extended-actions","Task Target Quick 3 Standard 6 Long 10 Major 15 Each roll adds its successes. No successes: take 1 Stress to press on, or it fails. A Complication loses a success or costs time; an Opportunity saves time or materials, never adds successes. See Extended actions.",""],["Rules","Compulsion and possession","Cheat Sheet › Compulsion and possession","cheatsheet.html#compulsion-and-possession","Compulsion: contested; one short command; broken by harm or a self-harming order; the target can take 2 Stress to act freely for a turn but is Stunned; afterward, they know. Possession: the possessor acts through the body with their own actions, paying 1 Stress per round they move it. Body's Strength, Dexterity and Stamina, possessor's everything else. Harm falls on the victim and doesn't break it. The victim can take 2 Stress on its turn to contest again. See Compulsion.",""],["Rules","Compulsion","Cheat Sheet › Compulsion and possession","cheatsheet.html#compulsion-and-possession","Compulsion: contested; one short command; broken by harm or a self-harming order; the target can take 2 Stress to act freely for a turn but is Stunned; afterward, they know. ",""],["Rules","Possession","Cheat Sheet › Compulsion and possession","cheatsheet.html#compulsion-and-possession","Possession: the possessor acts through the body with their own actions, paying 1 Stress per round they move it. Body's Strength, Dexterity and Stamina, possessor's everything else. Harm falls on the victim and doesn't break it. The victim can take 2 Stress on its turn to contest again. ",""],["Rules","Conditions","Cheat Sheet › Conditions","cheatsheet.html#conditions","Condition In short Beaten Down Attacking costs 1 Stress; surrender clears 1 Bleeding 1 lethal at the end of each turn until treated Blinded Sight rolls use a chance die; −3d against what you hear; no Dodge, −2d other defense Deafened Hearing rolls use a chance die; −1d to notice Determined Next Stress cost −1 Disoriented No reactions Exhausted −1d per stack; collapse at your Beaten Down threshold Frightened −1d, or −2d against the source; 1 Stress to move closer Grappled No Move, Dodge or Disengage; Light weapons or Grapple only Immobilized Can't move Inspired Reroll all failed dice once, then it ends Obscured −1d to see or target you; can hide Prone Melee +1d, ranged −1d against you; 1 action to stand Slowed Half Speed Stunned Two fewer actions, no reaction Surprised Slow turn, no reaction Unconscious Can't act or react See Conditions.",""],["Rules","Rest","Cheat Sheet › Rest","cheatsheet.html#rest","At the end of a rest: clear 1 Stress, heal all bashing and 1 lethal, remove 1 Exhausted, refresh once-per-day abilities. Each character takes two rest actions: Recover, Prepare, Respite, Forage or Scout (2 handfuls each for extras in a settlement). See Rest.",""],["Rules","Setting rules","Cheat Sheet › Setting rules","cheatsheet.html#setting-rules","The quick rules of the selected setting appear here.",""],["Setting","Duskworld primer","Duskworld","settings/duskworld/index.html","Long ago, humans and kehars shared Eladria in uneasy peace, until a handful of kehars reached for power the world couldn't give them and something answered. The krens came. What began as gifts became corruption, and for a hundred years, remembered as the Era of Sorrow, Eladria belonged to them: people enslaved, hunted, sacrificed, while kehars fled to the fringes and hid. Then the Allmother rose out of the last of humanity's hope and drove the krens back far enough to raise Dawnkeep, the first Free City. Her own First Followers murdered her for more power than she'd give them, an act so vile it left them wandering shells of themselves, still out there somewhere. In her place, the Dusk came. The Dusk The Dusk isn't simple darkness. It's corruption that spreads through reality itself. Wherever it reaches, color drains out, the land withers, and magic and runetech turn unpredictable: a ward flickers, a silo spoils its grain, a machine runs harder, weaker, or does something its makers never meant. Stay in it too long and it changes you, if it doesn't kill you first. Plants grow strange and enormous, animals turn monstrous, people lose pieces of their mind, or themselves. The ruined lan","duskworld"],["Setting","History","Duskworld › History","settings/duskworld/overview.html#history","Once, the world was full of light and color. Then Eladria succumbed to the Dusk and the grey. Long ago, humans and kehars lived throughout Eladria, mostly in peace. But some kehars sought powers beyond what the world could offer, and strange beings from the unknown answered them: the krens. Their gifts became corruption, and the krens spread across the world, turning Eladria into a realm of demons and monsters. During the Era of Sorrow, the people of Eladria were enslaved and hunted. Humans were gathered into camps and prisons and sacrificed in dark rituals. Kehars fled to the fringes of civilization and hid from their new masters. When all seemed lost, the Light appeared. The Allmother manifested from the hopes and prayers of the people and drove back the krens. The survivors pushed the krens far enough away to found Dawnkeep, the first Free City. The victory didn't last. The Allmother's First Followers, unsatisfied with the power she had given them, murdered her. The act stripped them of what remained of their humanity, and they still wander somewhere as shells of their former selves. One of them, the Mad Apostle, lost their mind after the Allmother's death and ruled the Keshi fr","duskworld"],["Setting","The Dusk","Duskworld › The Dusk","settings/duskworld/overview.html#the-dusk","The Dusk isn't simply darkness. It corrupts reality itself. Where it spreads, the world loses its color. Light dims, the land withers, and magic and runetech turn unpredictable: wards flicker, silos spoil their grain, and machines run harder, weaker, or do things their makers never meant. It twists living things. Plants mutate, animals become monstrous, and people exposed to it for too long may lose their minds or their humanity. Few understand what the Dusk is, or why it came with the Allmother's death.","duskworld"],["Setting","The Dusklands","Duskworld › The Dusk","settings/duskworld/overview.html#the-dusklands","The lands touched by the Dusk. The ground is dark grey, the vegetation black and twisted, and everything that survives has changed. Plants may grow enormous, grow strange materials in place of wood, or gain rudimentary sentience. Dusktouched: animals and people changed by the Dusk's corruption. Krens and their creations still roam the wilds. Duskborn: powerful horrors born from the Dusk itself. Some people live in the Dusklands, surviving alongside the monsters, protected from corruption by magic and sacrifice. They're called Duskroamers.","duskworld"],["Setting","Dusktouched","Duskworld › The Dusklands","settings/duskworld/overview.html#the-dusklands","Dusktouched: animals and people changed by the Dusk's corruption. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Krens","Duskworld › The Dusklands","settings/duskworld/overview.html#the-dusklands","Krens and their creations still roam the wilds. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Duskborn","Duskworld › The Dusklands","settings/duskworld/overview.html#the-dusklands","Duskborn: powerful horrors born from the Dusk itself. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Mendris","Duskworld › Mendris","settings/duskworld/overview.html#mendris","One of the last great bastions of civilization: the capital Dawnkeep and the fortified farmsteads around it, the Frontiers. The Great Houses hold the real power. Beacons: massive towers that combine the Allmother's Light with ancient kren technology. They keep the Dusk away from Dawnkeep and the farmsteads. The Prime Beacon was built by the Allmother herself; every other Beacon was built with her light, given through House Callarain, and House Shantari's runetech. Rigid society: social status is hard to change. Farmsteaders stay farmsteaders and cityfolk stay cityfolk. In Dawnkeep, your Sector decides much of your standing, your work and your circles. Controlled travel: movement between regions is tightly controlled, for order and for protection from what lies beyond the Beacons.","duskworld"],["Setting","Beacons","Duskworld › Mendris","settings/duskworld/overview.html#mendris","Beacons: massive towers that combine the Allmother's Light with ancient kren technology. They keep the Dusk away from Dawnkeep and the farmsteads. The Prime Beacon was built by the Allmother herself; every other Beacon was built with her light, given through House Callarain, and House Shantari's runetech. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Rigid society","Duskworld › Mendris","settings/duskworld/overview.html#mendris","Rigid society: social status is hard to change. Farmsteaders stay farmsteaders and cityfolk stay cityfolk. In Dawnkeep, your Sector decides much of your standing, your work and your circles. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Controlled travel","Duskworld › Mendris","settings/duskworld/overview.html#mendris","Controlled travel: movement between regions is tightly controlled, for order and for protection from what lies beyond the Beacons. ","duskworld"],["Setting","The Frontiers","Duskworld › Mendris","settings/duskworld/overview.html#the-frontiers","Simple, communal and dangerous. Most people work the fields or serve in the security forces that protect the settlements. Others keep the farmsteads running: runesmiths, wardwrights, artisans, hunters, and engineers who maintain the Beacons, machinery and grain silos. The silos use runetech to preserve and purify food, so the Frontiers are largely self-sufficient. Humans and kehars in the Frontiers lead similar lives. After generations of shared hardship, kehars are usually treated like their human neighbors, though old prejudices remain.","duskworld"],["Setting","Dawnkeep","Duskworld › Mendris","settings/duskworld/overview.html#dawnkeep","Broader, wealthier and more complicated. A city of rivers, canals, factories, markets, trains, Beacons and ancient technology, divided into Sectors with their own character and hierarchy. Most residents are laborers or artisans. Those born in prominent Sectors have better access to administrative, clerical, logistical and governmental positions. With wealth, connections or the right family name, people rise further: merchants who travel across Mendris, shareholders in trading companies, members of the institutions that control the city. The world is dying, but Dawnkeep is still alive.","duskworld"],["Setting","The Sectors","Duskworld › Mendris","settings/duskworld/overview.html#the-sectors","Three rivers meet in Dawnkeep, the Vern, the Loron and the Tamaric, and they divide it into four Sectors. The Midlands, between the Vern and the Loron: the most prominent Sector. The Hearing House, where the Great Council gathers, stands here. Lightport, in the east up to the rivers' confluence, is closed to all but those who live or work there, or are invited. It holds the Inner Council and the headquarters of the IEE. The Caliethan District is home to the Caliethan University and to SPEAR. The merchant district is a hub for the finest merchants and artisans, who trade directly with the elite. Its canals reach the rest of the Midlands, and its railways the whole of Mendris. Northbank, north of the Vern: the second most prominent Sector, of comfortable homes and stores, with farms along its northern edge. The Fork, where the Vern and Tamaric meet: a hub for trade and visitors, and a rough place of tenements, workhouses and gang territory, where parish churches do more good than the authorities. Smaller supporting Beacons hold it against the Dusk. Shurnen's Bend, its northern part, is a market haven. The Wastes, in the basin at its heart, are the poorest and most dangerous district ","duskworld"],["Setting","The Midlands,","Duskworld › The Sectors","settings/duskworld/overview.html#the-sectors","The Midlands, between the Vern and the Loron: the most prominent Sector. The Hearing House, where the Great Council gathers, stands here. Lightport, in the east up to the rivers' confluence, is closed to all but those who live or work there, or are invited. It holds the Inner Council and the headquarters of the IEE. The Caliethan District is home to the Caliethan University and to SPEAR. The merchant district is a hub for the finest merchants and artisans, who trade directly with the elite. Its canals reach the rest of the Midlands, and its railways the whole of Mendris.","duskworld"],["Setting","Northbank,","Duskworld › The Sectors","settings/duskworld/overview.html#the-sectors","Northbank, north of the Vern: the second most prominent Sector, of comfortable homes and stores, with farms along its northern edge. ","duskworld"],["Setting","The Fork,","Duskworld › The Sectors","settings/duskworld/overview.html#the-sectors","The Fork, where the Vern and Tamaric meet: a hub for trade and visitors, and a rough place of tenements, workhouses and gang territory, where parish churches do more good than the authorities. Smaller supporting Beacons hold it against the Dusk. Shurnen's Bend, its northern part, is a market haven. The Wastes, in the basin at its heart, are the poorest and most dangerous district of Dawnkeep: polluted, flooded often, and lost in a maze of canals. Outsiders aren't welcome.","duskworld"],["Setting","Southbank,","Duskworld › The Sectors","settings/duskworld/overview.html#the-sectors","Southbank, south of the Loron: Dawnkeep's docklands and factories, home of the Red Crow News Office. Trade, smuggling, and the place goods (sometimes whole convoys) go missing before anyone official notices. The Mackard District, on the Loron's bank, holds most of the great factories, among them Morris and Denker's. Its workers live here, and its dumps are what made the Wastes. Ovoron, west of Mackard, is a residential district of twisting canals, homes and local stores: the best of Southbank.","duskworld"],["Setting","Travel papers","Duskworld › Mendris","settings/duskworld/overview.html#travel-papers","Movement between regions of Mendris is controlled, and travel papers say where you may go. Most people only hold papers for their own region: Frontier folk for the Frontiers, guildfolk for the working Sectors, caravaners for the trade routes. Gentry hold full papers. Duskroamers have none. Checkpoints guard Beacon gates, Sector boundaries, train stations and the roads between settlements. Without the right papers, you're turned back, or detained if you're caught somewhere you shouldn't be. Ways around it: a bribe (a handful, more for a serious checkpoint), forged papers (an extended action with Academics or Expression, helped by a writing case), sneaking past (Stealth), or a patron who vouches for you. Firearms are legal only for Gentry, the Houses and the security forces. Anyone else caught with one faces the same trouble as having no papers, or worse. See Runetech.","duskworld"],["Setting","Checkpoints","Duskworld › Travel papers","settings/duskworld/overview.html#travel-papers","Checkpoints guard Beacon gates, Sector boundaries, train stations and the roads between settlements. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Without the right papers,","Duskworld › Travel papers","settings/duskworld/overview.html#travel-papers","Without the right papers, you're turned back, or detained if you're caught somewhere you shouldn't be. Ways around it: a bribe (a handful, more for a serious checkpoint), forged papers (an extended action with Academics or Expression, helped by a writing case), sneaking past (Stealth), or a patron who vouches for you. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Firearms","Duskworld › Travel papers","settings/duskworld/overview.html#travel-papers","Firearms are legal only for Gentry, the Houses and the security forces. Anyone else caught with one faces the same trouble as having no papers, or worse. See Runetech. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Life in Eladria","Duskworld › Life in Eladria","settings/duskworld/overview.html#life-in-eladria","Everyone knows civilization lives on borrowed time. The Beacons hold back the Dusk, the Frontiers provide food, hunters venture into the Dusklands for resources, soldiers protect the settlements, artisans maintain the machinery, and merchants move goods between distant communities. Beyond all of it lies the Dusk. Some stay behind the walls. Others venture beyond them. The Allmother's mantra. Said at every curfew bell: \"Her light is our home. Her light protects.\"","duskworld"],["Setting","Institutions and places (working canon)","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","Established through play. Treat as a strong starting point rather than fixed canon. House Callarain: one of the Great Houses. Its engineers' guild builds and maintains Beacon technology and controls apprenticeships into the trade. House Shantari: one of the Great Houses, a shanat family, and the head of all shanats. Its kren blood passed down the old codes that let them read and run kren factories nobody else understands. It also makes the redvials that let shanats feed: a taboo, never sold openly, passed from shanat to shanat. House Merovant: the Great House of trade, whose motto is We record the truth. It alone cuts empty glints into boxings, so it controls the currency of Mendris. Its rival is the Syndicate. The Syndicate: the recognized black market, an underworld power that works with the Great Houses rather than against them. Crime, debts, favors and leverage; it does the jobs the Houses can't be seen doing. The Sixth House: a revolutionary group born in the Fork, working out of the Rude Rooster's Club. Also an underworld power, but one that wants to overthrow the Great Houses: sabotage, smuggling and espionage. The Gutterlord: Brinton Slann, a crime lord of the Fork, who hol","duskworld"],["Setting","House Callarain","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","House Callarain: one of the Great Houses. Its engineers' guild builds and maintains Beacon technology and controls apprenticeships into the trade. ","duskworld"],["Setting","House Shantari","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","House Shantari: one of the Great Houses, a shanat family, and the head of all shanats. Its kren blood passed down the old codes that let them read and run kren factories nobody else understands. It also makes the redvials that let shanats feed: a taboo, never sold openly, passed from shanat to shanat. ","duskworld"],["Setting","House Merovant","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","House Merovant: the Great House of trade, whose motto is We record the truth. It alone cuts empty glints into boxings, so it controls the currency of Mendris. Its rival is the Syndicate. ","duskworld"],["Setting","The Syndicate","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","The Syndicate: the recognized black market, an underworld power that works with the Great Houses rather than against them. Crime, debts, favors and leverage; it does the jobs the Houses can't be seen doing. ","duskworld"],["Setting","The Sixth House","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","The Sixth House: a revolutionary group born in the Fork, working out of the Rude Rooster's Club. Also an underworld power, but one that wants to overthrow the Great Houses: sabotage, smuggling and espionage. ","duskworld"],["Setting","The Gutterlord","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","The Gutterlord: Brinton Slann, a crime lord of the Fork, who holds boxing nights in the alley beside his house. ","duskworld"],["Setting","The Tons","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","The Tons: the gang that controls most of the Wastes, run by the Shaun family of merchants. ","duskworld"],["Setting","The IEE","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","The IEE: investigates and reports on deaths, disappearances and accidents across Mendris. Thorough when the people involved are well connected; thin or silent when the missing are poor, or broke curfew. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Curfew","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","Curfew: settlements ring a curfew bell at dusk. People who go out after it often don't come back. Whether that's the Dusk reaching past the Beacons or something else is unknown; it's treated as grim common knowledge, not superstition. ","duskworld"],["Setting","The curse of the Dusk","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","The curse of the Dusk: a recognized affliction from prolonged exposure or corruption, distinct from being taken outright. It can be formally tested for. Those who fail, or are suspected, are usually sent somewhere like Brightgate. In the rules, the curse is the Dusking Scar. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Taken by the Dusk","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","Taken by the Dusk: any death that leaves no sign of physical harm, when the victim had the curse, is said to be the Dusk taking them. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Brightgate","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","Brightgate: a mental institution in a rehabilitated old mansion, where people suspected of the curse or deemed unwell are confined, sometimes for years. It's said to hold more restless presences than it was meant to treat. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Runetech items","Duskworld › Runetech items","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#runetech-items","Every runetech item holds a glint, which powers it like a Device's. Runetech (weapon and armor trait): the item holds a glint and has the runetech abilities listed in brackets, such as Runetech (Duskward). With an empty glint, or none, the abilities don't work. Using an ability: a free action that spends 1 charge from the item's glint, unless the ability says otherwise. Refitting and refilling: a runetech item's glint refills and is refitted like any glint. Dream phenomena and other strange forces can make runetech stop working for a while.","duskworld"],["Setting","Runetech","Duskworld › Runetech items","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#runetech-items","Runetech (weapon and armor trait): the item holds a glint and has the runetech abilities listed in brackets, such as Runetech (Duskward). With an empty glint, or none, the abilities don't work. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Using an ability","Duskworld › Runetech items","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#runetech-items","Using an ability: a free action that spends 1 charge from the item's glint, unless the ability says otherwise. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Refitting and refilling","Duskworld › Runetech items","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#runetech-items","Refitting and refilling: a runetech item's glint refills and is refitted like any glint. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Dream phenomena","Duskworld › Runetech items","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#runetech-items","Dream phenomena and other strange forces can make runetech stop working for a while. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Runetech abilities","Duskworld › Runetech items","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#runetech-abilities","Charged (firearms): the weapon's mechanism runs on its glint. It spends 1 charge the first time it's fired in each minute; with no charge to spend, it can't fire. Change Form: the item switches between its two forms, each with its own statistics. Bind (chainwhip): after a hit, spend a charge as a free action. The chain coils around the target, who is Grappled by it while you hold the whip, up to 2 meters away. For 1 action, you can drag them 2 meters toward you. They break free with the Grapple action, their Strength + Brawl against your Strength + Weaponry. Duskward: on armor, until the start of your next turn, its rating is doubled against attacks by dusktouched and duskborn creatures. On a weapon, until the end of the round, it has Piercing 2 against them.","duskworld"],["Setting","Charged","Duskworld › Runetech abilities","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#runetech-abilities","Charged (firearms): the weapon's mechanism runs on its glint. It spends 1 charge the first time it's fired in each minute; with no charge to spend, it can't fire. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Change Form","Duskworld › Runetech abilities","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#runetech-abilities","Change Form: the item switches between its two forms, each with its own statistics. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Bind","Duskworld › Runetech abilities","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#runetech-abilities","Bind (chainwhip): after a hit, spend a charge as a free action. The chain coils around the target, who is Grappled by it while you hold the whip, up to 2 meters away. For 1 action, you can drag them 2 meters toward you. They break free with the Grapple action, their Strength + Brawl against your Strength + Weaponry. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Duskward","Duskworld › Runetech abilities","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#runetech-abilities","Duskward: on armor, until the start of your next turn, its rating is doubled against attacks by dusktouched and duskborn creatures. On a weapon, until the end of the round, it has Piercing 2 against them. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Firearms","Duskworld › Firearms","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#firearms","Firearms rely on intricate runetech mechanisms rather than the wielder's strength: aim, and pull the trigger. In Mendris they're legal only for the Gentry, the Houses and the security forces (see Travel papers). They replace the core pistol and rifle, which don't exist in Duskworld and are marked as incompatible with it. Jams: a Complication on a firearm attack jams it. Clearing it takes 1 action. Ammunition isn't counted, like other consumables, but the glint is: a firearm with no charge left is dead weight. Loud (trait): a shot is heard by everyone within a few hundred meters. Firing ends any attempt to stay hidden, and in a settlement it brings the watch. Prices: a lock pistol costs 2 slabs, a Marason 3 slabs and a lock rifle 4 slabs, sold only to those allowed to carry them. Anyone else pays double on the black market, and risks the questions. The Vartan II and SPEAR's weapons aren't sold: one off the black market costs a shell. The firearms and weapons below join the weapon table, and duskwarding armor the armor table, while Duskworld is selected. Lock pistol: the most common gun in Mendris, also called a revolver. It fires as fast as its rotating charged wheel can be locked a","duskworld"],["Setting","Jams","Duskworld › Firearms","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#firearms","Jams: a Complication on a firearm attack jams it. Clearing it takes 1 action. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Ammunition","Duskworld › Firearms","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#firearms","Ammunition isn't counted, like other consumables, but the glint is: a firearm with no charge left is dead weight. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Loud","Duskworld › Firearms","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#firearms","Loud (trait): a shot is heard by everyone within a few hundred meters. Firing ends any attempt to stay hidden, and in a settlement it brings the watch. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Prices","Duskworld › Firearms","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#firearms","Prices: a lock pistol costs 2 slabs, a Marason 3 slabs and a lock rifle 4 slabs, sold only to those allowed to carry them. Anyone else pays double on the black market, and risks the questions. The Vartan II and SPEAR's weapons aren't sold: one off the black market costs a shell. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Lock pistol","Duskworld › Firearms","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#firearms","Lock pistol: the most common gun in Mendris, also called a revolver. It fires as fast as its rotating charged wheel can be locked after each shot. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Marason","Duskworld › Firearms","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#firearms","Marason: a heavy pistol that blurs the line between a piercing shot and an explosion. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Lock rifle","Duskworld › Firearms","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#firearms","Lock rifle: the SR30 is the staple rifle of the IEE's constables, protecting justice from a good distance. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Vartan II","Duskworld › Firearms","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#firearms","Vartan II: the staple rifle of SPEAR agents, made to resist the Dusk and blast it back. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Switchaxe","Duskworld › Firearms","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#firearms","Switchaxe and chainwhip: SPEAR weapons, carried by the Dawntouched. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Duskwarding armor","Duskworld › Firearms","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#firearms","Duskwarding armor: every SPEAR agent wears a duskwarding vest; full suits are kept for the most dangerous assignments. ","duskworld"],["Setting","SPEAR's kit","Duskworld › Firearms","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#firearms","SPEAR's kit: besides their weapons and vest, agents carry flashsticks and a DLF conductor, and dawnlamps for assignments that must keep the Dusk off something (see Duskworld Devices). ","duskworld"],["Setting","Dawnlight and Dimlight","Duskworld › Dawnlight and Dimlight","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#dawnlight-and-dimlight","Only the Allmother's light holds back the Dusk (see The Dusk). Dawnlight is created and given by the Allmother. House Callarain is gifted it through its faith and shares it with Mendris through the Beacons. It's the most powerful shield against the Dusk: it never fails. It holds back only the Dusk and its creations: it has no hold on krens or krenblooded creatures. Dimlight is artificial Dawnlight, made with runetech. It does the same work to a lesser degree, and less reliably, but it's far easier to come by. Lamp Function Dawnlamp Carries Dawnlight. It burns one dose an hour, and within 6 meters the exposure level is None. It needs no glint. Doses come from House Callarain: SPEAR issues them for assignments, and anyone else needs a favor with the House (a dose is worth about a slab) or the black market (2 slabs a dose, if one can be found at all). The dimlamp, which carries Dimlight, is a glint-powered Device: see Duskworld Devices.","duskworld"],["Setting","Dawnlight","Duskworld › Dawnlight and Dimlight","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#dawnlight-and-dimlight","Dawnlight is created and given by the Allmother. House Callarain is gifted it through its faith and shares it with Mendris through the Beacons. It's the most powerful shield against the Dusk: it never fails. It holds back only the Dusk and its creations: it has no hold on krens or krenblooded creatures. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Dimlight","Duskworld › Dawnlight and Dimlight","settings/duskworld/runetech.html#dawnlight-and-dimlight","Dimlight is artificial Dawnlight, made with runetech. It does the same work to a lesser degree, and less reliably, but it's far easier to come by. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Glints and boxings","Character Creation › Wealth › Glints and boxings","character-creation.html#glints-and-boxings","The money of Mendris comes from glints: cube-like sources of power built by the krens. There are a great many of them, but no one can make true glints. Mendris's workshops make weaker copies, forged glints; the true kren glints are found only in the abandoned kren factories of the Dusklands. Glints also power every Device (see Glints and Devices). Mendris's currency is cut from empty glints. The cut pieces are called boxings, and only House Merovant may cut them: the House of trade controls the currency of Mendris. Duskworld names the three wealth units after them: Unit Duskworld name Worth Handful A handful of boxings Everyday money Bag A slab: a large uncut piece of an empty glint 10 handfuls of boxings Chest A shell: a whole empty glint, never cut 10 slabs Most people only ever handle boxings. Merchants and caravans deal in slabs. Shells belong to the Houses and the trading companies.","duskworld"],["Rules","Human","Character Creation › Human › Human","character-creation.html#duskworld-human","Humans are found throughout Mendris and much of Eladria. They come in many shapes and forms, but their greatest strength is adaptability. They thrive in communities, use whatever resources are available, and have repeatedly survived circumstances that should have destroyed them. Humans use the racial traits in the core rules (Driven and Fortune's Favor). Only humans can carry Athren, the power of the orange-blooded: most are infused by SPEAR, a rare few are born with it (see the Athrenite).","duskworld"],["Rules","Kehar","Character Creation › Human › Kehar","character-creation.html#kehar","Kehars are said to have been born from dreams, and a rare few can shape their dreams into reality. In a world that is steadily losing its color, kehars remain vibrant and colorful. Their appearance and culture vary between communities. In Mendris, kehars are sometimes regarded with suspicion: some believe they're still connected to the krens because of what their ancestors did. As a result, kehars often form close-knit communities and rely heavily on one another. In the Frontiers, generations of shared hardship mean they're usually treated like their human neighbors. Primary: Lucid. To a kehar, a bad outcome is only a dream that can be dreamed again. After you roll, you can reroll any number of dice that didn't succeed, taking 1 Stress per die. You can't reroll the stakes die, or a chance die that rolled a 1. Secondary: Dreaming Calm. A part of every kehar is always dreaming, and the waking world weighs on them a little less. Once per hour, you can become Determined (your next Stress cost is reduced by 1).","duskworld"],["Traits","Lucid","Character Creation › Human › Kehar","character-creation.html#kehar","Primary: Lucid. To a kehar, a bad outcome is only a dream that can be dreamed again. After you roll, you can reroll any number of dice that didn't succeed, taking 1 Stress per die. You can't reroll the stakes die, or a chance die that rolled a 1. ","duskworld"],["Traits","Dreaming Calm","Character Creation › Human › Kehar","character-creation.html#kehar","Secondary: Dreaming Calm. A part of every kehar is always dreaming, and the waking world weighs on them a little less. Once per hour, you can become Determined (your next Stress cost is reduced by 1). ","duskworld"],["Rules","Shanat","Character Creation › Human › Shanat","character-creation.html#shanat","Shanats descend from humans and kehars touched by kren blood. Their ancestry is complicated, and most shanats don't discuss their origins openly. The blood manifests in different ways: some shanats develop supernatural abilities tied to aspects of darkness (see the Shadowcaller), while others show only subtle physical peculiarities. Kren blood is feared in Mendris, and using its powers is forbidden, so shanats who have them use them in secret. The Shantari, one of the Great Houses, are a shanat family. Their kren connection passed down the old codes: secret codes and keys that let them read and run kren factories and machines that remain a mystery to the rest of Mendris. This knowledge is taught, not inherited: a shanat raised outside the family doesn't have it. Shanat blood is dominant. A child with any Shanat parent is Shanat, and the bloodline never shows up as a mere heritage. Every Shanat still carries a human or kehar bloodline, from a parent or from older ancestry resurfacing. Shanat have only a primary trait. A Shanat character always takes a different race as their heritage, and Shanat can't be chosen as a heritage. Primary: Night Sight. You ignore dice penalties from dim ","duskworld"],["Traits","Night Sight","Character Creation › Human › Shanat","character-creation.html#shanat","Primary: Night Sight. You ignore dice penalties from dim light and darkness, but not from total darkness. The deep Dusklands count as total darkness. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Frontier Folk","Character Creation › 2. Community › Frontier Folk","character-creation.html#frontier-folk","The people of the fortified farmsteads (or homesteads) around Dawnkeep: working the fields, standing watch, and living close to the edge of the Dusklands. The farms on Northbank's northern edge are the closest thing the city has to them. Community skills: Survival, Athletics or Animal Ken. Starting gear: Work tools, a lantern, and travel papers for the Frontiers. Key: Farmstead Kin. The farmsteads look after their own. You count as having a patron among Frontier folk, and at any farmstead you can find food, shelter and a sympathetic ear. Watchful Sleeper after Farmstead Kin Wits 3+: Nights on the edge of the Dusklands taught you to wake at the wrong sound. You can't be Surprised while resting, and once per hour you can react while Surprised. Field-Hardened after Farmstead Kin Stamina 3+: Once per day, remove 1 stack of Exhausted.","duskworld"],["Traits","Farmstead Kin","Character Creation › 2. Community › Frontier Folk","character-creation.html#farmstead-kin","Key trait · The farmsteads look after their own. You count as having a patron among Frontier folk, and at any farmstead you can find food, shelter and a sympathetic ear.","duskworld"],["Traits","Watchful Sleeper","Character Creation › 2. Community › Frontier Folk","character-creation.html#frontier-folk-watchful-sleeper","Wits 3+ · Nights on the edge of the Dusklands taught you to wake at the wrong sound. You can't be Surprised while resting, and once per hour you can react while Surprised.","duskworld"],["Traits","Field-Hardened","Character Creation › 2. Community › Frontier Folk","character-creation.html#frontier-folk-field-hardened","Stamina 3+ · Once per day, remove 1 stack of Exhausted.","duskworld"],["Rules","Gentry","Character Creation › 2. Community › Gentry","character-creation.html#gentry","The administrators, officials and old families of Dawnkeep's prominent Sectors: the Midlands, seat of the Great Council, the Caliethan University and the trade of the elite, and Northbank, the second of them, with its fine homes and stores. Community skills: Politics, Academics or Persuasion. Starting gear: Fine clothes, full travel papers, and firearm permission: when your vocation gives you a weapon, you can take a pistol or rifle for one of them. Key: Sector Standing. You count as having a patron in your Sector's circles, and in high society. Clerks, officials and guards treat you as someone who matters: you can get an audience, a permit or a hearing that others would wait weeks for. Family Purse after Sector Standing: You live comfortably and don't track ordinary expenses: anything that costs a handful or less costs you nothing (see Wealth). Take 2 Stress to gain an Opportunity on a roll to get something with money or influence. Educated after Sector Standing Intelligence 3+: Gain an Academics or Politics specialty. When you roll an untrained Mental skill, you take −2d instead of −3d.","duskworld"],["Traits","Sector Standing","Character Creation › 2. Community › Gentry","character-creation.html#sector-standing","Key trait · You count as having a patron in your Sector's circles, and in high society. Clerks, officials and guards treat you as someone who matters: you can get an audience, a permit or a hearing that others would wait weeks for.","duskworld"],["Traits","Family Purse","Character Creation › 2. Community › Gentry","character-creation.html#gentry-family-purse","You live comfortably and don't track ordinary expenses: anything that costs a handful or less costs you nothing (see Wealth). Take 2 Stress to gain an Opportunity on a roll to get something with money or influence.","duskworld"],["Traits","Educated","Character Creation › 2. Community › Gentry","character-creation.html#gentry-educated","Intelligence 3+ · Gain an Academics or Politics specialty. When you roll an untrained Mental skill, you take −2d instead of −3d.","duskworld"],["Rules","Guildfolk","Character Creation › 2. Community › Guildfolk","character-creation.html#guildfolk","The laborers, artisans and guild members of the working Sectors that keep Dawnkeep running: the factories of Southbank's Mackard District, its docks and the homes of Ovoron, and the markets and canals of the Fork. Community skills: Crafts, Athletics or Streetwise. Starting gear: A tool kit, a guild token, and travel papers for the working Sectors. Key: Guild Ties. You count as having a patron among the laborers, guilds and unions of the working Sectors. You know how to find work, a bed for the night, or someone who owes the guild a favor. Iron Back after Guild Ties Stamina 3+: Years of hard labor made you tough. Your Beaten Down threshold is 1 higher. Street Sense after Guild Ties Wits 3+: Gain a Streetwise (Dawnkeep) specialty. You always know the fastest way through the working Sectors, and you get +1d to notice when you're being followed or cheated.","duskworld"],["Traits","Guild Ties","Character Creation › 2. Community › Guildfolk","character-creation.html#guild-ties","Key trait · You count as having a patron among the laborers, guilds and unions of the working Sectors. You know how to find work, a bed for the night, or someone who owes the guild a favor.","duskworld"],["Traits","Iron Back","Character Creation › 2. Community › Guildfolk","character-creation.html#guildfolk-iron-back","Stamina 3+ · Years of hard labor made you tough. Your Beaten Down threshold is 1 higher.","duskworld"],["Traits","Street Sense","Character Creation › 2. Community › Guildfolk","character-creation.html#guildfolk-street-sense","Wits 3+ · Gain a Streetwise (Dawnkeep) specialty. You always know the fastest way through the working Sectors, and you get +1d to notice when you're being followed or cheated.","duskworld"],["Rules","Gutterfolk","Character Creation › 2. Community › Gutterfolk","character-creation.html#gutterfolk","The people of the Fork who get by however they can: dockhands and canal boatmen, market hustlers, fences, smugglers, fighters in the boxing nights, and the gangs that run the streets. Uptown called them gutterfolk as an insult. The Fork kept the name. Community skills: Streetwise, Brawl or Subterfuge. Starting gear: A knife or a sap, thieves' tools or a disguise kit, papers for the Fork, and one of these: travel papers for either Southbank or the Midlands (you held an honest job there once), or a smuggler who owes you and will take you past a Sector checkpoint, once per week, no papers asked. Key: Fork Born. You count as having a patron among the Fork's gangs, fences and smugglers. You know where to buy what isn't sold, who'll hide you for a night, and a doctor who doesn't ask questions. Canal Runner after Fork Born Dexterity 3+: You grew up on the Fork's canals, roofs and alleys. You get +1d to Athletics and Stealth to flee or lose a pursuer in the city, and in the Fork you always know a way out. Fence's Eye after Fork Born Wits 3+: You know what anything is worth, and what it's worth to whom. You sell stolen and illegal goods at three quarters of their price instead of half, you ","duskworld"],["Traits","Fork Born","Character Creation › 2. Community › Gutterfolk","character-creation.html#fork-born","Key trait · You count as having a patron among the Fork's gangs, fences and smugglers. You know where to buy what isn't sold, who'll hide you for a night, and a doctor who doesn't ask questions.","duskworld"],["Traits","Canal Runner","Character Creation › 2. Community › Gutterfolk","character-creation.html#gutterfolk-canal-runner","Dexterity 3+ · You grew up on the Fork's canals, roofs and alleys. You get +1d to Athletics and Stealth to flee or lose a pursuer in the city, and in the Fork you always know a way out.","duskworld"],["Traits","Fence's Eye","Character Creation › 2. Community › Gutterfolk","character-creation.html#gutterfolk-fences-eye","Wits 3+ · You know what anything is worth, and what it's worth to whom. You sell stolen and illegal goods at three quarters of their price instead of half, you get 1 more slab when you fence a kren glint, and +1d to spot fakes, forgeries and cut goods.","duskworld"],["Rules","The Wards","Character Creation › 2. Community › The Wards","character-creation.html#the-wards","Those who passed through an institution rather than a family: an orphanage, a workhouse, a parish home, or a military school. Many come from the workhouses and parish homes of the Fork's Wastes. Community skills: Streetwise, Thievery or Academics. Starting gear: One kit of your choice (your institution taught a trade), worn clothes, and papers from your institution. Key: Fellow Wards. You count as having a patron among the others who passed through your institution. They're scattered across Mendris, in every kind of position, and they remember where they came from. Indomitable after Fellow Wards Resolve 3+: You learned not to break. Once per hour, after you lose a contested roll against a Mental or Social effect, reroll your defending dice that didn't succeed. Safe Place after Fellow Wards: You have a hidden place in Dawnkeep that only you know about. When you rest there, you clear 1 extra Stress.","duskworld"],["Traits","Fellow Wards","Character Creation › 2. Community › The Wards","character-creation.html#fellow-wards","Key trait · You count as having a patron among the others who passed through your institution. They're scattered across Mendris, in every kind of position, and they remember where they came from.","duskworld"],["Traits","Indomitable","Character Creation › 2. Community › The Wards","character-creation.html#the-wards-indomitable","Resolve 3+ · You learned not to break. Once per hour, after you lose a contested roll against a Mental or Social effect, reroll your defending dice that didn't succeed.","duskworld"],["Traits","Safe Place","Character Creation › 2. Community › The Wards","character-creation.html#the-wards-safe-place","You have a hidden place in Dawnkeep that only you know about. When you rest there, you clear 1 extra Stress.","duskworld"],["Rules","Caravaners","Character Creation › 2. Community › Caravaners","character-creation.html#caravaners","The merchants and drivers of the caravans that carry goods between the settlements of Mendris, always on the move. Community skills: Persuasion, Drive or Survival. Starting gear: A survival kit or climbing gear, and travel papers for the trade routes. Key: Road Contacts. You count as having a patron along the trade routes. In any settlement a caravan stops at, you know someone who can offer news, lodging or passage. Tongues of the Road after Road Contacts Persuasion 2+: Gain a Persuasion (Bargaining) specialty, and +1d to Socialize. Road-Wise after Road Contacts Survival 2+: You get +1d when you Forage or Scout.","duskworld"],["Traits","Road Contacts","Character Creation › 2. Community › Caravaners","character-creation.html#road-contacts","Key trait · You count as having a patron along the trade routes. In any settlement a caravan stops at, you know someone who can offer news, lodging or passage.","duskworld"],["Traits","Tongues of the Road","Character Creation › 2. Community › Caravaners","character-creation.html#caravaners-tongues-of-the-road","Persuasion 2+ · Gain a Persuasion (Bargaining) specialty, and +1d to Socialize.","duskworld"],["Traits","Road-Wise","Character Creation › 2. Community › Caravaners","character-creation.html#caravaners-road-wise","Survival 2+ · You get +1d when you Forage or Scout.","duskworld"],["Rules","Exposure","Core Rules › Hazards › Exposure","rules/damage-and-health.html#exposure","Every place has an exposure level. While characters are in it, they Endure against the Dusk at the interval shown. Level Where Endure every Successes needed None Behind the Beacons, in daylight — — Faint Outside after curfew, the edges of the Beacons' reach Day 1 Heavy The Dusklands 4 hours 2 Deep The deep Dusklands, duskborn lairs Hour 3 Enduring against the Dusk rolls Resolve + Composure, not Stamina + Resolve, needing the level's successes. Like any Endure, it costs your reaction in a fight; outside one, it costs nothing. Success: nothing happens. Failure: take Stress equal to the shortfall (the successes you were missing). It's the grey pressing on your mind. Like any Stress, it can overflow into Tenacity damage, but overflow never counts as damage from the Dusk. Complication, or any failure at Deep: also take 1 Tenacity damage from the Dusk (see Dusking). Duskborn and some kren creations can also deal Tenacity damage from the Dusk with their abilities. Protection: Grey-Weaned (Dusk trait, Dusking 1): Stress from Dusk exposure is reduced by 1. Hollow Calm (Dusk trait): once per day, a failed check costs no Stress. Wards and the Allmother's light: a wardwright's charm, or light ","duskworld"],["Rules","Success","Core Rules › Hazards › Exposure","rules/damage-and-health.html#exposure","Success: nothing happens. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Failure","Core Rules › Hazards › Exposure","rules/damage-and-health.html#exposure","Failure: take Stress equal to the shortfall (the successes you were missing). It's the grey pressing on your mind. Like any Stress, it can overflow into Tenacity damage, but overflow never counts as damage from the Dusk. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Complication, or any failure at Deep","Core Rules › Hazards › Exposure","rules/damage-and-health.html#exposure","Complication, or any failure at Deep: also take 1 Tenacity damage from the Dusk (see Dusking). ","duskworld"],["Rules","Grey-Weaned","Core Rules › Hazards › Exposure","rules/damage-and-health.html#exposure","Grey-Weaned (Dusk trait, Dusking 1): Stress from Dusk exposure is reduced by 1. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Hollow Calm","Core Rules › Hazards › Exposure","rules/damage-and-health.html#exposure","Hollow Calm (Dusk trait): once per day, a failed check costs no Stress. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Wards and the Allmother's light","Core Rules › Hazards › Exposure","rules/damage-and-health.html#exposure","Wards and the Allmother's light: a wardwright's charm, or light from the Allmother, gives +1d to Endure against the Dusk. Ordinary light (a lamp, a torch, a fire) helps you see, but doesn't hold back the Dusk. A dawnlamp burning Dawnlight keeps the Dusk out entirely, and a dimlamp lowers the exposure level around it. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Dusking","Core Rules › Scars › Dusking","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusking","Dusking is the Scar the Dusk leaves (see Scars). It's written on the sheet with its stage, such as Dusking 2, and never heals with rest or downtime. The Dusk takes, but it also gives: each stage brings a Dusk trait. Gaining Dusking: each point of Tenacity damage from the Dusk raises Dusking by 1. Mark the new stage, gain what it brings (below), then make the madness check. Penalty to Bouts: Dusking is subtracted from every Bout of Madness roll, whatever caused it: 3d6 − missing Tenacity − Dusking. The curse of the Dusk: having Dusking 1 or more is what Mendris calls the curse. The formal test detects it from stage 1. Stage Effect 1 Nothing visible, but the curse test detects it. Gain the Grey-Weaned Dusk trait. 2 Visible. Choose one Dusk quirk and one Dusk trait (below). Each stage after 2 Choose another quirk and another Dusk trait. Equal to Resolve + Composure Dusktouched. You become one of the Dusk's creatures. The character leaves play and becomes the GM's.","duskworld"],["Rules","Gaining Dusking","Core Rules › Scars › Dusking","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusking","Gaining Dusking: each point of Tenacity damage from the Dusk raises Dusking by 1. Mark the new stage, gain what it brings (below), then make the madness check. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Penalty to Bouts","Core Rules › Scars › Dusking","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusking","Penalty to Bouts: Dusking is subtracted from every Bout of Madness roll, whatever caused it: 3d6 − missing Tenacity − Dusking. ","duskworld"],["Rules","The curse of the Dusk","Core Rules › Scars › Dusking","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusking","The curse of the Dusk: having Dusking 1 or more is what Mendris calls the curse. The formal test detects it from stage 1. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Dusk traits","Core Rules › Scars › Dusking","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Dusk traits are gifts of the curse. Once you have every trait below, the GM and player invent new ones that fit. Grey-Weaned (stage 1): The Dusk has less hold on you. Stress you take from Dusk exposure is reduced by 1. Grey Sight: Your eyes have learned the grey. In the deep Dusklands, you see as if in darkness rather than total darkness (−3d to sight rolls instead of a chance die). Night Sight cancels that penalty. Hollow Calm: Something in you has gone quiet. Once per day, when you fail to Endure against the Dusk, you take no Stress from it. A Complication or a failure at Deep still deals Tenacity damage. Kindred Dread: Dusktouched and duskborn creatures sense something of their own in you. You get +1d to Stealth to avoid their notice, and the first attack each of them makes against you in a fight takes −1d. Unsettling: People feel the grey on you before they see it. +1d to Intimidation, but anyone who knows the signs recognizes the curse. Dusk quirks. The GM and player choose or invent one that fits. Each is a sign of the curse and comes with a drawback: Drained colour: your eyes, hair or skin turn grey. Anyone who knows the signs recognizes the curse. Wrong shadow: your shadow ","duskworld"],["Traits","Grey-Weaned","Core Rules › Scars › Dusk traits","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Grey-Weaned (stage 1): The Dusk has less hold on you. Stress you take from Dusk exposure is reduced by 1. ","duskworld"],["Traits","Grey Sight","Core Rules › Scars › Dusk traits","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Grey Sight: Your eyes have learned the grey. In the deep Dusklands, you see as if in darkness rather than total darkness (−3d to sight rolls instead of a chance die). Night Sight cancels that penalty. ","duskworld"],["Traits","Hollow Calm","Core Rules › Scars › Dusk traits","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Hollow Calm: Something in you has gone quiet. Once per day, when you fail to Endure against the Dusk, you take no Stress from it. A Complication or a failure at Deep still deals Tenacity damage. ","duskworld"],["Traits","Kindred Dread","Core Rules › Scars › Dusk traits","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Kindred Dread: Dusktouched and duskborn creatures sense something of their own in you. You get +1d to Stealth to avoid their notice, and the first attack each of them makes against you in a fight takes −1d. ","duskworld"],["Traits","Unsettling","Core Rules › Scars › Dusk traits","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Unsettling: People feel the grey on you before they see it. +1d to Intimidation, but anyone who knows the signs recognizes the curse. ","duskworld"],["Traits","Drained colour","Core Rules › Scars › Dusk traits","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Drained colour: your eyes, hair or skin turn grey. Anyone who knows the signs recognizes the curse. ","duskworld"],["Traits","Wrong shadow","Core Rules › Scars › Dusk traits","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Wrong shadow: your shadow lags or moves on its own. Animals shy away from you (−1d to Animal Ken). ","duskworld"],["Traits","Cold blood","Core Rules › Scars › Dusk traits","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Cold blood: you're cold to the touch and feel little warmth. −1d to Empathy rolls to comfort or connect. ","duskworld"],["Traits","Beacon-shy","Core Rules › Scars › Dusk traits","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Beacon-shy: a Beacon's light makes your skin crawl. −1d to all rolls while in a Beacon's direct light. ","duskworld"],["Traits","Grey dreams","Core Rules › Scars › Dusk traits","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Grey dreams: you dream of the Dusk. The first time each day you clear Stress at a rest, clear 1 less. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Taken by the Dusk","Core Rules › Tenacity and madness › Taken by the Dusk","rules/stress-and-madness.html#taken-by-the-dusk","A character can be lost to the Dusk in two ways: Taken by the Dusk: a character with Dusking 1 or more who dies on a Bout of Madness. In Mendris, any death that leaves no sign of physical harm, while the victim had at least Dusking 1, is said to be taken by the Dusk. Dusktouched: a character whose Dusking reaches their Resolve + Composure without dying first. They don't die. They change. Because Dusking only rises with Tenacity damage from the Dusk, missing Tenacity and Dusking tend to rise together during a trip, so the Bout penalty effectively counts twice. Chance of death when a failed madness check brings a Bout: Missing Tenacity + Dusking Death chance 1 + 1 0% 2 + 2 2% 3 + 3 9% 4 + 4 26% Tenacity heals in downtime; Dusking doesn't. A character who recovers between trips still carries their Dusking into every Bout.","duskworld"],["Rules","Taken by the Dusk","Core Rules › Tenacity and madness › Taken by the Dusk","rules/stress-and-madness.html#taken-by-the-dusk","Taken by the Dusk: a character with Dusking 1 or more who dies on a Bout of Madness. In Mendris, any death that leaves no sign of physical harm, while the victim had at least Dusking 1, is said to be taken by the Dusk. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Dusktouched","Core Rules › Tenacity and madness › Taken by the Dusk","rules/stress-and-madness.html#taken-by-the-dusk","Dusktouched: a character whose Dusking reaches their Resolve + Composure without dying first. They don't die. They change. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Glints","Crafting › Devices › Glints","crafting.html#glints","Glints are the core component of every Duskworld Device. Each glint has a rating from 1 to 5, which is its number of charges. The Device's own rating still sets its dice and the crafting limit, and any glint fits any Device. Glint Rating Where it comes from Refill Forged glint 1–3 Mendris's workshops, copying kren tech Each Prepare at a rest refills 1 charge Kren glint 4–5 Storage compartments in the abandoned kren factories of the Dusklands Refills to full at the end of each rest Charges: a Device spends 1 charge from its glint each time it's used. A Complication on a Device roll spends 1 more. Running dry: a glint that reaches 0 charges is empty for good, and the Device stops working until it's refitted. Neither rest, downtime nor Field Repair revives an empty glint. A rating 1 glint is single-use. Downtime: the end of a week of downtime refills every glint that isn't empty. Burning out: when a Device spends its glint's last charge, you can burn it out on purpose: the Device rolls with +2d and raises the stakes. Refitting: putting a new glint into a Device takes an hour of work and needs Technology 2+. Empty glints: an empty forged glint is scrap. An empty kren glint becomes a sh","duskworld"],["Rules","Charges","Crafting › Devices › Glints","crafting.html#glints","Charges: a Device spends 1 charge from its glint each time it's used. A Complication on a Device roll spends 1 more. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Running dry","Crafting › Devices › Glints","crafting.html#glints","Running dry: a glint that reaches 0 charges is empty for good, and the Device stops working until it's refitted. Neither rest, downtime nor Field Repair revives an empty glint. A rating 1 glint is single-use. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Downtime","Crafting › Devices › Glints","crafting.html#glints","Downtime: the end of a week of downtime refills every glint that isn't empty. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Burning out","Crafting › Devices › Glints","crafting.html#glints","Burning out: when a Device spends its glint's last charge, you can burn it out on purpose: the Device rolls with +2d and raises the stakes. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Refitting","Crafting › Devices › Glints","crafting.html#glints","Refitting: putting a new glint into a Device takes an hour of work and needs Technology 2+. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Empty glints","Crafting › Devices › Glints","crafting.html#glints","Empty glints: an empty forged glint is scrap. An empty kren glint becomes a shell, Mendris's largest unit of money. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Buying and trading","Crafting › Devices › Buying and trading","crafting.html#buying-and-trading","Forged glints are legal, and are bought and sold like other gear. Forged glint Price Rating 1 2 handfuls of boxings Rating 2 1 slab Rating 3 3 slabs Kren glints are another matter. Selling one is illegal: they belong to the Houses. Surrendering a kren glint to a House earns favors equal to its rating with that House (see Favors). Fencing one gets its rating in slabs, a fraction of what it's worth. Finding a buyer is a Manipulation + Streetwise roll; on a Complication, someone notices.","duskworld"],["Rules","Surrendering","Crafting › Devices › Buying and trading","crafting.html#buying-and-trading","Surrendering a kren glint to a House earns favors equal to its rating with that House (see Favors). ","duskworld"],["Rules","Fencing","Crafting › Devices › Buying and trading","crafting.html#buying-and-trading","Fencing one gets its rating in slabs, a fraction of what it's worth. Finding a buyer is a Manipulation + Streetwise roll; on a Complication, someone notices. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Duskworld Devices","Crafting › Common Devices › Duskworld Devices","crafting.html#duskworld-devices","These follow the Device rules, powered by a glint. Device Function Dimlamp Rating 3+. Carries Dimlight. Set it and spend a charge: for an hour, everyone within 5 meters treats the exposure level as one lower. It can't lower Deep. Each time those in its light Endure against the Dusk, it rolls first: with no successes, it flickers and does nothing for that interval. Purifier Spend a charge to make food and water from the Dusklands safe for a day (see Dusklands food), for up to twice its rating in people. Shock plate Hide it in a floor or path. It rolls against the Dexterity + Stamina of the first creature to step on it: on a hit, 1 bashing damage and Stunned until the end of its next turn. Dusk chime Set it over a camp or path. It rings when a dusktouched or duskborn creature comes within 20 meters, rolling against their Stealth. Kren seal Seal a door or container with a charge. Opening it takes Technology against its roll, or breaking it by force. Flashstick Rating 2, holding a single-use rating 1 glint. SPEAR issue. Crack it and throw it (Throwable): for a minute, everything within 10 meters of where it lands is in bright light. Dusktouched and duskborn caught in the first flash co","duskworld"],["Rules","Duskworld weapons","Core Rules › Weapons › Duskworld weapons","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#duskworld-weapons","Weapon Damage Traits Bulk Lock pistol 2 lethal Range 20 m, Piercing 1, Loud, Runetech (Charged) 1 Marason 3 lethal Range 15 m, Piercing 1, Knockdown, Loud, Runetech (Charged) 1 Lock rifle 4 lethal Range 60 m, Slow, Two-handed, Precise, Piercing 2, Loud, Runetech (Charged) 2 Vartan II 4 lethal Range 60 m, Slow, Two-handed, Precise, Piercing 2, Loud, Runetech (Charged, Duskward) 2 Switchaxe 2 lethal Axe: Versatile, Knockdown. Blade: Reach, Versatile. Runetech (Change Form) 2 Chainwhip 1 lethal Reach, Stunning, Runetech (Bind, Duskward) 1","duskworld"],["Rules","Duskwarding armor","Core Rules › Armor › Duskwarding armor","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#duskwarding-armor","Armor Rating Penalty Bulk Duskwarding vest 1 None. Runetech (Duskward) 1 Duskwarding suit 2 −1d to Stealth and Athletics. Runetech (Duskward) 2","duskworld"],["Rules","Duskworld at the table","Cheat Sheet › Setting rules › Duskworld at the table","cheatsheet.html#duskworld-at-the-table","The Dusk (exposure): Endure against the Dusk with Resolve + Composure at each interval. Exposure Every Successes None — — Faint (after curfew, Beacon edges) Day 1 Heavy (the Dusklands) 4 hours 2 Deep (deep Dusklands, lairs) Hour 3 Failure: Stress equal to the shortfall. Complication, or any failure at Deep: also 1 Tenacity damage from the Dusk, which raises Dusking by 1. Dusking is subtracted from every Bout of Madness. Stage 1 gives Grey-Weaned; from stage 2 a quirk and a Dusk trait per stage; at Resolve + Composure, Dusktouched. Protection: a ward or the Allmother's light +1d; a dawnlamp makes it None within 6 m; a dimlamp lowers it one level (not Deep). Unpurified Dusklands food: a daily Endure, 1 success, 1 Stress at most. Money (boxings): handful of boxings · slab (10 handfuls) · shell (10 slabs). Forged glints: rating 1 for 2 handfuls, 2 for a slab, 3 for 3 slabs. Magic at a glance: Vocation Pool Calling Cost on the roll Dreamshaper Reverie: 2 + Wits or Presence. Full after a rest with sleep; an hour's daydream regains Composure successes (at least 1) 2+ actions; stakes always raised, the stakes die replacing a pool die Opportunity half, success full, failure 1, Complication ","duskworld"],["Rules","Failure","Cheat Sheet › Setting rules › Duskworld at the table","cheatsheet.html#duskworld-at-the-table","Failure: Stress equal to the shortfall. Complication, or any failure at Deep: also 1 Tenacity damage from the Dusk, which raises Dusking by 1. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Dusking","Cheat Sheet › Setting rules › Duskworld at the table","cheatsheet.html#duskworld-at-the-table","Dusking is subtracted from every Bout of Madness. Stage 1 gives Grey-Weaned; from stage 2 a quirk and a Dusk trait per stage; at Resolve + Composure, Dusktouched. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Protection","Cheat Sheet › Setting rules › Duskworld at the table","cheatsheet.html#duskworld-at-the-table","Protection: a ward or the Allmother's light +1d; a dawnlamp makes it None within 6 m; a dimlamp lowers it one level (not Deep). Unpurified Dusklands food: a daily Endure, 1 success, 1 Stress at most. ","duskworld"],["Conditions","Beaten Down","Conditions","conditions.html#beaten-down","Making an attack costs 1 Stress, unless you have no chance to surrender (the enemy won't or can't accept it, such as a mindless beast). If you surrender, clear 1 Stress. Ends when your total damage drops to your Beaten Down threshold or below.",""],["Conditions","Bleeding","Conditions","conditions.html#bleeding","Persistent damage: 1 lethal damage at the end of each of your turns, until the duration runs out or the wound is treated (with First aid, Field Medicine or Avoid Death).",""],["Conditions","Blinded","Conditions","conditions.html#blinded","Rolls that rely on sight use a chance die. Attacking or acting against a target you can hear, such as an adjacent enemy, takes −3d instead, as in darkness. You can't Dodge, and your other defense rolls take −2d.",""],["Conditions","Deafened","Conditions","conditions.html#deafened","Rolls that rely on hearing use a chance die, and you take −1d to notice anything around you. You can't benefit from anything that needs you to hear someone, such as a shouted order or a command, unless you can see them.",""],["Conditions","Determined","Conditions","conditions.html#determined","Your next Stress cost is reduced by 1.",""],["Conditions","Disoriented","Conditions","conditions.html#disoriented","You can't use reactions.",""],["Conditions","Exhausted","Conditions","conditions.html#exhausted","−1d to all rolls. Exhausted can stack, giving −1d for each stack. When your stacks reach your Beaten Down threshold (Stamina + Resolve), you collapse: you fall Unconscious until your stacks drop below it, usually at the end of a rest. If you gain another stack while collapsed, you die.",""],["Conditions","Frightened","Conditions","conditions.html#frightened","−1d to all rolls, or −2d instead on rolls against the source of your fear. Moving closer to the source of your fear costs 1 Stress each time you choose to do it.",""],["Conditions","Grappled","Conditions","conditions.html#grappled","You can't Move, Dodge or Disengage, and you can only attack unarmed, with a Light weapon, or with the Grapple action. Ends when someone breaks free.",""],["Conditions","Immobilized","Conditions","conditions.html#immobilized","You can't move.",""],["Conditions","Inspired","Conditions","conditions.html#inspired","After a failed roll, reroll every die that didn't succeed. Inspired ends when used.",""],["Conditions","Obscured","Conditions","conditions.html#obscured","Rolls to see or target you take −1d, and you can try to hide with Stealth. Smoke, fog, shadow and some powers cause it.",""],["Conditions","Prone","Conditions","conditions.html#prone","You're on the ground: melee attacks against you get +1d, ranged attacks −1d (see Position). Standing up takes 1 action.",""],["Conditions","Slowed","Conditions","conditions.html#slowed","Your Speed is halved.",""],["Conditions","Stunned","Conditions","conditions.html#stunned","In combat, you lose any reactions, and on your turn you gain two fewer actions and don't gain a reaction. Out of combat, you're overwhelmed: you move and react more slowly to your situation, at the GM's discretion.",""],["Conditions","Surprised","Conditions","conditions.html#surprised","You must take a slow turn and can't react.",""],["Conditions","Unconscious","Conditions","conditions.html#unconscious","You can't act or react. If damage knocked you out, see Avoiding death and Waking up.",""]]};
